Perera2019191,2019,Machine learning methods to assist energy system optimization,yes,yes,Neural network,high,24,PCE / RSM,0,abstract/keywords,em optimization. a surrogate model is developed with the support of a supervised learning technique (by using artificial neural network) in order to bypass computationally intensive actual engineering model (aem). eight different neural network architectures are considered in the proc,Transfer learning,high,30,Latin hypercube sampling,0,abstract/keywords,this study evaluates the potential of supervised and transfer learning techniques to assist energy system optimization. a surrogate model is developed with the support of a supervised learning technique (by using artific,P5 uncertainty,high,9,P1 replacement,0,pdf,"explorative application of discrete bayesian networks as surrogate models for energy systems analysis rainer kelk a , 1 , luca podofillini a , * , vinh n. dang a , evangelos panos a , b a centers for nuclear engineering and science and for energy and environmental sciences, laboratory for energy systems analysis,paul scherrer institute, villigen psi, switzerland b chair of energy systems analysis, institute of energy and process engineering, eth zurich, zurich 8092, switzerland highlights • we present methods for building, validating, and using bbns as surrogate models. • with average relative error below 2 %, the bbn model is an adequate model surrogate. • benefits: interactive analysis, intuitive uncertainty propagation, goal-driven analysis. article info keywords: bayesian networks energy systems models surrogate models uncertainty propagation and analysis abstract this work investigates data-based discrete bayesian belief networks (bbns) as surrogate energy system models for result analysis and interactive analyses, such as what-if analyses. a simplified version of the swiss times (stem) model, referred to as stem-lite, is used for demonstration. a method to optimize the bbn model is devised, based on performance metrics related to the accuracy of the bbn predictions, calculated over data records unseen by the bbn in the training phase. further validation of the bbn on a set of seven scenarios yielded an average relative error below 2 %, suggesting adequate performance as surrogate model. the application of the surrogate bbn model is demonstrated to highlight its benefits, which include enabling interactive analysis (supported by the visualization of key variables, their relationships and interactions), fast and intuitive uncer - tainty propagation, and support for goal-driven analysis (backward reasoning from outcomes to the inputs that produce these outcomes). the surrogate bbn presented here was developed to elaborate the methods for con - structing, validating, and using bbn models for energy systems analysis and to demonstrate the benefits of such a model; at this stage, this model is not intended for energy systems and economics policy discussions. for practical applications, future work is needed to reduce the number of data records to construct the bbn, to introduce the option to treat the time dependence of the input variables, and to allow for larger bbn models (involving more variables) that reflect the increasing complexity of energy systems. 1. introduction energy systems models provide comprehensive and detailed repre - sentation of the energy system sectors, from production to energy con - version and consumption, and are typically used to identify energy system pathways that achieve one or more objectives under different policies, subject to technical, resource and other constraints [ 1 ]. today, a primary application of energy system models is the achievement of sustainability in the context of climate change. the models are built with detailed technology specifications and often employ linear program - ming techniques to minimize the overall cost of the energy provision. the generated outputs include estimates of technology capacity and utilization, marginal commodity prices, and emissions across the energy system. some of the analytical strengths of energy systems models include a consistent framework for specifying techno-economic perfor - mance, efficient goal-seeking within complex systems, and insights into * corresponding author. e-mail address: luca.podofillini@psi.ch (l. podofillini). 1 current affiliation: center for nuclear engineering and sciences, laboratory for reactor physics and thermal-hydraulics, paul scherrer institute, villigen psi, switzerland contents lists available at sciencedirect applied energy journal homepag e: www.else vier.com/loc ate/apene rgy https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apenergy.2025.126146 received 4 november 2024; received in revised form 18 april 2025; accepted 14 may 2025 applied energy 394 (2025) 126146 available online 24 may 2025 0306-2619/© 2025 the authors. published by elsevier ltd. this is an open access article under the cc by license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ). various energy futures aligned with policy objectives. the energy sys - tems analysis space is typically multi-dimensional, spanning over energy technologies, commodities (e.g, electricity, gas, etc.), sectors (e.g., buildings, electricity sector, transport, etc.) as well as addressing mul - tiple time and space scales. for this reason, the resulting mathematical model couples time, space and technology among the dimensions as well as within each dimension. the extensive scope of energy system models has led to increasing complexity as various approaches and features are developed to enhance the realism of internal model dynamics [ 2 , 3 ]. due to the complexity of the problem and the large number of interdependent variables (e.g. policy parameters, technology costs, consumer needs and uses, sector coupling), the analyses can be computationally expensive (i.e. hours on standard computers). also, interpreting the results in terms of the interactions among the variables and the model outputs requires expertise in energy economics and en - ergy systems analysis, as well as familiarity with the underlying modelling frameworks. the treatment and communication of un - certainties and the transparency of the connection among assumptions, models and the produced insights, e.g. in terms of the drivers of the results themselves and of the derived policy recommendations, are recurrently identified as areas in need of improvement [ 4 – 6 ]. when the computational demands of models are high, uncertainty propagation and analysis, sensitivity analyses, as well as prompt what-if analyses can be carried out by introducing surrogate models, compu - tationally efficie",Problem UQ,high,18,_other,0,pdf,"ve error below 2 %, the bbn model is an adequate model surrogate. • benefits: interactive analysis, intuitive uncertainty propagation, goal-driven analysis. article info keywords: bayesian networks energy systems models surrogate models uncertainty propagation and analysis abstract",yes
