f the copula using (3). step 2. dynamic correlation modeling. • divide the copula cdfs into multiple intervals so that each interval contains the same number of original samples. each interval is deﬁned as a state. • create the markov chain empirical probability of going to state( j) from state (i) via statistical calculations. • create empirical discrete distributions (of interval size dt) for each state. step 3. weather simulation generation. • generate a sample state path using a markov chain. • simulate a discrete copula cdf when in a particular state. • calculate the cdf of the outdoor temperature and solar radiation by calculating the inverse function of the discrete copula cdf. •generate the sample points for the outdoor temperature and solar radiation by calculating the inverse function of the weather marginal cdfs. the pdf of the multidimensional outdoor temperature can be assumed to be a normal distribution [3]: pdf(t) = 1 σsh √ 2π exp [ − (t − μsh)2 2σ2 sh ] , (2) where t is the outdoor temperature and μsh and σsh are the mean and standard deviations, respectively. a copula function exists with independent variables, which are the marginal cumulative probability distributions of the radiation and temperature: y = copulacdf(cdf(g), cdf(t), rho), (3) where cdf ( •) is a cumulative distribution function, copu- lacdf (•) is a cdf for a copula, and rho is a matrix of linear correlation parameters. the copula cdf is controlled to model the dynamic correla- tion among capricious factors in weather samples. the markov chain model for regime-switching time series is built by using a stochastic generation to simulate the copula cdf by setting up several copula cdf regimes. based on the formulas pro- vided above, the method of stochastic simulation in accordance with realistic samples is given in procedure 1. remark 1: traditionally, the mutual correlation is fully mod- eled, but the state transition probability (i.e., the scenario dynamic change law) is neglected. 