nt that needs to be estimated. the approximation accuracy of (31) depends on p.t h e number of unknown deterministic coefﬁcients is na = (n + p)! n!p! , (32) the sample size l is l = 2 × na. (33) the unknown coefﬁcients in the polynomial chaos expan- sion can be determined by minimizing an appropriate norm of the residual [26], and we use the least squares method to struc- ture a surface. note that the sample size of the input variables is twice as large as the number of undetermined coefﬁcients: n srsm = na, (34) where nsrsm is the sample size of the input variables, whose corresponding response vector is obtained using the power ﬂow calculation as experimental data. when the input size is far less than the actual number of scenarios, it is obvious that we can improve the calculation speed of the ppf. a second-order srsm is introduced to estimate the ppf, and digital characteristics of the response vector can be calculated using e(y) = a 0, (35) v(y) = k∑ i=1 a2 i + 2 k∑ i=1 a2 ii + k−1∑ i=1 k∑ j>i a2 ij, (36) where e(•) is the mathematical expectation function for the srsm and v(•) is the variance function for the srsm. by cal- culating the coefﬁcients, we can not only obtain the probability authorized licensed use limited to: fh technikum wien. downloaded on may 11,2026 at 14:08:53 utc from ieee xplore. restrictions apply. 2910 ieee transactions on smart grid, vol. 11, no. 4, july 2020 digital characteristics but also the responses corresponding to a group of inputs using (31). we use the srsm to reduce the dpf calculations and sce- nario size, the purpose of which is to reduce the ppf time cost. the number of scenarios is also the sample size of the input data, and scale reduction is an important reason for the improvement in the ppf calculation’s efﬁciency using the srsm. according to (31), the higher the dimension is, the more items there are in the formula. more items lead to a more complicated calculation process. the application con- dition of the srsm can be summarized as follows: (a) the time cost of the dimensionality reduction + the time cost of the srsm < the dpf time reduction accompanied by the sample size reduction. when the total time cost of the srsm with a certain low dimensionality and dimensionality reduc- tion is equal to that of the reduced dpf calculations, that low dimensionality is the limitation for the srsm. (b) according to (33), the sample size needs to be twice the number of srsm coefﬁcients to realize the srsm calculation. if the number of srsm coefﬁcients is half of the sample size of the primal data, the sample size will not be further reduced. in this case, the number of scenarios is not reduced, and the workload of the dpf calculation is not reduced. therefore, the premise of using the srsm to calculate the ppf is that the number of srsm coefﬁcients is less than half of the original sample size. we can obtain the limitation for a low dimensionality accord- ing to the limitation for the number of srsm coefﬁcients using (32). considering the time cost of the dimensionality reduction and the srsm, the number of srsm coefﬁcients should be much smaller than half of the original sample size. remark 5: essentially, the srsm is an analytical method that combines statistics with mathematics. compared with the academic idea of a scenario reduction, the srsm considers the probabilistic relationship between the input (uncertainty in the power supply and load) and the output (state variable), while the scenario reduction equates the probabilistic charac- teristics of the output with the probabilities of the scenarios. v. s tochastic optimal planning a. model stochastic optimal planning has been an essential subject of research in distribution networks, and a stochastic pro- gramming model should be applied instead of deterministic models when intermittent renewable generators are developed. the economy objective function of the proposed programming model is min f obj(s, num) = mean(ploss), (37) where ploss is the total active power loss in the distribution networks, s is the capacitor capacities, and the variable mum denotes the capacitor bus number. the constraints of the proposed programming model are ⎧ ⎪⎪ ⎨ ⎪⎪ ⎩ s min ≤ s ≤ smax 1 < num < numsys sj < slimit,j pr(vmin < vi < vmax) ≥ α, (38) where smin is the lower limit of the capacity; smax is the upper limit of the capacity; numsys is the number of buses; sj is the transmission power of the jth branch; slimt,j is the limit of the jth branch; viis the ith bus voltage amplitude; vmin is the voltage lower limit; vmax is the voltage upper limit; and α is the minimum of the qualiﬁed voltage conﬁdence level for capacitor buses. now we use the chebyshev inequality in our development of (38) to further combine the ppf and stochastic optimal planning models: pr(|vi − e(vi)| <ε) ≥ 1 − v(vi) ε2 . (39) the inequality in (39) can be extended to the interval [vmin, vmax]: pr(vmin < vi < vmax) ≥ 1 − v(vi) + [ e(vi) − vmin+vmax 2 ]2 ( vmax−vmin 2 )2 . (40) remark 6: obviously, both (37) and (40) can be obtained using (35) and (36). the contribution of this paper is to use the fast ppf calculation method to improve the efﬁciency in planning solutions with the help of inequality theory. the plan- ning model can be adjusted and improved according to actual engineering needs. b. conservative discussion it should be noted that the chebyshev inequality is used for security constraints rather than the economy objective in this paper. if the objective function is too conservative it will reduce the economy performance, but this paper uses the mathematical expectation to describe the objective; for the safety constraints, if it meets the conservative condition it must also meet the safety constraints under the worst con- ditions. the conservative security constraints may reduce the feasible solutions, but this does not directly lead to a decline in the objective performance. only when the eliminated fea- sible solution region includes the optimal solution does the objective performance decline. the chebyshev inequality is used in the stochastic optimal planning model, but in principle the constraint derived by the chebyshev inequality is conservative. monte carlo can compare the chebyshev inequality to address this issue. the chebyshev inequality uses very little information, and only a mathematical expectation and variance are involved. the chebyshev inequality tells us that the probability for a ran- dom variable deviating from its mean value is limited by its variance. when ε is smaller, the calculation results are more accurate. the chebyshev inequality cannot be wrong; at most it is conservative. monte carlo has a better accuracy than the chebyshev inequality, but the time cost of monte carlo is greater than that of the chebyshev inequality. note that the srsm can improve the ppf efﬁciency by reducing the conventional dpf calculations accompanied by reducing the scenario size. there are a few scenarios needed for the srsm, so the sample size for the dpf calculations is small. there are not a large number of responses in the srsm authorized licensed use limited to: fh technikum wien. downloaded on may 11,2026 at 14:08:53 utc from ieee xplore. restrictions apply. fu et al.: statistical machine learning model for stochastic optimal planning of distribution networks 2911 fig. 2. thermal behavior of an hv ac unit. calculation results, but the mathematical expectation and vari- ance are present. the application of chebyshev inequality only requires the existence of a mathematical expectation and variance. however, monte carlo requires many com- puting resources. in essence, the chebyshev inequality can estimate a reliable constraint level with only a small number of dpf calculations, and this advantage is why we chose the chebyshev inequality. vi. c ase study and discussions we use a step-by-step validation method to demonstrate our example. first, we introduce the simulation results of a sin- gl