Gao2025,2025,"Multi-objective optimization and posteriori multi-criteria decision making on an integrative solid oxide fuel cell cooling, heating and power system with semi-empirical model-driven co-simulation",yes,yes,Neural network,high,8,PCE / RSM,0,pdf,ical model or machine learning model. yuan et al. [20] optimized a chp system energy effi- ciency based on an artificial neural network model of input operating parameters and output efficiency relationship through the genetic al- gorithm calculation. the results showed that the syste,,unassigned,7,Latin hypercube sampling,0,,,P5 uncertainty,high,9,P1 replacement,0,pdf,". a self-sufficient combined cooling, heating and power system is modelled integrating with a semi- empirical surrogate model of solid oxide fuel cell to interact with other balance of plant types efficiently. total energy efficiency and annual total cost are optimized through parametrical analysis on device size of each component (battery, electrolyzer and solid oxide fuel cell) and analysis of variance for contribution ratio quantification. results indicate that, the size increase in electrolyzer and solid oxide fuel cell will improve system total energy efficiency by 13.635 % and 2.194 %, but promote annual total cost by 4.042 × 10 4 $ and 2.389 × 10 3 $, respectively. besides, sensitivity analysis indicates that the electrolyzer size prioritizes other design pa- rameters in techno-economic performance. optimal sizes of battery, electrolyzer and solid oxide fuel cell are in cell number range of 333 – 403, 17 – 20, and 26 – 30, respectively, with corresponding optimal total energy efficiency and annual total cost at 70.861 % – 72.147 % and 6.723 × 10 4 $ – 7.325 × 10 4 $, respectively. the research results can provide guidance on hydrogen-based cooling, heating and power system design and oper- ation with techno-economic feasibility for low-carbon district energy transition. 1. introduction as a common pursuit of carbon neutrality target around the world [1] , hydrogen energy has experienced substantial growth in both in- dustrial sectors and academic circles in recent years [2] , due to its prominent merits of extreme clean [3] , high enthalpy [4] , carbon free and easy long-term storage [5] . solid oxide fuel cell (sofc) has been attracted increasing attention because of its ability to convert hydrogen into heat and electricity with high-quality heat. sofc stack consists of series of cells containing interconnected bipolar plate, anode and cath- ode electrodes, and electrolyte for transporting ions. wherein hydrogen will be oxidized and release electrons in anode. the released electrons will be transported into cathode by outside circuit, and reduce oxygen into ions in cathode. afterwards, the oxygen ions will be transported to anode across the electrolyte, and combined with hydrogen ions to generate water. there are several types of sofc, such as samarium- doped ceria (sdc) and yttria stabilized zirconia (ysz) for electrolyte * corresponding author at: sustainable energy and environment thrust, function hub, the hong kong university of science and technology (guangzhou), nansha, guangzhou, 511400 guangdong, china. e-mail address: yuekuan.zhou@outlook.com (y. zhou). contents lists available at sciencedirect energy conversion and management journal homepag e: www.el sevier.com/loc ate/enconm an https://doi.org/10.1016/j.enconman.2024.119371 received 10 september 2024; received in revised form 29 november 2024; accepted 2 december 2024 energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 available online 9 december 2024 0196-8904/© 2024 elsevier ltd. all rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, ai training, and similar technologies. material. compared to ysz-based sofc with operating temperature above 800 ◦ c, sdc-based sofc operates under the temperature of 500 ◦ c – 800 ◦ c [6] , which is much more time- and energy-saving during start up and long-time operation, and gained increasing concentrations by academia recently [7] . the sofc has been applied in combined heating and power (chp) system or combined cooling, heating and power (cchp) system to cover the daily energy demands of buildings, contributing significantly to carbon neutrality transformation in green buildings [8] . the cchp/chp system will be integrated with other devices to adapt to variable sce- narios, such as photovoltaic (pv) panel [9] , battery [10] and electro- lyzer [11] , for long-time standalone operations. those key device sizes, i.e., the cell number of the battery, electrolyzer, sofc and etc., play a significant role in system economic feasibility and energy efficiency. modeling analysis is an effective way to investigate the device size ef- fects, with attributes of high efficiency and cost saving compared to in- field experiments [12] . by relying on computational simulation, it is necessary to guarantee the detailed information of integrated components and calculation efficiency of the cchp/chp system modeling for overall performance analysis. besides, the capacity size of key devices will determine the invest- ment cost of the cchp/chp system [13] , and affect the system economic efficiency and implementation feasibility. on the other hand, the depth of discharge (dod) and service lifetime (like battery and sofc) will also be dependent on their sizes [14] . therefore, it is necessary to design appropriate sizes of key devices to avoid performance overestimation and improve techno-economic feasibility. optimization analysis is an effective way to identify the optimal designs of cchp/chp systems, including operating control [15] , ma- terial selection [16] and structure size [17] . high-dimensional modeling is usually used for device performance analysis and figuring out the system detailed characteristics by optimization. pang et al. [18] opti- mized a steam ejector applied in a chp system by comparing three design cases, through the finite element modeling and computational fluid dynamics analysis in fluent software, which will consume a large amount of time for device performance calculation. they found that the nomenclature a area, m 2 c specific heat capacity, j/(kg ⋅ k) ca battery capacity, kwh cn cycle number cost cost, $ cr capital recovery factor d depth of discharge d thickness, m e potential; overpotential, v δ e voltage degradation, v f faraday constant, c/mol f energy efficiency i current, a i current density, a/m 2 l lifetime lhv low heat value, j/kg m molar mass, kg/mol m mass flow rate, kg/s; mass, kg mf mass fraction n cell number n electron number p power, w p pressure, atm q cooling capacity, kw r gas constant, j/(mol ⋅ k) rc relative capacity δ rc degradation rate r interest rate s surface area, m 2 t temperature, k t time period, h w energy, j greek symbols α stoichiometric ratio β , γ , δ coefficient ζ maintenance coefficient κ electron conductivity, s/m λ membrane water content μ degradation rate, μ v/h σ ionic conductivity, s/m ω degradation rate, %/h subscripts and superscripts a anode act active am ambience ba battery c cathode cell cell co consumption com compressor con concentration cr constant rated power state ct constant turning power state de degradation dep depreciation eff effective el electrolyzer ele electrolyte fl low power fluctuation state fh high power fluctuation state ge generation he heat exchanger in inlet idling idling state ini initial state int interconnector lim limiting current density loss loss main maintenance max maximum mt maintenance state ner nernst ohm ohmic out outlet peak peak point ra radiation re reversible potential s solid oxide fuel cell sem semi-empirical sys system valley valley point t thermal; time point total total b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 2 ejector designed with the optimized thermodynamic method possessed higher heat recovery efficiency. gao et al. [19] proposed a cross-scale model of chp system and established a fuel cell device model by computational fluid dynamics analysis in fluent software, aiming to improve the fuel cell model accuracy. it is distinct that detailed modeling in high-dimensional is time-consuming for calculation because it needs to be solved by nonlinearly iterative calculation based on gradient descent method in each boundary condition solving, not to mention the global optimization by algorithm. while in order to achieve high-efficiency global optimization, the system devices are usually established in zero dimension, such as physical model or machine learning model. yuan et al. [20] optimized a chp system energy effi- ciency based on an artificial neural network model of input operating parameters and output efficiency relationship through the genetic al- gorithm calculation. the results showed that the system energy effi- ciency reached 95.62 %. liu et al. [21] developed a zero-dimensional sofc cchp system model to analyze the thermal-economic perfor- mance by multi-objective optimization. they found that the total energy output and exergy efficiency could increase by 35.58 kw and 0.20 %, respectively. besides, some research also explores methods to balance the calculation efficiency and model complexity. darzi et al. [22] pro- posed a partial high-dimensional modeling method to optimize the spark plug location in chp system, namely one-dimensional modeling of en- gine and three-dimensional modeling of cold flow in engine space, which can improve the calculation efficiency and guarantee the model accuracy to some extent, whereas the calculation efficiency is still limited. obviously, high-dimensional model is hard to be applied in long-time scenario simulation and optimization, and device modeling is usually simplified to achieve a high-efficiency calculation in global optimization, with sacrifice or simplification of the device detailed characteristics. it is highly necessary to balance both model complexity and computational efficiency in the multi-scale optimization. sizing analyses of cchp/chp systems have been conducted to reduce total cost [23], improve energy saving [24], and reduce carbon emission [25], etc. liu et al. [25] analyzed the capacity of prime mover in a cchp system for optimal economic feasibility, and finally they identified the best capacity of the prime mover at 78.146 kw. shahsavari et al. [26] analyzed the best energy cost of a chp system in rural resi- dential areas by sizing the system configuration, and they found that the optimal cost could be achieved through the design of 39.4-kw pv, 10- kw diesel generators, 20.1-kw converter, device number of 20 wind turbines and 96 batteries. benalcazar et al. [27] proposed a mixed- integer linear programming approach to find the optimal capacity of a chp system thermal energy storage. they found that the integration of thermal energy storage capacity would save the cost of € 0.57 m – € 0.64 m in poland. wang et al. [23] investigated the optimal size of equipment in the photovoltaic/thermal (pv/t) panel cchp system by a bi-level optimization. the results indicate that the improvement of pv/t- cchp system size could save the annual total cost of 5.82 % and reduce the carbon emission of 37.51 %. however, there are few studies about sofc cchp/chp system sizing, and the cchp/chp system per- formance is highly dependent on the sofc electrical/thermal efficiency, while the impacts of sofc size on the sofc electrical/thermal efficiency has been rarely discussed. besides, the system sizing research has sel- domly connected the device size design with its degradation perfor- mance, and this ignorance will lead to inaccurate estimations on the system lifetime and economic efficiency. the collaborative sizing optimization of multiple devices in cchp/ chp system is able to improve the system performance comprehensively [23]. guo et al. [28] optimized the sizes of the proton exchange mem- brane fuel cell (pemfc) and auxiliary chiller in a cchp system. it indicated that the system energy efficiency could be improved by 23.21 %. wang et al. [29] optimized the device capacity size of a cchp system by multi-objective optimization algorithm for optimal carbon emission and economic cost. the results showed that the carbon emission and economic cost have been reduced by 99.81 % and 66.70 % respectively compared to single objective optimization. shahsavari et al. [26] iden- tified the power capacity of pv panels, diesel generator, and converter, and installed number of wind turbines, battery and boiler in a chp system. the results indicate that the energy cost could be reduced by 34.8 % under the optimal identification. nonetheless, few studies have discussed the capacity sizing optimization of sofc cchp/chp system. in addition, the contribution ranks of different key devices on cchp/ chp system performances have not been figured out during the sizing optimization, especially combining the impacts of dynamic degradation condition and energy conversion efficiency. based on the above literature review, the research gaps can be summarized as follows: 1) the global optimization analysis of sofc cchp system has usually scarified or simplified the model complexity of detailed character- istics to guarantee the computational efficiency in zero-dimensional modeling, while the simplified model has limited the authenticity and reliability of the cchp system model. it is necessary to well balance the model complexity and computational efficiency of the cchp system. 2) in the academia, studies are quite limited on the sizing analysis regarding the integrative sofc cchp system, and the impact of device size (like battery, electrolyzer and sofc) on their degradation performance has been usually ignored, resulting in inaccurate esti- mation of the overall techno-economic performance. besides, the underlying mechanism of the sofc size on the overall electrical/ thermal efficiency has been seldomly investigated. furthermore, sensitivity analysis of key device sizes on techno-economic perfor- mances of the integrative sofc cchp system has not been figured out. 3) in the academia, studies are quite limited on the size capacity opti- mization of integrative sofc cchp system, and the optimal designs of key device sizes to maximize techno-economic performances of the integrative sofc-cchp system have not been investigated, especially considering the dynamic degradation performance and energy efficiency simultaneously. main contributions of this study are summarized below: 1) in order to well balance both model complexity and computational efficiency of the integrative sofc-cchp system, a multi-software optimization platform is established based on matlab-trnsys co-simulation framework of a cchp system coupling with a sofc semi-empirical surrogate model. this aims to enable the multi- objective optimization with high computational efficiency and model complexity. 2) in order to clearly demonstrate the significance of key device size design (like battery, electrolyzer and sofc) on the integrative sofc- cchp system, the impact of key device size analyses has been con- ducted on dynamic electrical/thermal efficiency and overall techno- economic performance of the integrative sofc-cchp system (like system energy efficiency, degradation level and annual cost), which also considers dynamic degradation performance. furthermore, significance ranks of key device sizes on techno-economic perfor- mances of the integrative sofc-cchp system are identified through the analysis of variance (anova) method, in terms of system total energy efficiency and annual total cost. 3) multi-objective optimization and posteriori multi-criteria decision making on an integrative sofc-cchp system have been conducted. the optimal size designs are selected by normalized euclidean dis- tance along the multi-objective pareto front to identify the ‘best of the best’ solution to maximize system total energy efficiency and minimize annual total cost. this study is organized as follows. section 2 introduces the estab- lishing process of the sofc-cchp system model based on multi-software b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 3 optimization platform integrating with the sofc semi-empirical model; section 3 introduces the device size sensitivity analysis of battery, electrolyzer and sofc on the cchp system total energy efficiency and annual total cost, with the contribution rank identification and poste- riori multi-criteria decision making for the ‘best of the best ’ device sizing. the study results can provide an advanced modeling method of cchp system simulation and optimization for energy and economic ef- ficiency improvement and provide guidance for the cchp system design with high techno-economic feasibility for low-carbon district energy transition. 2. methods the multi-software optimization platform will be introduced in this section, and the modeling process, operating scenario, control strategy and estimation criteria of the cchp system will be illustrated. in the end, the logic flow of the multi-objective optimization based on the multi- software optimization platform will be depicted. the multi-software optimization platform is established with as- sumptions to ensure the simplification and accuracy: fig. 1. multi-software optimization platform based on co-simulation integrating with sofc semi-empirical model. (note: nsga-ii, sofc, cchp, pv, dhw, ac refer to the non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm ii, solid oxide fuel cell, combined cooling, heating and power, photovoltaic, domestic hot water and absorption chiller; the number upon the routes indicate the sequence of the energy/substance flows.). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 4 (1) gas species are all ideal gas transporting in the cchp system; (2) water crossing-membrane effects are not considered in electro- lyzer and sofc; (3) fuel gas in afterburner is burned totally in the excess oxidant mixing; (4) the sofc anode gas consists of h 2 and h 2 o, and cathode gas only contains o 2 , n 2 , and h 2 o. 2.1. multi-software optimization platform fig. 1 describes the framework design of the multi-software optimi- zation platform, consisting of the cross callings between matlab and trnsys. as for matlab-calling-trnsys, the non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm ii (nsga-ii) module in matlab will control the trnsys-calling module to open trnsys and then activate the cchp system model running, and simultaneously input the independent var- iables into the cchp system model by interaction files. the cchp system model in trnsys is integrated with the residential building power de- mands, which is based on the specific climate effects from meteonorm database. the control strategy is conducted on cchp system during the operation process for cost-effectiveness. the cchp system performances (such as energy efficiency, annual cost, etc) will be output back to nsga- ii algorithm module in matlab by interaction files after finishing the scenario operations. as for trnsys-calling-matlab, the cchp system model in trnsys has integrated with the sofc model in matlab by matlab-calling type, and drive the sofc model calculating in each time step during scenario operation. the operating conditions (inlet gas pressure, temperature, species, load current, etc) will be input to the sofc theoretical model to calculate the sofc performance, including voltage, electrical power, thermal power, and etc. afterwards, the fitting coefficients of the sofc semi-empirical model will be estimated based on the load current and electrical/thermal power performances. the estimated semi-empirical model of sofc will then calculate the sofc stack performance corresponding to the variable load current, and interact with other balance of plant (bop) models for high-efficiency response. when the operating boundary conditions (inlet gas pressure, stoichiometric ratio, inlet gas fraction and inlet gas temperature) are changed, the fitting coefficients of the semi-empirical model will be updated by once fitting correspondingly. the cchp system operation is self-sufficient by only renewable en- ergy support without power grid input, which can be applied in some standalone scenarios, such as the building energy coverage in stand- alone islands. the lithium iron phosphate (lfp) battery is adopted for energy storage in cchp system, due to its merits of low cost, high safety and excellent thermal stability [30] . besides, the proton exchange membrane (pem) electrolyzer is used to produce h 2 in cchp system because of its quickly dynamic response and high energy efficiency advantages [31] . as shown in fig. 1 , the pv panels will convert solar energy into electricity, and charge the battery (shown as route 1) for electrical energy storage with charging rate of 0.2c (i.e., 20 % of the capacity for each hour), when the battery state of charge (soc) is below 60 %. the battery charging will not be stopped until the soc reaches 95 %. after the battery is completely charged, the surplus electrical energy will be delivered to electrolyzer for h 2 production through the elec- trolysis process (shown as route 3, 4). specifically, the input power to the electrolyzer will drive the h 2 o in anode to produce o 2 , electrons and protons, and protons will cross the electrolyte to cathode and combine with electrons to produce h 2 . the h 2 from electrolyzer will be stored in hydrogen tank by hydrogen compressor (shown as route 5, 6). after- wards, the h 2 and air will be supplied by hydrogen tank and air compressor respectively (shown as route 7, 10) to sofc stack for elec- tricity and heat production. the h 2 and air flow will be heated up in gas heat exchanger (high-temperature exhaust mixed gas) and gas heater (electrical power) successively before entering the sofc stack (shown as route 8, 9, 11, 12). after crossing the sofc stack, the surplus h 2 and air are mixed in afterburner for adequate burning to further improve the temperature (shown as route 13, 14). the high-temperature exhaust mixed gas will cross the cathode and anode gas heat exchanger to heat the inlet h 2 and air flow (shown as route 16, 17). then it will cross the absorption chiller (ac) heat exchanger (shown as route 18, 19) to heat the generator of absorption chiller by cycle water (shown as route 20 – 22) for cooling energy supply in summer (shown as route 23 – 26). after that the high-temperature mixed gas will cross the domestic how water (dhw) heat exchanger to heat water (shown as route 27, 28), which is used to supply heating energy to buildings for dhw use and space heating in winter (shown as route 29 – 35). the battery and sofc will both supply electrical energy to cover the building energy demands and bop energy consumption (shown as route 2, 15), and their cooperations will be introduced in section 2.3 . 2.2. combined cooling, heating and power system modeling in this section, the key process of cchp system modeling is illus- trated, and the detailed process can refer to the previous work [32] , including the modeling of battery, electrolyzer, sofc and other bop components. 2.2.1. battery modeling battery effective capacity can be calculated by eq. (1) . ca eff = n ba • ca ini , cell • rc + p ba • t ba (1) where ca eff is the effective battery capacity, wh; n ba is the battery cell number; ca ini , cell is the battery initial cell capacity, wh; p ba is the net charging-discharging power, w; t ba is the battery operating duration time, h; rc is the real-time relative capacity, which is related to the charging-discharging cycle number and relative capacity degradation of each cycle, calculated by eq. (2) [33] . rc = 100%- ∑ cn i = 1 | δ rc | (2) where cn is the cycle number; δ rc is the relative capacity degradation in each cycle, corresponding to the cycle number and each cycle dod, calculated by eqs. (3) – (4) . δ rc = β 1 ⋅ cn 2 + β 2 ⋅ cn + β 3 (3) β j = ∑ 8 i = 0 γ j , i ⋅ d i (4) where β j ( j = 1, 2, 3) is the coefficients related to the depth of discharge d ; γ j,i is the coefficients for polynomial fitting between d and δ rc co- efficients, as shown in table 1 . the battery depth of discharge d can be calculated by eq. (5) . d = soc peak -soc valley (5) where soc peak and soc valley are the peak value and valley value of the state of charge in a certain cycle number. 2.2.2. electrolyzer modeling the electrolyzer produced h 2 mass rate m h 2 , ge (kg/s) can be calcu- lated by eq. (6) . m h 2 , ge = i el ⋅ n el ⋅ m h 2 n ⋅ f (6) where n el is the electrolyzer cell number; m h 2 is the h 2 molar mass, kg/ mol; n is the electron number; f is the faraday constant, 96,485c/mol; i el is the electrolyzer input current (a), calculated by eq. (7) . i el = p el /( n el ⋅ e eff el , cell ) (7) where p el is the total input power to electrolyzer, w; e eff el , cell is the b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 5 electrolyzer effective cell voltage (v), calculated by eq. (8) . e eff el , cell = e el , cell + δ e el (8) where e el,cell is the electrolyzer cell voltage, v; δ e el is the degradation voltage of electrolyzer. wherein the electrolyzer cell voltage e el,cell can be calculated by eq. (9) . e el , cell = e el , re + e el , act + e el , ohm (9) where e el,re is the reversible potential (v), described as eq. (10) . e el , re = 1 . 229-8 . 5 × 10 - 4 ⋅ ( t el -298 . 15 ) (10) where t el is the operating temperature, 353.15 k [34] . e el,act is the active overpotential (v), calculated by eq. (11) [35] . e el , act = rt el f ⋅ ln ⎛ ⎝ 1 2i el , a + ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅ 1 + ( i el 2i el , a ) 2 √ ⎞ ⎠ + rt el f ⋅ ln ⎛ ⎝ 1 2i el , c + ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅̅̅̅̅̅ 1 + ( i el 2i el , c ) 2 √ ⎞ ⎠ (11) where r is the gas constant, 8.314 j/(mol ⋅ k); i el,a and i el,c are the anode and cathode exchange current density of electrolyzer, a/m 2 . e el,ohm is the ohmic overpotential (v), calculated by eq. (12) . e el , ohm = i el ⋅ ∫ d el o dx σ ( λ ) (12) where d el is the thickness of electrolyzer membrane, 5 × 10 -5 m [35] ; δ ( λ ) is the ionic conductivity (s/m), which is calculated by eq. (13) . σ ( λ ) = ( 0 . 513 λ -0 . 326 ) ⋅ exp [ 1268 ⋅ ( 1 303 - 1 t el ) ] (13) where λ is the membrane water content. the degradation voltage δ e el is calculated by eq. (14) [36] . δ e el = n el ⋅ ( t mt ⋅ μ mt + t ⋅ μ fl + t ct ⋅ μ ct + t ⋅ μ fh + t cr ⋅ μ cr ) (14) where t mt , t fl , t ct , t fh , t cr are the operating time duration of the mainte- nance operation, low power fluctuation operation, constant turning power operation, high power fluctuation operation and constant rated power operation; μ mt , μ fl , μ ct , μ fh , μ cr are the voltage degradation rate in corresponding conditions, as shown in table 2 . the electrolyzer energy loss includes idling energy loss, auxiliary energy loss, overpotential energy loss and degradation energy loss, expressed as eq. (15) . w el , loss = w el , idling + w el , auxi + w el , e + w el , de (15) the idling energy loss of electrolyzer w el,idling can be calculated by eq. (16) . w el , idling = ∫ 8760 0 v el , idling ⋅ i el , idling dt (16) where v el,idling is the electrolyzer idling voltage (v), namely the mini- mum allowable voltage for activation; i el,idling is the corresponding current under the idling voltage, a. the auxiliary energy loss w el,auxi is the water pump energy consumption for the electrolyzer cooling, which can be output from tnrsys types. the overpotential energy loss w el, e is calculated by eq. (17) . w el , e = ∫ 8760 0 i el ⋅ ␀ e el , act + e el , ohm ) dt (17) the degradation energy loss w el,de is calculated by eq. (18) . w el , de = ∫ 8760 0 i el ⋅δ e el dt (18) 2.2.3. solid oxide fuel cell theoretical modeling the cell number of sofc affects the electrical/thermal energy effi- ciency, as shown in fig. 2 (a). specifically, when the output electrical power demand of sofc stack is similar, each cell of sofc stack will hold a large power load if the stack cell number is small. on the contrary, each cell power load will be low if the stack cell number is large. thus, the sofc cell power of large cell number design will be lower than that of small cell number design when covering a similar electrical power demand. a lower cell power of sofc indicates a higher cell voltage and lower overpotential, i.e., the higher electrical energy efficiency and lower thermal energy efficiency. besides, the performance degradation will promote thermal energy efficiency, as shown in fig. 2 (b). the sofc contains a lower cell voltage after degradation when covering a similar electrical power demand, indicating that sofc has a higher over- potential with more heat production. thus, the sofc cell number and performance degradation effects are non-negligible on combined heat- ing and power co-supply during the long-time operation. the sofc stack electrical power p s,e is calculated by eq. (19) . p s , e = e eff s , cell ⋅ i s ⋅ a s ⋅ n s (19) where e eff s , cell is the sofc effective cell voltage, v; i s is the sofc current density, a/m 2 ; a s is the active area of sofc, m 2 ; n s is the sofc cell number. the sofc thermal power p s,t can be calculated by eq. (20) . p s , t = ( 1 . 254-e eff s , cell ) ⋅ i s ⋅ a s ⋅ n s (20) the effective sofc cell voltage e eff s , cell is described as eq. (21) . e eff s , cell = e s , cell ⋅ ( 1- ω s ⋅ t s ) (21) where e s,cell is the sofc cell voltage, v; ω s is the sofc performance degradation rate, %/h; t s is the sofc operating time duration, h. wherein the sofc cell voltage e s,cell is calculated by eq. (22) . table 1 the coefficients γ j,i for polynomial fitting [32] . γ j,i i = 0 i = 1 i = 2 i = 3 i = 4 i = 5 i = 6 i = 7 i = 8 j = 1 ␀ 7.076 × 10 -11 8.014 × 10 -10 ␀ 6.182 × 10 -9 1.406 × 10 -8 0 ␀ 3.915 × 10 -8 0 6.358 × 10 -8 ␀ 4.086 × 10 -8 j = 2 7.654 × 10 -7 ␀ 2.129 × 10 -6 2.446 × 10 -5 ␀ 5.134 × 10 -5 0 1.742 × 10 -4 0 ␀