 2 ) (26) where i lim , h 2 , i lim , h 2 o , and i lim , o 2 are the limiting current density of h 2 , h 2 o and o 2 . the performance degradation rate ω s (%/h) is calculated by eq. (27) [39] . ω s = 0 . 59 ⋅ mf h 2 + 0 . 74 1000 [ 1 + exp ( t s - 1087 22 . 92 ) ] [ exp ( 2 . 64i s 10000 ) -1 ] (27) where mf h 2 is the mass fraction of h 2 consumption. the thermal energy conservation of sofc stack can be described by eq. (28) . p in t , a + p in t , c + p s , t = p out t , a + p out t , c + p t , loss (28) where p in t , a , p in t , c , p out t , a , p out t , c , and p t , loss refer to the anode inlet thermal power, cathode inlet thermal power, anode outlet thermal power, cathode outlet thermal power, and thermal power loss in ambience. wherein the thermal power loss p t,loss in ambience can be calculated by eq. (29) . p t , loss = ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ h ∫ s 0 ( t am -t s ) ds + σ ⋅ γ ⋅ ∫ s 0 ␀ t 4 am -t 4 s ) ds ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ (29) where h is the convective heat transfer coefficient, 9 w/(m 2 ⋅ k) [4] ; s is the surface area of the sofc stack; σ is the stefan-boltzmann constant, 5.67 × 10 -8 w/(m 2 ⋅ k 4 ); γ is the emissivity of the sofc surface, 0.5 [40] ; t am is the ambient temperature, k. in order to improve the authenticity and detailedness of sofc model, the outlet gas temperature of sofc stack is calculated by nonlinear conservation solving method of least square method (lsm) based on the thermal energy conservation, which can be described by eq. (30) . t out gas = fsolve ( p in t , a + p in t , c + p s , t -p out t , a -p out t , c -p t , loss = 0 ) (30) where t out gas is the outlet gas temperature of the sofc stack, k; fsolve refer to the lsm solver, and the numerical relationships between gas temperature and thermal powers can refer to the previous work [32] . the anode inlet mass flow rate of sofc stack m a (kg/s) is calculated as eq. (31) . m a = i s ⋅ a s ⋅ n s ⋅ m h 2 ⋅ α a n h 2 ⋅ f ⋅ mf in h 2 (31) where α a is the anode stoichiometric ratio; mf in h 2 is the inlet h 2 mass fraction. the cathode inlet mass flow rate of sofc stack m c (kg/s) is calculated as eq. (32) . fig. 2. effects of sofc cell number and performance degradation on cell voltage for electrical/thermal power generation: (a) cell number effects; (b) performance degradation effects. (note: p demand , p s,cell and e s,cell refer to the output electrical power demand, sofc cell power and sofc cell voltage). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 7 m c = i s ⋅ a s ⋅ n s ⋅ m o 2 ⋅ α c n o 2 ⋅ f ⋅ mf in o 2 (32) where α c is the cathode stoichiometric ratio; mf in o 2 is the inlet o 2 mass fraction. the battery, electrolyzer and sofc parameter settings of structures, materials and operating conditions are illustrated in table 3 . 2.2.4. solid oxide fuel cell semi-empirical modeling due to the time-consuming characteristic of nonlinear conservation solving to calculate sofc stack performance in each time step during year-scale scenarios, a semi-empirical model of sofc stack is developed to surrogate the theoretical model, for high-efficiency interaction response with bop models during the system operation, aiming to accelerate the multi-objective optimization process. the semi-empirical cell voltage e sem s , cell can be calculated by eq. (33) . e sem s , cell = 1 . 254-e sem s , act -e sem s , ohm -e sem s , con (33) where e sem s , act , e sem s , ohmic , and e sem s , con refer to the semi-empirical active over- potential, semi-empirical ohmic overpotential, and semi-empirical concentration overpotential, calculated by eqs. (34) – (36) [19] . e sem s , act = δ 1 ⋅ ln ( i s ) (34) e sem s , ohm = δ 2 ⋅ i s (35) e sem s , con = δ 3 ⋅ exp ( δ 4 ⋅ i s ) (36) where δ 1 , δ 2 , δ 3 , δ 4 indicate the fitting coefficients changed with the operating condition variations. the semi-empirical electrical power p e,sem is calculated by eq. (37) . p e , sem = e sem s , cell ⋅ ( 1- ω s ⋅ t s ) ⋅ i s ⋅ a s ⋅ n s (37) the semi-empirical thermal power p t,sem is calculated by eq. (38) . p t , sem = [ 1 . 254-e sem s , cell ⋅ ( 1- ω s ⋅ t s ) ] ⋅ i s ⋅ a s ⋅ n s (38) the outlet gas temperature t out sem , gas is calculated by eq. (39) . t out sem , gas = t in gas + p t , sem δ 5 ⋅ c gas ⋅ m gas (39) where t in gas is the inlet gas temperature of sofc stack, k; δ 5 is the fitting coefficient; c gas and m gas refer to the average specific heat capacity (j/ (kg ⋅ k)) and average mass flow rate (kg/s) of the inlet and outlet mixed gas of sofc stack. 2.2.5. balance of plant component modeling the bop components of cchp system include pv panels, inverter, compressor, heat exchanger, heater, afterburner, pump, absorption chiller, air heater, hot water tank, water diverter, water mixer, and building power demand type. the afterburner model is established based on the energy conservation solving, and other bop components are selected from trnsys types. the modeling process and parameter settings of bop component types can refer to the previous work [32] . 2.3. scenario and control strategy in this section, the operating scenario and corresponding control strategy of the cchp system will be described, which will operate in a residential building in beijing, china. 2.3.1. scenario the cchp system will be applied in a residential building located in beijing, china (temperate area). the dhw supply temperature in buildings is set as 60 ◦ c, and the temperature setting of cooling air for space cooling is 22 ◦ c. fig. 3 exhibits the electrical power demand, dhw heating power demand, space cooling power demand, and space heating power demand of the residential building during a whole year [41] . specifically, the dhw heating power demand is stable around the year. the electrical power will be much higher during winter (0 – 1460 h, 8030 – 8760 h) and summer (3650 – 5840 h), with peak value of 10.005 kw. the space cooling power demand and space heating power demand only appear in summer and winter, with peak values of 8.006 kw and 8.331 kw, respectively. in order to guarantee the computational effi- ciency, the time step size of the simulation is set as 0.25 h. thus, the transient behaviors of the devices maintaining within 0.25 h will not be considered in the simulation. 2.3.2. control strategy the control strategy of the cchp system has distinguished different dominated power-supply devices (battery and sofc) during daily operation, and taking two days in winter and summer respectively for instances, fig. 4 illustrates the daily power demands of the residential building and operation conditions of battery and sofc. the peak power demand period during the whole day will be covered by sofc energy supply, i.e., 6:00 am – 10:00 am and 17:00 pm – 2:00 am, and the valley power demand period will be covered by battery energy supply, including periods of 2:00 am – 6:00 am and 10:00 am – 17:00 am. be- sides, the start-up period of sofc stack will be set as 2 h [42] (4:00 am – 6:00 am and 15:00 am – 17:00 am), during which the battery will supply the bop component operation and assist the sofc stack to heat up from the ambient temperature (non-working state) to setpoint temperature (preparing-working state). the period-classified power supply by sofc and battery alternating domination is able to achieve more cost-effective performance, due to the alternately intermittent rest of the power supply devices, which can reduce by 11.83 % annual total cost compared to the operation strategy of both battery (only bop energy supply) and sofc (only building demand supply) working continuously without rest [32] . 2.4. multi-criteria performance estimation the estimation criteria of the cchp system performance include total energy efficiency and annual total cost. the system total energy efficiency f sys can be calculated by eq. (40) [43] . f sys = ∫ 8760 0 ( p sys e + p sys heating + p sys cooling ) dt ∫ 8760 0 ( p ra a pv n pv ) dt + w ba , ini + w h 2 , ini (40) table 3 the parameter settings of the key devices [32] . devices parameter settings values battery cell capacity (wh) 400 electrolyzer active area (m 2 ) 0.125 operating temperature (k) 353.15 minimum allowable cell voltage (v) 1.4 membrane thickness (m) 5 × 10 -5 membrane interface water content λ 14 sofc active area (m 2 ) 0.25 anode thickness (m) 4.00 × 10 -4 cathode thickness (m) 4.00 × 10 -5 electrolyte thickness (m) 2.00 × 10 -5 interconnector thickness (m) 8.50 × 10 -5 anode inlet gas temperature (k) 873.15 cathode inlet gas temperature (k) 873.15 anode inlet gas pressure (atm) 2 cathode inlet gas pressure (atm) 2 anode inlet h 2 mass fraction (%) 50 anode inlet h 2 o mass fraction (%) 50 cathode air mass fraction (%) 100 anode stoichiometric ratio 1.1 cathode stoichiometric ratio 5.0 b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 8 where f sys is the system energy efficiency; p sys e is the system electric power output, w; p sys heating is the system heating power output, w; p sys cooling is the system cooling power output, w; p ra is the solar radiation power density, w/m 2 ; a pv is the area of pv panel, m 2 ; n pv is the installed pv panel number; w ba,ini is the battery initial energy consumption, i.e., the capacity difference between year-end condition and initial condition, dependent on the net charging-discharging power p ba , which can be calculated by eq. (41) . w ba , ini = ∫ 8760 0 p ba ⋅ t ba dt (41) w h 2 , ini is the h 2 initial energy consumption, calculated by eq. (42) . w h 2 , ini = m net h 2 ⋅ lhv h 2 (42) where lhv h 2 is the low heat value of h 2 , j/kg; m net h 2 is the h 2 net con- sumption mass (kg), i.e., the h 2 mass difference in the hydrogen tank between year-end condition and initial condition, calculated by eq. (43) . m net h 2 = ∫ 8760 0 ( m co h 2 -m ge h 2 ) dt (43) where m co h 2 and m ge h 2 is the real-time h 2 consumption rate and generation rate, kg/s. the system annual total cost cost total can be calculated by eq. (44) . cost total = cost ini ⋅ cr + cost main + cost dep (44) where cost ini is the initial investment cost, as shown in table 4 ; cr is the capital recovery factor, calculated by eq. (45) . cr = r ⋅ ( r + 1 ) l ( r + 1 ) l -1 (45) where r is interest rate, 5 %; l is the cchp system lifetime, 20 years [44] . cost main is the maintenance cost ($), which is dependent on the initial investment cost, calculated by eq. (46) . cost main = ζ⋅ cost ini (46) where ζ is the maintenance coefficient, 6 % [43] . cost dep is the annual total depreciation cost ($), indicating the degradation levels of battery, electrolyzer and sofc stack, which can be calculated by eq. (47) . cost dep = rc de rc de , max ⋅ cost ini , ba + δ e el δ e el , max ⋅ cost ini , el + δ e s δ e s , max ⋅ cost ini , s (47) where rc de,max , δ e el,max and δ e s,max refer to the maximum allowable degradation capacity of battery, 20 % [45] , the maximum allowable degradation voltage of electrolyzer, 0.28 v [46] , and the maximum allowable degradation voltage of sofc, 0.2 v [14] ; cost ini,ba , cost ini,el , and cost ini,s are the initial investment costs of battery, electrolyzer and sofc respectively, as shown in table 4 . 2.5. multi-objective optimization by co-simulation the key device sizes (i.e., the cell numbers of battery, electrolyzer and sofc) of the cchp system will be optimized to achieve the optimal annual total cost and total energy efficiency based on the multi-software optimization platform, described as eq. (48) . [ min ( cost total ) , max ( f sys ) ] = function ( n ba , n el , n s ) (48) fig. 5 shows the logic flow chart of the multi-objective optimization process. the original population consists of original battery, electrolyzer and sofc cell number database, and corresponding cchp system per- formances (annual total cost and total energy efficiency). the original population is established by co-simulation. specifically, the original database of battery, electrolyzer and sofc cell number will be generated randomly in the main function, and then input to the cchp system to reconstruct the numerical models of battery, electrolyzer and sofc. after finishing the calculation of the whole-year scenario operation, the performances of cchp system will be delivered back to the main func- tion, including the degradation level, total input energy, fuel con- sumption mass, operating time duration, etc. afterwards, the criteria of annual total cost and total energy efficiency will be calculated. after the original population establishment, the self-checking program will be conducted, and namely the operating duration time of cchp system will be checked to figure out whether the operation is effective. the oper- ating time duration lower than 8760 h (one year) indicates that the operation has been stopped halfway, which means that the cchp system model cannot be solved in such device sizes, and the ineffective device sizes will be output before the program stops. if the operating time duration is equal to 8760 h, the original population will be executed of cross calculation, mutation calculation, non-dominated sorting, crowd- ing distance calculation, and pareto ranking successively. each time of cchp system model solving will activate the self-checking program to guarantee the effectiveness of the population database, including the fig. 3. power load of residential building in beijing including electric power, dhw heating power, cooling load and heating load. (note: dhw refer to the domestic hot water). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 9 cross calculation and mutation calculation. the loop will not be stopped until the setting number of iterations is finished. anova analysis can be used to quantify the influence ability of input factors on the output objectives by significance test [4] , which is effective to identify the contribution ranks of different device sizes to the cchp system performance. the process can be illustrated as: sst = ssf + sse (49) dft = dff + dfe (50) msf = ssf / dff (51) mse = sse / dfe (52) f = msf / mse (53) where sst is the sum of squares of total objective values; ssf is the sum of squares for factor variation influences among different groups; sse is the sum of squares of error effects within group; dft is the total freedom degree; dff is the freedom degree of different factor groups; dfe is the freedom degree of error effects in group; msf is the mean square sum of different factor groups; mse is the mean square sum of error effects in group; f is the f value. the much higher of f value, the much more significant of input factors on the output objectives. in present research, the anova analysis is used to rank the significance of the key device sizing on system techno-economic performances, and give a guidance for the priority of the key device sizing design. the ‘best of the best ’ optimal cases can be selected by normalized fig. 4. daily power demands of the residential building and energy-supply device operation conditions: (a) daily power demands of the residential building; (b) energy-supply device operation conditions. b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 10 euclidean distance judging method based on the pareto front results. the normalized euclidean distance can be calculated by eq. (54) . edis = ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅ ∑ n i = 1 ␀ x n , i -x n , i ) 2 √ (54) where edis refers to the normalized euclidean distance; n is the dimen- sion number of the study cases; x n, i is the normalized study case co- ordinates; x n, i is the normalized optimal case coordinates in theory. the normalized study case coordinates can be calculated by eq. (55) . x n , i = x i -x min x max -x min (55) where x i , x max and x min refer to the study case coordinates, study case maximum coordinate value and study case minimum coordinate value. 3. results and discussions in this section, the sofc semi-empirical model is validated. the size effects of the key device (battery, electrolyzer and sofc) are analyzed on their degradation level, system annual total cost and system total energy efficiency. the optimized key device sizes and their contribution ratios are identified based on optimal annual total cost and total energy efficiency. the battery analyzed size range is set within cell number of 180 – 960 to independently operate for 4 – 22 h before charging. in order to control the working power density below 6 w/cm 2 [36] and cell voltage over the idling value of 1.4 v, the electrolyzer size range is set from 8 to 80 cell number. the sofc analyzed size is set within 15 – 30 cell number to keep the cell voltage within 0.6 – 0.85 v in the scenario to prevent from destructive degradation. 3.1. model validation the theoretical model and semi-empirical model of sofc will be validated by experiments in this section. considering the operating temperature of sdc-based sofc (500 ◦ c – 800 ◦ c [6] ), and large energy consumption in long-time scenario operation of cchp system for high- temperature stabilization, the operating temperature of the sofc will be set as 600 ℃ in present research. the sofc semi-empirical model are validated by comparing with the experimental data [38] , in which the boundary conditions are set as 0.05 kg/s mass flow rate containing vol. 90 % h 2 & vol. 10 % h 2 o in anode and vol. 100 % air in cathode under the operating temperatures of 550 ◦ c and 600 ◦ c. in order to keep pace with the experimental conditions, and verify the generalization of the sofc models, the boundary condition settings of the sofc models are the same as the experiments. the semi-empirical model shows a high accuracy, as shown in fig. 6 . for reference, the r 2 of semi-empirical model data and theoretical model data fitting are 1.000 in 550 ◦ c con- dition and 0.999 in 600 ◦ c condition, indicating the surrogate feasibility of semi-empirical model to the theoretical model. besides, the r 2 be- tween semi-empirical model and experiment results reach 0.959 in 550 ◦ c condition and 0.909 in 600 ◦ c condition, which shows a reliable predicting ability of the sofc model in a large range of operating temperature conditions. 3.2. system energy efficiency analysis this section will introduce the effect analyses of battery, electrolyzer and sofc size designs on the total energy efficiency of the cchp system. 3.2.1. battery effects the annual cchp system total energy efficiency is dependent on the battery initial energy consumption w ba,ini (calculated by eq. (41) ) and h 2 net consumption mass m net h 2 (calculated by eq. (43) ). when the h 2 production mass by electrolyzer is larger than the consumption mass by sofc, the surplus net produced h 2 will be stored in the hydrogen tank (i. e., m net h 2 is minus in eqs. (40) - (43) ). the more net h 2 production mass for storage indicates the more solar energy converting into h 2 energy with less energy loss during the conversion process, leading to a much higher system total energy efficiency. the total energy efficiency of the cchp system is fluctuated in a limited range without any distinct trend with battery size variation, as shown in fig. 7 (a). specifically, the total energy efficiency is variable within a small range of 64.888 % – 64.936 %, when the battery cell number increases from 180 to 960. the fluctuation of total energy ef- ficiency mainly results from the variable battery initial energy con- sumption, compared to the stable variation of h 2 net production mass stored in hydrogen tank. in detail, the battery initial energy consump- tion fluctuates in the range of 6.235 × 10 7 j to 3.642 × 10 8 j without any variation trends along with the cell number increase. it results from the different charging-discharging cycle times and energy of the battery, as shown in fig. 7 (b), which exhibits the battery charging powers in april with cell number of 540 and 600 for examples. taking one week for instance, the battery will be charged on tuesday, wednesday, friday and sunday in cell number of 540, with whole-year total charging en- ergy of 6.049 × 10 10 j. however, the battery will be charged on monday, wednesday, thursday and saturday in cell number of 600, with whole- year total charging energy of 6.077 × 10 10 j. it is because that the battery charging from intermittent solar energy is unavailable during nighttime operation, and it leads to a different dod and discharging energy before sunrise. therefore, the battery sizing designs play a limited effect on the total energy efficiency of the cchp system. 3.2.2. electrolyzer effects the electrolyzer sizing design will affect the h 2 production directly, and meanwhile, the total energy efficiency and h 2 net production mass of the cchp system will be improved when the electrolyzer size is enlarged, as shown in fig. 8 (a). to be more specific, the system total energy efficiency will be improved from 64.966 % to 78.601 % (by 13.635 %), and h 2 net production mass will be raised from 18.960 kg to 579.117 kg (by 560.157 kg), along with the electrolyzer cell number increase from 8 to 80. the underlying mechanism is the reduction of electrolyzer energy losses in its size enlargement, as shown in fig. 8 (b). for example, the total energy loss of electrolyzer (sum of idling energy loss, auxiliary energy loss, overpotential energy loss and degradation energy loss) will decrease from 1.742 × 10 11 j to 1.043 × 10 11 j with the cell number rising from 8 to 80. the reason is that the input power held by each electrolyzer cell will decline under the size enlargement, leading to a much lower cell voltage when holding a similar input power, which indicates a lower overpotential loss. therefore, the enlargement of electrolyzer cell number from 8 to 80 will improve the total energy ef- ficiency of the cchp system by 13.635 % and h 2 net production mass by table 4 initial investment costs of the cchp system. components initial investment cost ($) battery [47] 209 × ca ba electrolyzer [44] 1000 × p el sofc [48] 8194 × p s afterburner [49] 46 . 08 × m in 0 . 995-0 . 98 × ( 1 + e 0 . 018t out - 26 . 4 ) pv panel [50] 82.615 × a pv hydrogen tank [51] 734 × v tank pump [52] 200 × p pump 0.65 compressor [13] 91562 × ( p com 4555 ) 0 . 67 inverter [52] 10 5 × ( p inverter 500 ) 0 . 7 heat exchanger [13] 130 × ( a he 0 . 093 ) 0 . 78 heater [44] 0.3 × m hot water tank [53] 0.954 v tank + 832.7 absorption chiller [44] q cooling 0.67 b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 11 560.157 kg. 3.2.3. sofc effects the electrical energy and thermal energy converted from h 2 energy will be supplied by sofc and afterburner. as shown in fig. 9 , with the increase in sofc cell number, the output electrical energy efficiency of h 2 energy will be improved, but the output thermal energy efficiency of h 2 energy will be reduced. to be more specific, the output electrical energy efficiency will be improved from 65.663 % to 70.659 % (by 4.996 %), but the output thermal energy efficiency will decrease from 26.033 % to 23.678 % (by 2.355 %), when the sofc cell number in- creases from 15 to 30. the underlying mechanism is related to the different sofc cell voltage. specifically, the increase of sofc cell number will reduce the power output by each cell, leading to a higher cell voltage of sofc, which indicates a higher electrical energy con- version efficiency and lower thermal energy conversion efficiency, as shown in fig. 2 . besides, the output electrical energy is increased but thermal energy will be reduced with the increase in sofc cell number. for reference, the sofc electrical energy will increase from 1.137 × 10 11 j to 1.165 × 10 11 j, while the thermal energy will decrease from 4.508 × 10 10 j to 3.903 × 10 10 j. the reason is similar to the energy conversion efficiency with the same variation trends. the enlargement of sofc cell number will play a positive effect on the cchp system total energy efficiency and h 2 net production mass, as shown in fig. 10 . specifically, the system total energy efficiency will be improved from 65.423 % to 67.614 % (by 2.191 %), and the h 2 net production mass will be improved from 47.535 kg to 111.115 kg (by 63.580 kg), with the increase of sofc cell number from 15 to 30. this results from the higher utilization efficiency of h 2 energy with the in- crease in sofc cell number. specifically, the electrical energy efficiency is enhanced with the sofc cell number rise, indicating that sofc will consume less h 2 mass when covering the same electrical energy de- mand. meanwhile, the produced thermal energy is enough to cover both start yes program break error display (labeled as error 1) yes program break error display (labeled as error 2) yes program break error display (labeled as error 3) population combination population crowding distance calculation population non-dominated sorting niteration ≥ nset ? pareto ranking end yes no original battery, electrolyzer and sofc cell number generation cchp system energy efficiency, annual total cost calculation original population generation t = 8760 h ?cell number and operating time output no battery, electrolyzer and sofc cell number cross calculation population cross calculation cchp system energy efficiency, annual total cost calculation battery, electrolyzer and sofc cell number mutation calculation cchp system energy efficiency, annual total cost calculation population mutation calculation t = 8760 h ?cell number and operating time output no t = 8760 h ?cell number and operating time output no cchp system simulation building power demands input battery, electrolyzer, sofc model reconstruction sofc surrogate model reconstruction cchp system operation cchp system performance calculation cell number input performance output interaction fig. 5. logic flow chart of multi-objective optimization based on the multi-software optimization platform. b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 12 heating and cooling demands without requiring more h 2 mass con- sumptions, even if the thermal conversion efficiency decreases. there- fore, the sofc size enlargement from cell number of 15 to 30 will improve the electrical energy efficiency by 4.996 %, system total energy efficiency by 2.194 % and h 2 net production mass by 63.580 kg. in addition, comparing fig. 8 (a) to fig. 10 , the trends of hydrogen net production mass and system total energy efficiency with the elec- trolyzer and sofc cell number rise are closely similar. specifically, the hydrogen net production mass is increased by 560.157 kg and 63.580 kg under the cell number rise of electrolyzer (from 8 to 80) and sofc (from 15 to 30), respectively. the system total energy efficiency is also increased by 13.635 % and 2.191 % by the same size enlargement of electrolyzer and sofc, respectively. the underlying mechanism is the overpotential mitigation of both electrolyzer and sofc with their size enlargement. to be more specific, the power and current held by each cell of electrolyzer and sofc will be reduced with their cell number rise when covering the similar output power load. according to the tafel equation and ohm ’ s law, the lower current held by each cell, the less active overpotential loss and ohmic overpotential loss of electrolyzer and sofc, leading to a higher conversion efficiency of h 2 production and a higher energy efficiency of the cchp system. overall, the size enlargement of battery has little effect on the cchp system total energy efficiency, while the electrolyzer size enlargement of cell number from 8 to 80 will improve the total energy efficiency by fig. 6. comparisons of sofc experimental data, theoretical model data and semi-empirical model data under operating temperature of 550 ◦ c and 600 ◦ c. (note: sofc refers to the solid oxide fuel cell; sofc data comparisons are under boundary conditions of vol. 90 % h 2 & vol. 10 % h 2 o in anode and vol. 100 % air in cathode, and the experiment data refers to [38] ). fig. 7. (a) h 2 net production mass (|m net h 2 |), battery initial energy consumption ( w ba,ini ) and total energy efficiency ( f sys ) of the cchp system during a whole year in variable battery cell numbers; (b) battery charging power with cell number designs of 540 and 600 during a whole year. (notes: h 2 net production mass (|m net h 2 |) is calculated by eq. (43) ; battery initial energy consumption ( w ba,ini ) is calculated by eq. (41) ; system total energy efficiency ( f sys ) is calculated as eq. (40) ). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 13 13.635 %, and the size increase of sofc from cell number of 15 to 30 will improve the electrical energy efficiency by 4.996 % and total energy efficiency by 2.194 %. 3.3. economic performance analysis under degradation in this section, the cchp system annual costs will be analyzed in the variable sizing of battery, electrolyzer and sofc with the performance degradation impacts. 3.3.1. battery economic performance analysis the battery capacity degradation after one-year operation will decrease in general with the cell number rise, as shown in fig. 11 . specifically, the battery capacity degradation amplitude will decrease from 20.556 % to 2.762 % (by 17.794 %) in general with the cell number rise from 180 to 960. the underlying mechanism is the less charging- discharging cycle number in larger battery capacity. in detail, the more cell number of the battery, the higher energy capacity, leading to less charging-discharging cycles when covering a similar energy de- mand. the low frequency of charging-discharging cycle will cause less damage to the battery electrolyte. besides, the battery capacity degradation is also dependent on the dod of each charging-discharging cycle. for example, the battery ca- pacity will decline by 4.186 % in cell number of 540, but by 6.255 % in cell number of 600. the reason is that the battery contains more charging-discharging cycles with high dods in cell number of 600 than that in cell number of 540, and high dod will cause large damage to the battery. in detail, battery contains 5 charging-discharging cycles with dod ≥ 0.5 in cell number of 600, but the dods are all below 0.5 in the cell number of 540 design. the different battery dods of charging- discharging cycles result from the mismatch between power demand output and intermittent solar power input, as described in fig. 7 (b). fig. 12 exhibits the variation of annual total cost, initial investment cost, maintenance cost and depreciation cost of the cchp system along with battery cell number increase. the result indicates that cchp system annual total cost has little correlations to the battery size. specifically, the cchp system annual total cost fluctuates in the range from 1.193 × 10 5 $ to 1.299 × 10 5 $ without any trend with cell number variation. it is fig. 8. h 2 net production mass, total energy efficiency of the cchp system and electrolyzer energy loss in the whole-year operation: (a) h 2 net production mass (|m net h 2 |) and total energy efficiency ( f sys ) of the cchp system; (b) idling energy loss ( w el,idling ), auxiliary energy loss ( w el,auxi ), overpotential energy loss ( w el,e ), degradation energy loss ( w el,de ) and total energy loss ( w el,loss ) of the electrolyzer. (notes: h 2 net production mass (|m net h 2 |) is calculated by eq. (43) ; total energy efficiency is calculated by eq. (40) ; electrolyzer energy loss calculation refers to eqs. (15) – (18) ). fig. 9. impact of sofc cell number designs on output electrical energy, output thermal energy, electrical energy efficiency and thermal energy efficiency of h 2 energy after the whole-year operation. (note: electrical energy is output by sofc stack, and thermal energy is output by both sofc stack and afterburner with electrochemical reaction and burning). fig. 10. hydrogen net production mass and system total energy efficiency in variable sofc stack cell numbers. b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 14 because of the offset effects between initial investment cost increment and depreciation cost reduction. specifically, the initial investment cost increases from 2.612 × 10 4 $ to 3.136 × 10 4 $ with cell number rising from 180 to 960, but the depreciation cost decreases from 9.904 × 10 4 $ to 9.466 × 10 4 $ due to the capacity degradation mitigation with cell number rise. fig. 11. battery degradation amplitudes in different cell numbers during the whole-year operation, and battery cycle numbers under different depth of discharge in cell number designs of 540 and 600. fig. 12. cchp system annual total cost, initial investment cost, maintenance cost and depreciation cost with battery variation from 180 to 960. fig. 13. electrolyzer degradation voltage and cchp system annual costs in variable cell numbers: (a) electrolyzer degradation voltage and period-operating time of representative sizing designs; (b) cchp system annual total cost, initial investment cost, maintenance cost and depreciation cost in variable cell numbers. (note: maintenance operation, low power fluctuation operation, high power fluctuation operation and overrated power operation of electrolyzer refer to the input power density of ≤ 0.6 w/cm 2 , 0.6 – 4 w/cm 2 , 4 w/cm 2 , 4 – 6 w/cm 2 , and > 6 w/cm 2 ). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 15 therefore, the battery capacity degradation is dependent on both size design and charging cycle time, with degradation amplitude declining from 20.556 % to 2.762 % when cell number rising from 180 to 960. meanwhile, annual total cost of the cchp system is independent on the battery size due to the offset effects between battery initial in- vestment cost and depreciation cost variation. 3.3.2. electrolyzer economic performance analysis the electrolyzer voltage degradation will be relieved with cell number rise after whole-year operation, as shown in fig. 13 (a). in detail, the electrolyzer cell voltage degradation amplitude will decrease from 0.150 v to 0.022 v (by 0.128 v) with the cell number rise from 8 to 80. the reason is the decreasing cell power of electrolyzer with the cell number rise, and lower power held by each cell will cause a slighter damage to the electrolyzer materials. for reference, the electrolyzer cell will hold high power density of 4 – 6 w/cm 2 (high power fluctuation period) for 518.75 h and > 6 w/cm 2 (overrated power period) for 69.50 h in cell number of 8, but it will only hold power density of ≤ 4 w/cm 2 (maintenance period and low power fluctuation period) in cell number of 80. the cchp system annual total cost will be raised in general when the electrolyzer cell number increases, as shown in fig. 13 (b). specifically, the cchp system annual total cost will increase from 5.637 × 10 4 $ to 9.679 × 10 4 $ (by 4.042 × 10 4 $) with the electrolyzer cell number rising from 8 to 80. the underlying mechanism is that the increment of initial investment cost (resulting from electrolyzer investment increase) will overwhelm the decrement of depreciation cost (caused by voltage degradation mitigation) with the cell number rise. therefore, the capacity enlargement of electrolyzer (cell number rising from 8 to 80) will mitigate its voltage degradation amplitude (from 0.150 v to 0.022 v), but increase the cchp system annual total cost (from 5.637 × 10 4 $ to 9.679 × 10 4 $). 3.3.3. sofc economic performance analysis the sofc degradation will be mitigated with its cell number in- crease, as shown in fig. 14 (a). for example, the sofc voltage degra- dation rate will decline from 3.590 % to 1.412 % (by 2.178 %) with the cell number rise from 15 to 30. the reason is that the output power shared by each sofc cell is reduced with cell number increase, leading to a lower cell voltage (shown in fig. 2 (a)), and lower cell voltage of sofc will play a slighter damage on the electrolyte. to be more specific, the sofc will operate for 273.50 h with cell voltage < 0.7 v in cell number of 15, but it will operate with cell voltage > 0.7 v all the time in cell number of 30. the annual total cost of the cchp system will be increased with the increase in sofc cell number, as shown in fig. 14 (b). specifically, the cchp system annual total cost will increase from 1.200 × 10 5 $ to 1.224 × 10 5 $ (by 2.389 × 10 3 $) when the sofc cell number rises from 15 to 30. the reason is similar to that of electrolyzer, i.e., along with the sofc cell number increase, the increment of initial investment cost over- whelms the decrement of depreciation cost due to the sofc degradation mitigation. therefore, the sofc size enlargement will relieve its performance degradation, with voltage degradation rate declining from 3.590 % to 1.412 % when cell number rising from 15 to 30. besides, it will also increase the annual total cost of the cchp system from 1.200 × 10 5 $ to 1.224 × 10 5 $. overall, the battery capacity degradation is dependent on both its size and charging cycles, and its capacity degradation will be mitigated from 20.556 % to 2.762 % with its cell number rising from 180 to 960. the size enlargement of electrolyzer and sofc will mitigate their per- formance degradation, but increase the annual cost of the cchp system. the case study shows the increase in system annual total cost by 4.042 × 10 4 $ with the electrolyzer cell number rising from 8 to 80, and by 2.389 × 10 3 $ with the sofc cell number rising from 15 to 30. 3.4. multi-objective optimization and posteriori multi-criteria decision making for integrated energy system sizing based on the above analysis, it is obvious that the size designs of the key devices play trade-off roles in total energy efficiency and annual total cost of the cchp system. the device size enlargement will improve the system total energy efficiency but increase the annual total cost. in this section, the sizes of battery, electrolyzer, and sofc will be opti- mized with objectives of annual total cost and total energy efficiency by calculating 17500 cases based on the nsga-ii algorithm. the contri- butions of each device size to the system performances are then quan- tified by anova analysis. besides, along the pareto front optimal cases, ‘the best of the best ’ cases are then identified by normalized euclidean distance judging, so as to guide the optimal design and operation. fig. 14. sofc voltage degradation rate and cchp system annual cost in variable cell numbers: (a) sofc voltage degradation rate and period-operating time of representative cell number designs; (b) cchp system annual total cost, initial investment cost maintenance cost and depreciation cost in variable cell numbers. (note: maintenance operation, low power operation and high power operation refer to the durations of sofc cell voltage e cell ≥ 0.8 v, 0.7 v < e cell < 0.8 v, and e cell ≤ 0.7 v, respectively.). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 16 3.4.1. co-effect and contribution analyses fig. 15 exhibits the co-effects of device sizes on the annual total cost and total energy efficiency of the cchp system throughout the multi- objective optimization process with 17500 sizing cases. it is distinct that the battery and sofc sizes have little impact on system annual total cost compared to that of the electrolyzer, and cases of low annual total cost are mostly centralized in the scope of small electrolyzer size designs, as shown in fig. 15 (a). specifically, annual total cost of the cchp system will be lower than 7.220 × 10 4 $ when the electrolyzer cell number is less than 30. the reason is that, along with the increase in cell number, the overwhelming effect of initial investment cost increase to the depreciation cost decrease is more obvious in electrolyzer than that of battery and sofc, due to the slighter degradation mitigation in elec- trolyzer with cell number rise. meanwhile, the cases of high system total energy efficiency are centrally distributed in the scope of large electrolyzer size designs, as shown in fig. 15 (b). for instance, the system total energy efficiency will be above 77.250 % when the electrolyzer cell number is more than 65. the underlying mechanism is that the enlargement of electrolyzer size will reduce the energy loss distinctly (as shown in fig. 8 (b)), and the conversion efficiency from solar energy to h 2 energy will be improved significantly. the contribution ratios of the battery, electrolyzer and sofc sizes to the cchp system annual total cost and total energy efficiency are identified by anova analysis based on the 17500 design cases, and the results are exhibited in table 5 . according to table 5 , the electrolyzer size design plays the most significant role in both annual total cost performance and total energy efficiency performance, occupying the contribution ratios of 0.996 and 0.991, respectively. it indicates that the sizing adjustment of electrolyzer can effectively improve the cchp system techno-economic performances, which needs to be prioritized compared to other devices. besides, the contribution ratios of battery (0.007 in total energy efficiency and 0.003 in annual total cost) and sofc (0.002 in total energy efficiency and 0.001 in annual total cost) are very close but much lower than that of electrolyzer. it indicates that adjusting the battery and sofc sizes plays slight roles in system per- formance improvement. therefore, adopting an electrolyzer with low- cost but high-power capacity in practice is the most effective to improve the overall performances of the cchp system. 3.4.2. posteriori multi-criteria decision making from pareto front the results of multi-objective optimization for both optimal cchp system annual total cost and total energy efficiency are shown in fig. 16 , including all the study cases and pareto front cases. as shown in fig. 16 (a), by optimally designing device sizes, the annual total cost can be reduced from 1.019 × 10 5 $ to 4.856 × 10 4 $, and the total energy ef- ficiency can be improved from 64.661 % to 79.997 %. fig. 16 (b) shows the normalized study cases and pareto cases, with 10 selected ‘best of the best ’ optimal cases according to the ascending sort of normalized euclidean distance relative to the optimal case coordinate (0,1), as exhibited in table 6 . distinctly, the best sizes of battery, electrolyzer and sofc are in the cell number range of 333 – 403, 17 – 20, and 26 – 30, respectively. the corresponding optimal range of total energy efficiency is in 70.861 % – 72.147 %, and the optimal range of annual total cost is within 6.723 × 10 4 $ – 7.325 × 10 4 $. therefore, the electrolyzer size design contributes to the cchp sys- tem annual total cost and total energy efficiency mostly, occupying 0.996 and 0.991 respectively based on the anova analysis. the optimal sizes of battery, electrolyzer and sofc are in the cell number range of 333 – 403, 17 – 20, and 26 – 30, respectively. the best ranges of total en- ergy efficiency and annual total cost can be controlled within 70.861 % – 72.147 % and 6.723 × 10 4 $ – 7.325 × 10 4 $, respectively. overall, the study proposed a multi-software optimization platform to analyze the optimal key device sizes of a cchp system consisting of pv, electrolyzer, battery, sofc and absorption chiller. the sofc semi- empirical model is established to substitute the sofc module for high- efficiency interaction with bop component models. impacts of bat- tery, electrolyzer and sofc sizes on system total energy efficiency, de- vice degradations and annual total cost has been comprehensively investigated. the optimal size designs of the key devices have been calculated by multi-objective optimization, and their contribution ratios to system techno-economic performances have been evaluated by anova analysis. however, some limitations need to be noticed as follows: (1) the fitting coefficient estimation of sofc semi-empirical model needs to be updated when the operating parameters change, and the updated process of the semi-empirical model will delay the operating simulation to wait for the training database generation by the sofc theoretical model. further improvement in calcu- lation efficiency is still necessary. (2) the sofc model accuracy is validated in a limited range of operating parameters, such as the operating temperature in 550 ℃ and 600 ℃ , and the generalization of the model can be further improved. (3) the sensitivity and contributions of device sizes are influenced by the cell number selection boundary and operating parameter settings (such as operating temperature, etc). the contribution fig. 15. multi-objective optimization with impacts of battery, electrolyzer and sofc cell numbers on the cchp system annual total cost and total energy efficiency: (a) annual total cost; (b) total energy efficiency. b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 17 ratios and optimal designs of the device sizes would be different when the analyzed cell number boundaries and operating pa- rameters are changed. (4) significance of each criterion has not been considered when making decisions to select the optimal cases. based on the above limitations, the future work will focus on these aspects: (1) a more comprehensive and universal surrogate model of sofc will be established combining the machine learning techniques and semi-empirical models, aiming to reduce the period of delayed time in variable operating conditions. (2) the sofc model can be further calibrated in a larger range of operating parameter conditions to improve the model general- ization performance and practicability. (3) the contribution ratios and optimal designs of device sizes will be further investigated in a larger size boundary and wider operating parameter ranges to obtain more comprehensive size design cases, and guide the system size designs in different capacity demands and operating conditions. (4) a multi-criteria decision strategy can be developed for the device sizing decision considering the significances of different criteria, in order to satisfy the designed requirements adaptive to the variable application scenarios. table 5 anova analysis results for device sizing contributions to the system performances. objectives devices dff dfe msf mse f contribution ratios total energy efficiency battery 78 17,421 3.003 × 10 3 7.608 394.716 0.007 electrolyzer 46 17,453 1.264 × 10 4 0.195 6.482 × 10 4 0.991 sofc 15 17,484 2.128 × 10 3 12.850 165.603 0.002 annual total cost battery 78 17,421 5.052 × 10 10 9.073 × 10 7 556.817 0.003 electrolyzer 46 17,453 1.895 × 10 11 1.041 × 10 6 1.821 × 10 5 0.996 sofc 15 17,484 3.151 × 10 10 1.394 × 10 8 226.040 0.001 (note: the calculation process can refer to the eqs. (49) – (53) ). fig. 16. multi-objective optimisation results of the cchp system annual total cost and total energy efficiency, including all study cases and pareto front cases: (a) study cases before normalization; (b) study cases after normalization. table 6 selected optimal cases of key device sizing parameters. rank device sizing parameters total energy efficiency (%) annual total cost ($) normalized euclidean distance battery cell number electrolyzer cell number sofc cell number 1 394 17 30 71.204 6.803 × 10 4 0.680 2 403 18 28 71.419 6.959 × 10 4 0.684 3 403 18 30 71.572 7.033 × 10 4 0.684 4 370 18 27 71.258 6.889 × 10 4 0.686 5 370 20 26 71.815 7.163 × 10 4 0.687 6 360 19 28 71.672 7.102 × 10 4 0.687 7 378 17 28 70.943 6.752 × 10 4 0.689 8 360 20 30 72.147 7.325 × 10 4 0.690 9 333 18 28 71.289 6.955 × 10 4 0.691 10 403 17 26 70.861 6.723 × 10 4 0.691 (note: the calculation process can refer to the eqs. (54) – (55) ). b. gao and y. zhou energy conversion and management 325 (2025) 119371 18 4. conclusions in this study, a multi-software optimization platform is established based on matlab-trnsys co-simulation framework for a cchp system key device sizing (i.e., battery, electrolyzer and sofc). a sofc semi- empirical model is developed and integrated with the co-simulation framework to substitute the sofc stack for interacting with other bop models for high-efficiency response. the size effects of battery, elec- trolyzer and sofc are analyzed comprehensively on the system total energy efficiency, device degradation level and annual total cost. be- sides, the optimal size designs of battery, electrolyzer and sofc are investigated by multi-objective optimization, considering optimal annual total cost and total energy efficiency. meanwhile, the contribu- tion ratios of the key device sizes are identified on system techno- economic performances by anova analysis. the main results are summarized as follows: 1) to well balance the modelling complexity and computational effi- ciency cchp system, the developed sofc semi-empirical model is highly accurate in both theoretical model surrogate and sofc per- formance prediction. the sofc semi-empirical model can surrogate the theoretical model with fitting r 2 of 1.000 and 0.999 in 550 ◦ c and 600 ◦ c conditions, respectively. meanwhile, the fitting r 2 be- tween semi-empirical model data and experimental data reach 0.959 and 0.909 in 550 ◦ c and 600 ◦ c conditions, respectively. 2) the size enlargement of electrolyzer and sofc will improve the cchp system total energy efficiency but increase the annual total cost. the system total energy efficiency will be improved by 13.635 % but annual total cost will be increased by 4.042 × 10 4 $, with electrolyzer cell number rising from 8 to 80. the electrical energy efficiency and total energy efficiency will be enhanced by 4.996 % and 2.194 % respectively, but annual total cost will be increased by 2.389 × 10 3 $ with sofc cell number rising from 15 to 30. the electrolyzer size design contributes to the cchp system techno- economic performances mostly, occupying 0.991 on total energy efficiency and 0.996 on annual total cost, respectively. 3) the posteriori multi-criteria decision making based on the multi- objective optimization is effective to obtain the optimal the total energy efficiency and annual total cost of the cchp system. the 10 advanced optimal size designs of battery, electrolyzer and sofc are in the cell number range of 333 – 403, 17 – 20, and 26 – 30, respec- tively, and the corresponding optimal total energy efficiency and annual total cost are within 70.861 % – 72.147 %, and 6.723 × 10 4 $ – 7.325 × 10 4 $, respectively. the research results provide a new modeling method by integrating with semi-empirical surrogate model to improve the co-simulation ef- ficiency, and give a guidance for the cchp system sizing for excellent techno-economic feasibility. besides, the contribution rank analysis figures out the device significances in the cchp system design. in addition, the research work contributes to improving the widespread application of hydrogen energy in buildings, and accelerates the decarbonization process in communities, which will enable the carbon neutrality in districts for extreme climate mitigation. credit authorship contribution statement bin gao: writing – original draft, visualization, validation, meth- odology, investigation, formal analysis, data curation, conceptualiza- tion. yuekuan zhou: writing – review & editing, writing – original draft, visualization, validation, supervision, software, resources, project administration, methodology, investigation, funding acquisi- tion, formal analysis, data curation, conceptualization. declaration of competing interest the authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. acknowledgement this work was supported by national natural science foundation of china (nsfc) (52408137), guangdong provincial natural science foundation general project (2414050003253, 2024a1515011609), yangcheng scholars leading talen