Petrova2025,2025,Thermal interference in aquifer thermal energy storage: Insights from stochastic surrogate modelling of well placement,yes,yes,Tree ensembles,high,48,RBF / kernel,17,abstract/keywords,"nimize computational costs, we designed a workflow where the full order physics-based model was replaced by a random forest classifier (rfc) that predicts thermal interference with 97% accuracy accounting for technical and geological uncertainties in a typical deep ht-ates",Quasi-Monte Carlo / sparse-grid collocation,high,8,Latin hypercube sampling,0,pdf,of parameter combinations and sampling uniformly the para- metric space. this is done by using a quasi-random halton sequence with a uniform prior probability density function. physical parameter values of the model range over different scales and have different units (table,P5 uncertainty,high,18,P2 acceleration,8,pdf,"om/locate/ate research paper thermal interference in aquifer thermal energy storage: insights from stochastic surrogate modelling of well placement elena petrova a ,∗, mauro cacace a, stefan kranz a, arno zang a , guido blöcher a,b a gfz german research center for geosciences, potsdam, germany b technical university berlin, department of engineering geology, berlin, germany a r t i c l e i n f o keywords: aquifer thermal energy storage (ates) well placement stochastic modelling ates in urban areas optimal use of subsurface heat recovery factor a b s t r a c t hot temperature aquifer thermal energy storage (ht-ates) systems have gained interest due to their high storage capacity, cost-effectiveness, and environmental sustainability. especially in urban areas with surplus heat, geothermal district heating becomes highly beneficial.wells in ates must be spaced to avoid thermal interference, but urban space and regulations often constrain well placement. in this numerical study, we provide placement criteria for well doublets in urban areas. in contrast to previous model approaches, the approach optimizes the trade-off between thermal interference and well spacing by maximizing energy recovery and minimizing well distance to minimize computational costs, we designed a workflow where the full order physics-based model was replaced by a random forest classifier (rfc) that predicts thermal interference with 97% accuracy accounting for technical and geological uncertainties in a typical deep ht- ates system. the model was applied to an ates system planned in berlin-adlershof. results show that the well distance between 130 and 160 meters is optimal from the thermal and hydraulic perspective over a wide range of parameters. the global sensitivity analysis of the surrogate model reveals that influential parameters for thermal interference are well placement, aquifer thickness, flowrates, charging temperature, permeability, and porosity. by providing insights into key design priorities and enabling an explicit understanding of thermal interference, the developed rfc is an efficient tool for pre-evaluating possible ates configurations and supporting urban planners’ decision making. 1. introduction decarbonization and elimination of greenhouse gas emissions have been in focus of the united nations society since the last 20 years [1]. as one of the solutions, ht-ates systems are an effective and rela- tively low-cost technology for reusing and storing energy [2]. ht-ates enables high-efficiency, seasonal thermal energy storage while offering low operational cost, minimal land use, and near-zero emissions, which makes it a scalable and cost-effective solution for integrating into district heating and cooling systems. in high temperature ates systems, excessive heated water is in- jected into the subsurface in the summer time while the colder water is extracted for cooling the system. during winter, the process is reversed: the previously injected hot water is extracted from production wells and circulated through heat exchangers to heat buildings, with the cold water being reinjected into the aquifer. this ates technology has been successfully implemented in over 1000 sites worldwide, mainly in the netherlands [3], delivering an attractive alternative to carbon energy sources. especially in urban areas which are characterized by ∗ corresponding author. e-mail address: elenape@gfz.de (e. petrova). excessive heat sources, ates has the potential to play a decisive role in the energy storage [see e.g.4,5]. while the appeal of energy storage with ates is significant, its successful implementation requires a thorough understanding of sub- surface properties, alongside a careful planning and management of technical solutions [1,6]. several analytical solutions and key perfor- mance characteristics have been developed to evaluate the efficiency of an ates project [7,8], among those the most widely used is the heat recovery factor (hrf) [9–11]. physics-based numerical models are typically used for simulation of complex natural conditions such as aquifer heterogeneity and reactive behaviour [12–14], prediction of ates efficiency under various ambient flow conditions [15] both at the district scale [16] and city scale [17], as well as targeting interacting systems [18]. the typical outcome of such models are temperature and pressure evolution curves over the whole lifetime of ates system, which allows a proper quantification of the extracted energy and aquifer performance. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.applthermaleng.2025.127012 received 20 march 2025; received in revised form 17 may 2025; accepted 29 may 2025 applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 available online 13 june 2025 1359-4311/© 2025 the authors. published by elsevier ltd. this is an open access article under the cc by license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ). e. petrova et al. from a design perspective, natural parameters such as aquifer prop- erties (porosity, permeability, aquifer thickness, thermal conductivity, and heat capacity) and relatively low ambient flow velocity are neces- sary for high ates efficiency [5,19–21]. at the same time, technical parameters such as distance between wells, charging temperature and flowrate, and duration of injection and extraction cycles define the boundary conditions to be operated in the injection and extraction wells [22–24]. to ensure optimal performance and efficiency, both natural and technical parameters of ates systems have to be carefully estimated [25]. sommer et al. [26] highlighted the importance of an optimal well spacing. recent modelling studies [27,28] demonstrated the rel- evance of the optimization of the initial ates design, which entails an accurate characterization of the system’s parameters. besides tech- nical design parameters, natural aquifer parameters, are influenced by various uncertainties due to limited subsurface data [29,30]. the ensemble-based stochastic modelling has shown its capability in uncertainty reduction and decision making [29,31]. the challenge of stochastic approaches is the high computational cost of a single simulation. to overcome the bottleneck of the high computational cost of stochastic simulations on a spatially resolved hydraulic–thermal model, surrogate modelling approaches have been developed [32,33]. regression-based surrogate models were used to target different ques- tions of optimal design [8,34–37] and sensitivity analysis [38]. rohmer et al. [19] proposed using multifidelity cokriging approach for fast yet accurate ates characterization for technical ates parameters and aquifer properties to predict the evolution of well temperatures for dif- ferent aquifer properties. by using a surrogate modelling approach [28] were able to determine optimal production rate for a site in crystalline rocks. despite significant advancements in uncertainty quantification and regression analysis for optimizing ates design, only limited amount of studies focused on the optimal well spacing by accounting for both tech- nical and natural uncertainties [8,39,40]. current design approaches often rely on generalized analytical equations such as multiplied ther- mal radius [26,27] and only define the minimum distance between the wells while ignoring the maximum distance estimation. this study aims to bridge this gap by newly introducing both hydraulic and thermal criteria on the well placement in the ht-ates doublet. while the thermal criteria define the minimum distance be- tween the wells, the hydraulic criteria are capable to determine the maximum distance, thus leading to the range of possible distances for the doublet system. based on an ensemble of spatially resolved numerical models, we confirmed the hypothesis of asymptotic devel- opment of hrf over the broad parameter ranges, and suggested a possible analytical form of it. to speed up the model evaluation, we developed a model reduction technique that retains the complexity of full-scale models while facilitating efficient predictions. we used the key characteristic of the reduced physics-based model to create a computationally effective surrogate model. to predict ates efficiency and optimize the well distance, a random forest classifier [40,41] was trained on an ensemble of 1500 spatially resolved numerical models. the methodology was applied to an ates project in berlin, germany, where spatial constraints and regulatory requirements make optimal well placement crucial for system success. the outcome was compared with the conventionally used thermal radius approach for the well distance selection. the results offer a scalable solution for ates design in similar urban contexts globally. 2. methods the method section includes the description of the site (section 2.1), the physics-based ates model developed in moose-golem frame- work (section 2.2), the key performance characteristics which lead to reduction of the model order to address model interference (sec- tion 2.3) and the model reduction (section 2.4). 2.1. study area the ates system considered in this study is analogous to the planned ates in the southeast of berlin district of adlershof. this ates utilizes fine-grained jurassic sandstones to store and provide heat for the district heating network. the hettang aquifer sediments are predominantly weakly consolidated, fine-grained sandstones with a high quartz content and varying organic fractions (coal flakes) [16]. the planned ates will consist of two wells with filter zone penetrating the full aquifer thickness at the depth intervals 360 to 390 m (fig. 1). the ates system in berlin adlershof is targeting an aquifer which has a thickness of 30 to 60 m and consists of transgression–regression cycles of clay, siltstones, and sandstones. the hettang jurassic aquifer is isolated from the above positioned artesian aquifer by the rupelian clay layer with thickness variation from 40 to 120 m and the screened thickness at the adlershof site is more than 100 m [42]. with the ambient temperature of 24 ◦c and high salinity, the targeted for ht- ates hettang aquifer is well isolated from the artesian aquifer [43]. the hettang aquifer is characterized by slow (less than 0.1 m/year) ambient groundwater velocity, which makes it plausible for geothermal storage utilization [44]. preliminary site investigations, complemented by regional data, indicated that the permeability ranges from 1 ⋅ 10−13 to 1 ⋅ 10−12 m2, with porosity between 0.1 and 0.4 [16]. for modelling purposes, a permeability range of 1⋅10−14 to 5⋅10−12 m2 is considered to encompass most realistic porous aquifers suitable for ates systems. to ensure a comprehensive approach, no empirical relationship between porosity and permeability was imposed, allowing these parameters to be treated independently. the initial assessment suggests that the hettang aquifer at adlershof is approximately 60 m thick, although it remains uncertain pending further drilling results. to account for lateral variations in aquifer thickness and associated uncertainties, a wide range of thick- nesses from 10 to 100 m were considered in the study. the parameter ranges were extended to account for parametric uncertainty (table 1). the urban setting of the ates site presents specific challenges. due to the dense urbanization and limited access to the properties, the choice of well distance in adlershof is limited. on the existing drilling site, the maximum distance possible in the reservoir depth is approximately 100 metres. because of the complex ownership and leas- ing conditions in the densely populated area, larger distances require additional approvals. therefore, careful planning of the well placement is crucial to optimize the system’s efficiency while complying with regu- latory requirements. from the technological perspective, the adlershof site offers a range of possible technical solutions for the ates system, as summarized in table 1. the considered charging temperatures vary between 70 to 100 ◦c, and charging/extraction rates of 50 to 150 m3/h. in such ht-ates systems, optimizing the distance between wells is crucial for maximizing energy performance. wells placed too close reduce pressure drop and improve hydraulic efficiency, but risk ther- mal interference, which lowers energy recovery. conversely, spacing wells too far apart minimizes thermal interference but increases flow resistance and drawdown, potentially violating hydraulic constraints. thus, both minimum and maximum well distances must be considered to balance thermal and hydraulic performance. 2.2. physics-based ates simulation the ates is simulated by system of pde describing flow and heat transport in a fully saturated aquifer. the flow of fluid through a porous medium is governed by darcy’s law: 𝒒 = − 𝒌 𝜇 ( ∇𝑝 − 𝜌𝑓 𝒈) , (1) where 𝒒 is the darcy velocity (m/s), 𝒌 is the permeability tensor (m2), 𝜇 is the fluid dynamic viscosity (pa s), 𝑝 is the fluid pressure (pa), 𝜌𝑓 is the fluid density (kg/m3), 𝒈 is the gravitational acceleration (m/s2). applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 2 e. petrova et al. fig. 1. conceptual model of ates. table 1 ranges of technical and natural ates parameters used for stochastic modelling. parameters unit min max technical parameters well distance m 50 400 charging temperature ◦c 60 100 flowrates m3/h 50 150 natural parameters permeability m2 1 ⋅ 10−14 5 ⋅ 10−12 porosity – 0.1 0.4 the mass conservation equation for fluid flow in the porous medium is given by: 𝜕 ( 𝜙𝜌𝑓 ) 𝜕𝑡 + ∇ ⋅ ( 𝜌𝑓 𝒒) = 𝑠, (2) where 𝜙 is the porosity of the medium (-), 𝑠 denotes sources or sinks, and 𝜕(⋅) 𝜕𝑡 is the time derivative. the heat transport equation, considering both advection and con- duction, is: ( 𝜕𝜌𝑏𝑐𝑝𝑏 𝑇 ) 𝜕𝑡 + ∇ ⋅ ( 𝜌𝑓 𝑐𝑝𝑓 𝒒𝑇 − 𝝀∇𝑇 ) = 𝑓 , (3) where 𝑇 is the temperature (k), 𝜌𝑏 is the bulk density (kg/m3), 𝑐𝑝 is the specific heat (j/(kg k)), where the subscripts 𝑓 and 𝑏 stand for fluid and bulk, respectively, 𝝀 is the thermal conductivity tensor, which for porous media transport equals 𝝀 = 𝜙𝜌𝑓 𝑐𝑝𝑓 𝑫𝑇 with 𝑫𝑇 being the thermal dispersion tensor (m2/s), and 𝑓 is denoting source/sink terms. the initial conditions refer to the natural temperature gradient in the 3d domain: 𝑇 (𝐱, 𝐲, 𝐳, 𝑡 = 0) = 𝑇nat(𝐱, 𝐲, 𝐳), (4) to condition the model to the natural state, dirichlet boundary con- ditions were applied at the lateral and bottom boundaries to represent undisturbed geothermal conditions [45,46], while no-flow (neumann) conditions are assumed at the top boundary to reflect low permeability of an aquitard. internal boundary conditions for ates are given by 0.5 years injection and extraction cycles in warm and cold wells. the modelled system will focus on a basic ates setup consisting of two wells penetrating the full aquifer thickness. the excessive hot water is injected into warm well to store the heat while at the same time, aquifer groundwater is extracted from the cold well. after half a year, the previously injected warm water is extracted from the warm well to provide heating, and the cooled (until aquifer temperature) water is injected into the cold well. together, injection and extraction periods from a cycle which is repeated (for example, seasonally) during ates functioning (fig. 1). the study focused on ates where ambient groundwater flow is negligible. lateral boundary conditions were based on global modelling results, considering both expected and observed data from nearby locations. no-flow boundary conditions were applied along the bottom and top horizontal boundaries to simulate impermeable rock layers. the aquifer was assumed to have homogeneous isotropic properties within the model domain. the computational domain was discretized using a mesh of ver- tically extruded triangular prisms, with horizontal spacing varying from 0.1 m around the wells to 50 m at the model’s boundary to ensure the high resolution around the well. this non-uniform grid was generated using the open-source software gmsh [47] (fig. 2, left). coupled flow and heat transport equations within porous media were solved using the finite element software moose-golem [48, 49]. the aquifer was simulated as a single material layer with ho- mogeneous parameters (porosity, permeability, heat capacity). this simplification is commonly used to reduce model complexity and com- putational load, especially at ates planning stages when site-specific heterogeneity data is limited due to limited amount of deep boreholes reflecting on the spatial permeability–porosity distribution. previous studies e.g., frick et al. [46] show that homogeneous models effectively capture dominant transport behaviour. while small-scale features like layering or anisotropy are simplified, the approach offers a solid basis for system understanding and can be extended with spatial variability in future work. density and viscosity were set temperature and pressure dependent updated according to iapws-97 [50]. for compliance, we neglected interactions with adjacent low hydraulic conductivity layers. additionally, we did not consider storage periods where injection and extraction phases are separated by intervals of inactivity. during the simulation, the hot water is injected at a constant flowrate into the aquifer while the ambient temperature water is pro- duced from another well, and after 6 month the flow direction in the wells is reversed. the simulation included ten yearly cycles of continuous injection and extraction, each phase lasting six months. this 10-year period was chosen based on convergence studies, which indi- cate that system behaviour stabilizes over this time window [51]. the performed simulations were stabilizing within the 6 to 10 years period. we made use of an adaptive time stepping based on the computed courant number for the system, with a default time step size of 1 day. charging and extraction flowrates were kept constant throughout the simulation. key model parameters, including porosity, permeability, charging temperatures, flowrates, aquifer thickness, and well spac- ing are detailed further in section 5. the model outputs included a fully 3d, spatio-temporally resolved field of temperature and pressure distributions (see fig. 2, right). 2.3. key performance characteristics one of the most broadly used characteristics of ates efficiency is the heat recovery factor (hrf) which is computed as the ratio between the produced and injected energy [9,52]: 𝐻𝑅𝐹 = ∫ 𝑡2 𝑡=𝑡1 ̇𝑉out(𝑡)𝜌𝑐𝑝 ( 𝑇out,w(𝑡) − 𝑇out,c(𝑡)) 𝑑𝑡 ∫ 𝑡1 𝑡=𝑡0 ̇𝑉1(𝑡)𝜌𝑐𝑝 ( 𝑇in,w(𝑡) − 𝑇in,c(𝑡)) 𝑑𝑡 , (5) where ̇𝑉 , 𝜌, 𝑐𝑝, 𝑇 , 𝑡 are flowrate (m3/h), density of groundwater (kg/m3), specific heat capacity of groundwater (kj/kgk), temperature applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 3 e. petrova et al. fig. 2. model setup: mesh of extruded triangular prisms with a vertical exaggeration of 10 (left); model output: timeseries of pressure and temperature in the cold and warm well (right). (◦ c), and time (0 corresponds to the beginning of injection of the hot water, 1 is stop of injection and start of the extraction, 2 is the end of the cycle). the subscripts out and in indicate discharging energy and charging energy and 𝑤, 𝑐 stand for warm well and cold well respectively. an hrf value of 1 indicates that all injected energy can be recov- ered from the aquifer. an hrf value lower than one indicates that part of the energy is not recovered, due to for example, dispersive–diffusive losses, conditioning of the aquifer due to heating the solid phase of the aquifer, or thermal interference. an ates system must be designed such that the loss term is minimized while hrf is maximized. another important metric for evaluating ates system performance is the stored energy (se). se quantifies thermal losses due to inter- ference with the cold well, defined as the ratio between the energy injected into the aquifer (represented by the rectangular area in fig. 3, top-left) and the energy stored, which is calculated as the difference between the injected temperature and the temperature in the cold well during the charging phase (fig. 3 top-left and bottom-left). for equal injected and extracted volumes se can be expressed as: 𝑆𝐸 = ∫ 𝑡1 𝑡=𝑡0 𝜌𝑐𝑝(𝑇w,in − 𝑇c,out) 𝑑𝑡 ∫ 𝑡1 𝑡=𝑡0 𝜌𝑐𝑝𝑇w,in 𝑑𝑡 , (6) a se value of 1 indicates that all injected energy is successfully stored, while se values below 1 reflect energy losses due to thermal interference. when combined with hrf estimates, these two parame- ters are crucial for assessing the overall technical performance of ates systems. the typical characteristic parameter to limit the thermal influence in a ates system is the thermal radius [9,24]: 𝑅𝑡ℎ = √ 𝑐𝑤 ̇𝑉 𝑡 𝑐𝑎𝜋𝐻𝑛 , (7) where ̇𝑉 , 𝑡, 𝑐𝑤, 𝑐𝑎, 𝐻, and 𝑛 are charging flowrate, duration of the injection, heat capacity per unit of water, bulk heat capacity per unite volume (solid plus fluid), aquifer thickness, and porosity correspond- ingly. this method assumes that the thermal front in the aquifer has a cylindrical shape. the engineering approach is that thermal inter- ference can be effectively avoided if the distance between the wells exceeds 1.5 to 3 times the thermal radius [1]. productivity index (pi) characterizes well performance of ates and shows how much of pressure change is induced by the current extraction flowrate: pi = ̇𝑉 𝛥𝑝 (8) fig. 3. a scheme of the temperature evolution in cold and warm wells during a single injection–extraction cycle: (left) without thermal interference, where heat interference is minimized, demonstrating optimal temperature retention in the wells; (right) with thermal interference, showing heat transfer between wells, resulting in diminished thermal storage efficiency; subscripts w and c correspond to warm well and cold well, respectively. the drawdown in the ates well doublet is the super positioning effect of the cold and warm well. for an isotropic homogeneous aquifer is described by the superposition of theis functions: 𝛥𝑝 = 𝜌𝑓 𝑔 ̇𝑉 4𝜋𝑇 (𝑊 (𝑢1) − 𝑊 (𝑢2)) (9) where 𝑇 is the transmissivity of the aquifer (m2/s), 𝑊 (𝑢) is the theis well function, 𝑢 = 𝑟2𝑆 4𝑇 𝑡 is the dimensionless time parameter, 𝑟 is the radial distance from the pumping well (m) with well diameter for the extraction well and distance between the wells for the injection well, 𝑆 is the storage coefficient (-). the injection well increases pressure, while the extraction well decreases it, with their interaction influenced by well placement, flowrates, and aquifer properties. 2.4. model order reduction to quantify the technical feasibility of an ates system, we would need to compute for each model realization time histories of hrf and se indices (fig. 4 bottom) based on computed charging/discharging temperature timeseries (fig. 4 top) to assess the technical feasibility of ates, it is necessary to compute timeseries hrf and se for each model realization. these indices are typically derived from time series data representing the temperature variations during injection and produc- tion. training a surrogate model to predict these indices directly from applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 4 e. petrova et al. fig. 4. left: model output of a 3d physics-based model with distance between the wells 59 m, charging temperature 86 ◦c, charging flowrate 103.3 m3/h, aquifer permeability 5e−14 m2, porosity 0.3, thickness 44.7 m. right: model output of a 3d physics-based model with distance between the wells 120 m, charging temperature 98 ◦c, charging flowrate 95.4 m3/h, aquifer permeability 7e−14 m2, porosity 0.27, thickness 28.6 m. for both cases, the top plot refers to the temperature time series in the boreholes, the bottom plot shows hrf and se development over the charging–extraction cycles analytical fit of the hrf and se evolution: filled cycles refer to the values obtained from the model, dashed lines refer to the analytical fit (eq. (10)–(11)). time series data presents challenges. while multiple-output surrogate models are increasingly common, studies have shown that training complexity and data requirements grow with the increasing dimension- ality of the output space, which significantly impacts computational effort and model accuracy [53,54]. to enhance the computational efficiency, it is advantageous to reduce the model order rather than using time series data directly [55]. previous studies found out that the efficiency and hrf have asymptotic behaviour [51] (fig. 4, dashed lines at the bottom plot). in this work, we propose to fit the hrf and se time series with simple asymptotic equations: 𝐻𝑅𝐹 (𝑡) = 𝑎 ⋅ 𝑡 𝑏 + 𝑡 , (10) 𝑆𝐸 (𝑡) = 𝑐 ⋅ 𝑡 𝑑 + 𝑡 , (11) where t refers to time since ates start, a, b, c, and d are empirical coefficients. by making use of those analytical coefficients, the data complexity is substantially reduced. in such a way, each model produces only two scalars which allow for a full reconstruction of the hrf or se time series. fig. 4 - top presents an example of a single model output with temperature evolution in the warm and cold well. the corresponding hrf and se computed for each cycle from eq. (5)–(6) are shown as solid circles, while the approximated values, obtained by fitting a and b (c and d for se) coefficients to eqs. (10)–(11), as dashed curves. we note a perfect fit of the derived asymptotic functions (eq. (10) and (11)) to the model output. fitting results for all models are discussed in section 4.1. to validate eq. (10)–(11) across a broad parametric space, the fit of the asymptotic approximation was compared with an ensemble of 1500 full model runs with randomly sampled parameter combi- nations. to fit the function to the model output, we used non-linear least squares method utilizing levenberg–marquardt algorithm imple- mented in ‘‘curve_fit ’’ function in python [56]. in such a way, we reduced the fully resolved model to a simple asymptotic approxima- tion(eq. (10)–(11)) without information loss. the time derivative of se which is represented by 𝑑 coefficient serves as a crucial indicator in the context of thermal interference prediction. a negative derivative suggests a decrease in the system efficiency due to thermal interference (fig. 4, bottom-left). consequently, optimal well placement focuses on monitoring this derivative as a key output metric in model simulations. to target the optimal well spacing, the model can be thus further reduced into a binary output where 1 (d < 0) corresponds to the case when thermal interference is causing a decrease in efficiency and drop in se (fig. 4, bottom-left, and 0 (d ≥ 0) the opposite, when no decrease in hrf and efficiency is observed (fig. 4, bottom-right). 3. surrogate model setup and optimization in section 3.1, we present the workflow for ensemble generation using quasi-random sampling, with adjustments for key parameters. in section 3.2, we detail the approach of training a random forest classifier (rfc) and compute the probability of thermal interference, and outline the global sensitivity analysis technique. in section 3.3, we describe the infill procedure and a confusion matrix as a quality assessment of the surrogate model. 3.1. ensemble generation the surrogate model has to cover all parametric space and reflect on the parameter connections of the physics-based model, which requires high prediction accuracy. we enable this by creating a relatively large ensemble of parameter combinations and sampling uniformly the para- metric space. this is done by using a quasi-random halton sequence with a uniform prior probability density function. physical parameter values of the model range over different scales and have different units (table 1). the permeability and well distance values were log-transformed to smooth over the parameter space. in case of the well distance which varies from 50 to 400 m, we gain more samples from the small distance values, which tend to produce thermal interference. this way, the sampling resolution around small well distances was increased. then, parameters were normalized in their ranges in order to homogenize each of them between 0 and 1. the model is constrained by the appearance of turbulent flow in the vicinity to the well. this was avoided by limiting the charging and extraction rates based on the reynolds number. 3.2. calibration of a surrogate model a single run of a spatially resolved numerical model requires several hours on a standard pc with 8 cores at 2.4 ghz, 16 gb ram, ubuntu 20.04. considering the high computational cost of a single model, this applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 5 e. petrova et al. fig. 5. parity plot for model reduction: mean hrf (left) and se(right) values from the 3d moose-golem simulation (abscissa) against corresponding mean values acquired from the asymptotic approximation (ordinata). the solid black line corresponds to 1:1 ratio. puts the global sensitivity of ht ates simulation under constraint of computational resources. to address this issue, using the proxy models, also referred to as surrogate models, has been shown to be advantageous for maintaining model accuracy while reducing computational efforts [19,28,35]. in this study, we employed rfc to model a binary classification problem with six input parameters: permeability, porosity, aquifer thickness, charging temperature, flowrate, and distance between two wells. the algorithm [41] is an ensemble learning technique that constructs a multitude of decision trees during training and outputs the mode of the classes for classification tasks or the mean prediction for regression tasks. the rfc offers several advantages for our purpose in comparison to other ml methods. as such, rfc is faster to train than gradient boosting methods since it trains trees in parallel which speeds up the training, and has more intuitive hyperparameter tuning, yet allowing the sensitivity analysis [57,58]. the rfc handles larger datasets better than support vector machines and does not necessarily require normalization, and finally, it is targeting to use categorical data which is the goal of the study [58–60]. this method is particularly effective in handling high-dimensional data and mitigates the risk of over-fitting. the model input parameters were used as input features for the rfc with a binary output. we configured the rfc with 100 trees, where each tree was built using a subset of the data and features, selected through bootstrapping and random feature selection, respectively. the implementation of the classifier was performed through the ‘‘random- forestclassifier’’ function in the ‘‘sklearn’’ python library [61]. to optimize model performance, the hyperparameters were tuned using grid search with cross validation [61]. key parameters included the 100 of trees estimators, the maximum depth of each tree 10, and the minimum number of samples required to split an internal node was set to 2. for the tuning, we used the gridsearchcv function from the ‘‘sklearn’’ package in python [61], which allows for automated tuning of hyperparameters across specified parameter grids while also imple- menting k-fold cross-validation to ensure robust model evaluation. we set the number of folds to 5, enabling a comprehensive assessment of model performance on different subsets of the training data. global sensitivity analysis (gsa) was used to determine the influ- ence of input variables on the output of a model [62,63]. the sobol sensitivity analysis was performed on the rfc using the salib python library [64]. first, sobol samples were generated for the input variables using the saltelli sampling method [65]. the model was then evaluated on these samples to obtain the output probabilities and compute the first-order (𝑆𝑖) and total-order (𝑆𝑇𝑖) sobol indices. . in this study, the binary output of the rfc (0 or 1) is treated as a continuous probability output, to represent probability likelihood. 3.3. objective function as a quality metric of the trained model we used a confusion matrix [66] and the probability of success, which is defined as the ratio between the sum of false predictions and the size of the sampled ensemble. to ensure robust predictions, an ensemble of full physics-based models (section 2.2) was created, with its size determined through convergence testing: random forest classifier accuracy and output variance converged to a plateau beyond 1500 models, indicating suffi- cient parameter space coverage and justifying the choice to stop at this ensemble size. the rfc is employed to compute the minimal distance between two wells as the minimum probability of thermal interference: p = ∑𝑆 𝑖=1(𝑦𝑖) 𝑆 (12) where 𝑃 is the probability of thermal interference, 𝑦𝑖 is the outcome of a single parameter combination, and 𝑆 is the sample size. to compute the probability values, we considered 100 logarithmically spaced distances between the wells from 50 to 400 m. the use of logarithmically spaced distances between 50 and 400 m is particularly beneficial when precise analysis is required for shorter distances. 4. results in this section we present the proof of the hypothesis of asymptotic model fit (section 4.1), verify the quality of the surrogate model and provide the sensitivity analysis of the thermal interference(section 4.2), and use thermal influence to estimate the minimal distance between the wells (section 4.3), as well as hydraulic effects to estimate the maximal distance between the wells (section 4.4). 4.1. asymptotic fit the asymptotic approximations (eq. (10)–(11)) were applied to the full time series data of hrf and se for each model in the ensemble. for visualizing the fitting results, we used the average values of hrf and se (fig. 5). the mean hrf values exhibit high variability, ranging from 0.5 to 1.0 and reflecting diverse conditions modelled across the parameter space. as follows from eq. (10) is always less or equal to 1. the hrf = 1 refers to the ideal case where all the injected thermal energy is recov- ered during the extraction phase of an ates system. for this to happen, the system has to have no heat loss to the surrounding formation, no thermal dispersion or diffusion, no ambient groundwater flow (fulfilled in this study), no buoyancy-driven heat loss, symmetrical flow paths, applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 6 e. petrova et al. fig. 6. confusion matrix of the random forest classifier prediction on the holdout dataset. the matrix illustrates the classification performance of rfc with the 𝑦-axis shows the true class labels derived from the physics-based model, while the 𝑥-axis indicates the predicted class labels from the rfc.. no mixing of thermal plumes with ambient groundwater. very high hrf values (>0.8) refer to cases when the majority of these conditions have only a small influence, however, since this study considers natural conditions, therefore, hrf values are often lower. at the same time, the se values fall within a narrower range of 0.8 to 1.0, indicating less variability in the thermal interference across the different model runs, limited by the minimum distance between wells considered in the study of 50 m. the mean hrf values computed using the asymptotic approximation closely match the results of the physics-based model, accurately representing the noted before asymptotic behaviour. this accuracy is also observed for the se values. the ‘‘goodness’’ of fit is quantified by an rmse of 0.99 for hrf and 0.97 for se. the parity plot (fig. 5) further illustrates a correlation between the model outputs and their asymptotic approximations, highlighting the effectiveness of the reduced model for capturing the key performance metrics of ates systems. 4.2. surrogate model and sensitivity analysis to validate the trained rfc, 20% of the models and their associated parameter combinations were used as a holdout dataset, which did not participate in the training process. this holdout set was exclusively used to evaluate the classifier’s performance. the potential overfitting was excluded by k-fold cross-validation and multiple random train-test splits, which ensure consistent perfor- mance and generalizability across the parameter space (section 3.2). the validation results, illustrated in the form of a confusion matrix (fig. 6), reflects 97% fit of the surrogate model to the physics based model. 8 out of 352 models (top-right square) tend to overestimate the thermal interference, while no model underestimates the interference. this reflects a high accuracy of the selected approximation procedure and enables the global sensitivity analysis using the surrogate model. the sobol sensitivity analysis [64] was performed on the rfc and technical and natural variables (table 1). first, sobol samples were generated for the input variables using the saltelli sampling method [65]. the model was then evaluated on these samples to obtain the output probabilities and compute the first-order (𝑆𝑖) and total-order (𝑆𝑇𝑖) sobol indices for two scenarios: including and excluding the well distance as a variable. the results are depicted in fig. 7. results of the full dataset (fig. 7, top-left) confirm that the well distance has a first-order influence on thermal interference. the well distance has by far the highest sensitivity index of 0.9. the next influential parameters are geometry and hydraulic boundary condition of the aquifer, that is, thickness and charging flowrate. to better resolve the effects of less dominant parameters, the same metrics computed excluding well distance from analysis, are depicted in fig. 7, right panel. this procedure allows to avoid masking effects, as highly influential parameters can dominate the variance, making it difficult to detect the contributions of others [67]. first order sensitivity index allies with the total order sensitivity index for all parameters. when the well distance is fixed, thickness, and charging flowrate have the highest influence on the thermal interference. this makes sense since both parameters are also relevant for computing the thermal radii. the next parameters in terms of importance are the charging temperature with a total order index of about 0.3 and porosity and permeability, which share a similar result with 0.2. the second-order sensitivity indices (fig. 7, the bottom panels) also show the highest sensitivity for the well distance (bottom-left) along with the aquifer thickness. besides the well distance, thickness together with permeability, as well as charging rate, are important parameters for thermal interference. the sensitivity analysis shows that the well spacing significantly af- fects ates efficiency. this finding implies that even slight adjustments to the spacing can prevent thermal losses, directly improving energy recovery rates and reducing the overall costs associated with energy wastage and over-compensation through external energy sources. 4.3. minimal distance between wells traditional well-spacing methods, such as those based on the ther- mal radius approach (e.g. [9,27,68]), typically rely on simplified ana- lytical solutions to estimate the extent of thermal plumes. while these methods offer computational efficiency and intuitive design criteria, they often overlook the aquifer complexity and the impact of uncer- tainty in subsurface properties. in contrast, the rfc employed in this study captures nonlinear interactions between input parameters and thermal interference outcomes, offering a data-driven alternative that adapts to site-specific conditions. to compare the rfc with the thermal radius method, we ran the rfc prediction over an ensemble of possible parameters (table 1). for each well distance, an ensemble of random parameters within the sample size of 100000 members was generated enabling convergence of a stochastic simulation. this large sample size ensures that the statistical results stabilize, and the output does not vary significantly with additional sampling. obtained probability values at each distance are depicted in fig. 8. the black curve represents the probability of thermal interference between wells, as derived using rfc developed in this study. the likelihood of thermal interference is the highest for distances below approximately 130 m and decreases rapidly as well spacing increases. the orange curve corresponds to corresponding thermal radius approach, enabling a direct comparison of rfc with traditional methods. the fig. 8 illustrates the normalized probability density functions (pdfs) and histograms of different multiples of the thermal radius 𝑅𝑡ℎ. the 𝑅𝑡ℎ values were computed for each model in the ensemble of physics-based models and serve as a common measure to estimate the optimal spacing between wells in ates systems. the studies and regulations suggest a distance of at least 1.5 𝑅𝑡ℎ between the wells to exclude thermal interference. the regulations in netherlands suggest the well distance to be higher than 3 𝑅𝑡ℎ. the orange curve represents the pdf for 𝑅𝑡ℎ, while the blue and green curves show the pdfs for 1.5 and 3 times 𝑅𝑡ℎ, respectively. corresponding histograms are depicted for each pdf, reflecting the distribution of 𝑅𝑡ℎ within the ensemble. based on the 1.5 𝑅𝑡ℎ measure (blue curve), a well distance of more than 230 m should be generally suitable for a well placement of any ates doublet from the selected ensemble. if instead 3 𝑅𝑡ℎ applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 7 e. petrova et al. fig. 7. results of the global sensitivity analysis. top: first-order sobol indices, showing the influence of individual parameters on the output. the left panel (all parameters included) indicates the dominance of well distance, the right panel (well distance excluded) highlights the significance of charging flowrate and thickness. bottom: heatmaps representing parameter interactions based on second-order sobol indices, illustrating the pairwise influence of parameters on system variability. the colour intensity corresponds to the magnitude of interaction effects: lower for purple colours and higher towards yellow colours. fig. 8. comparison of rfc- -based probability of thermal interference and conven- tional thermal radius approach. the black line represents the probability of thermal interference estimated by the rfc, based on a stochastic analysis of physics-based model outcomes. the coloured lines (orange, blue, and green) correspond to thermal radii of 1, 1.5 and 3 𝑅𝑡ℎ computed from the parameters ensemble. the histogram of 1.5 𝑅𝑡ℎ refers to widely used engineering analytical approach for avoiding thermal interference. . are considered plausible, the distance between wells should generally exceed 400 m. at the same time, the results from rfc suggest that lower distances can be favourable for well placement. within the classification task only increasing temperature in the cold well over time was considered as thermal interference while even a small increase in the cold well temperature and following decrease was classified as non-interference. 4.4. maximal distance between the wells productivity indices (pi) characterize the well performance of ates and are limited by the allowed pressure change in the aquifer (eq. (8)). closer well distance produces less pressure change and is thus favourable for hydraulic ates functioning. the histogram of pis of the same ensemble of parameters as in case of rfc is depicted on figure (9, left) with the minimum value is 0.4 m3/h/bar, the maximum 39 m3/h/bar, the mean and median are 6.8 m3/h/bar and 3.9 m3/h/bar correspondingly. with regard to the planned site in adlershof, the pre- liminary estimates of a possible geological conditions suggest possible maximum drawdown of 150 m which corresponds to the minimum pi of 3.4 m3/h/bar for the flowrate of 50 m3/h, and for the minimum pi of 10 m3/h/bar for the flowrate 150 m3/h (fig. 9, left). the highest distance between the wells in adlershof is limited by the maximum feasible drawdown (eq. (8)–(9)) (which corresponds to the minimum pi) (fig. 9). the drawdown and corresponding pi for each well distance were computed for the maximum charging flowrate 150 m3/h after 6-month cycle of ates for the maximum permeability of 1𝑒−12 m2 (fig. 9, right). the drawdown increases progressively with greater well spacing due to the reduced hydraulic influence of the charging well. at the adlershof site, this effect imposes a maximum feasible well spacing of 160 m, constrained by hydraulic and technical considerations. when combined with knowledge of thermal interfer- ence, which establishes a minimum well spacing, it is possible to define a plausible interval for well distances at the adlershof site. this finding is important for the effective planning of ates systems in urban envi- ronments, as it delineates spatial constraints arising from the interplay of hydraulic and thermal properties. for the adlershof site, the optimal well spacing lies between 130 m and 160 m. these results highlight the importance of site-specific analyses to identify feasible well placement intervals, ensuring technical performance and sustainable operation in urban conditions, providing a solid quantitative yet computationally feasible base for ates design and permission. such integrated evalua- tions are essential for balancing efficiency, resource sustainability, and the spatial limitations often present in urban settings. applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 8 e. petrova et al. fig. 9. left: histogram of productivity indices computed from the ensemble of physics-based models, showing the minimum (0.4 m3/h/bar), median (3.9 m3/h/bar), mean (6.8 m3/h/bar), and maximum (39.0 m3/h/bar) values. the green arrow shows pi indices plausible for the adlershof site. right - well distance dependent pi variation computed for the conditions of adlershof site. the shaded green region indicates the plausible range of well distances where pi values satisfy technical and operational constraints. 5. summary and discussion this study presents a comprehensive approach to optimize ates doublet systems, integrating a novel asymptotic approximation for hrf and se with machine learning techniques, and presenting a robust methodology for predicting and mitigating thermal interference under varying geological and technical conditions. we introduced a novel asymptotic approximation for the time development of the hrf and se in an ates doublet system operating with constant six-month-long cycles. this approximation, validated across a broad range of input parameters, enables the reduction of a spatially resolved numerical model of ates without sacrificing accuracy. our detailed model anal- ysis reveals that the time derivative of stored energy is a critical factor in determining optimal well spacing. a key innovation of this work is the development of an rfc trained on an ensemble of spatially resolved numerical models. the trained classifier reflects on the parameter dependencies and allows stochastic prediction of the thermal interference and global sensitivity analy- sis. the comparison with the conventional thermal radius approach (e.g. [9,27,68]) has demonstrated similarity with the rfc approach for a simple model (section 4.3). while the typically applied thermal radius approach (eq. (7)) is an effective and simple analytical estimate of the well distancing, it has several assumptions, such as simplified geology, constant boundary conditions, nonlinear effects, and long-term effects of ates functioning. the physics-based model used to train rfc, on the contrary, is capable of capturing these effects and complex geology, which enables a precise estimate of reliable long-term ates system performance. the outlined here approach provides a framework for the stochastic well distance evaluation while accounting for the model complexity while reducing computational cost by up to 10 times in comparison to monte carlo approaches. these results highlight the effectiveness of the proposed model in capturing the complex thermal interactions in ates systems, suggesting that in urbanized areas it may be profitable to implement detailed simulations in order to decide about optimal well distance. the model considered in this study serves as a demonstration of the applicability of this approach for more complex geological or technical conditions, providing a tool for the uncertainty-acknowledged planning of ates. the modelling framework was applied to the planned ates in berlin adlershof, germany. the result of probabilistic computations for an ates doublet shows that the probability of the thermal interference is 1 for small well distances and is constantly decreasing to 0 for the distance between the wells more than 130 m. the productivity index consideration, which takes into account the hydraulic response of the aquifer, suggests a maximum well distance of 160 m. considered together, hydraulic and thermal characteristics significantly narrow down the possible range of well spacings which has to be considered in planning ates in urban areas. based on such estimates, the optimal well spacing at the adlershof site lies between 130 m and 160 m. while the proposed model offers improvements in fast evaluation of the optimal well placement for ates under parametric uncertainty, several limitations must be acknowledged to contextualize the results and inform future research. the model assumes homogeneous aquifer properties such as per- meability and porosity, which may not be representative of the highly heterogeneous geological environments where ates systems are im- plemented [12]. although the model accounts for a broad range of parameter values, real-world aquifers may exhibit parameter variability beyond the considered ranges. this can lead to discrepancies between model predictions and actual system performance, especially in aquifers with strong stratification or fractures. future studies should incorporate more advanced geological characterization, including field data on heterogeneity and anisotropy, to improve model accuracy. the model neglected the influence of ambient groundwater flow, which may be non-negligible for shallow aquifers [15]. in addition, the model does not account for long-term conduction through the aquitard. by focusing primarily on advection and dispersion within the aquifer, the model may underestimate the total thermal losses, especially over extended storage periods. including these conductive effects in future iterations of the model would provide a more comprehensive under- standing of the long-term energy recovery efficiency. even considering very slow ambient groundwater flow in the adlershof [44,46], the next steps in site characterization therefore include groundwater isotope sampling and corresponding dating, geophysics, and tracer tests to quantify the seepage velocity. the model operates on the assumption of consistent operational cycles, with fixed injection and extraction periods of equal duration (six months). in reality, seasonal variations in energy demand, groundwater flow, and ambient temperatures can significantly impact the perfor- mance of ates systems [26]. these factors may lead to suboptimal system operation if they are not considered during the design phase. a future extension of the model should explore how seasonal and climate- related variability affects ates efficiency, enabling more adaptive and resilient system designs. while the model successfully optimizes technical parameters, it does not account for economic constraints or regulatory requirements that often influence the feasibility of ates projects [21]. factors such as drilling costs, land ownership disputes, and regulatory compliance for well placement in urban areas can significantly alter the viability of a given design. integrating economic models and policy frameworks into the simulation would allow for more realistic feasibility assessments. broader validation against a range of real-world ates systems is necessary to generalize the findings. since the geological and technical settings can vary widely between sites, further field validation in di- verse geological contexts is essential to confirm the model’s robustness and adaptability. while the current model provides a robust framework for optimizing ates system design, particularly in urban settings, future work must address these limitations to improve its real-world applicability. incor- porating more detailed geological data, accounting for long-term vari- ability of the charging temperature and charging/extraction flowrates, applied thermal engineering 278 (2025) 127012 9 e. petrova et al. and expanding validation efforts will enhance the model’s predictive power and practical relevance in varied geological contexts. while the model’s current framework is tailored to specific as- sumptions, future work should explore the integration of additional factors, such as seasonal temperature variations and varying storage periods, to enhance its applicability across diverse geological con- texts. for the porous systems, this implies extending existing ensem- ble parameter variations and including in rfc additional parameters such as heterogeneity degree or statistical parameters, duration of injection/withdrawal, injection temperature, etc. the framework for fractured aquifers is generally similar, but would consider modelling within a discrete fractures network paradigm and consequently consid- ering distributions of fracture aperture, length, and connectivity, which contributes to the system uncertainty. the next steps will focus on validation of the predictive capacity of the developed workflow by com- paring with existing ates systems, extending the modelling approach to account for possible ambient groundwater flow and different degrees of heterogeneity, and release of the tool as an open source for urban ht-ates planners. calibration and validation against the field data will provide means for uncertainty reduction at later stages of ates planning. the present study establishes a computationally efficient and adaptable framework for improved ates system design, contributing to more sustainable energy systems in urban environments. 6. conclusions the key findings and novelty of this study can be described as follows: - the developed analytical asymptotic approximations are capable to precisely mimic heat recovery factor and stored energy decade- scale evolution for a 6-month injection/withdrawal cycles of ates over a broad parameter range. - the developed random forest classifier reflects on the parameter dependencies and allows stochastic prediction of the thermal interfer- ence and global sensitivity analysis under possible natural or technical uncertainties. in comparison to conventionally used thermal radius ap- proach, random forest classifier methodology is based on ensemble of physics-based models which can capture complex geology and dynamic conditions, providing a robust framework for reliable well distance planning in urbanized areas under parametric uncertainty. - until now, only the thermal consideration has been considered to identify the minimum distance. we have shown that the hydraulic consideration also helps to identify the maximum distance between the wells leading to the optimal well placement. based on the thermal and hydraulic considerations for berlin adlershof, the optimal distance between the wells should be between 130 and 160 m. for optimal well design, both constraints have to be considered. - the well spacing recommendation derived from ensemble simula- tions is probabilistically bounded and reflects a risk-informed decision framework based on rfc-derived probabilities. - the global sensitivity analysis reveals that influential parame- ters for thermal interference are (descending order): well placement (by far), aquifer thickness, charging flowrate, charging temperature, permeability, and porosity. the model’s limitations stem from the assumptions made for the spatially resolved model, the considered pa- rameters, and their ranges. however, these constraints do not diminish the model’s value in advancing ates design. the study developed a generic methodology for urban planners and engineers for rapid and accurate feasibility assessments of ates systems under various geological and technical conditions. the ran- dom forest classifier can be integrated into spatial planning tools to rapidly evaluate geothermal well-spacing scenarios under technical and natural uncertainties. the random forest classifier allows for real-time screening of design alternatives, supporting zoning decisions, energy district planning, and changing strategies in urban environments. the approach enhances the efficiency and reliability of ates systems, par- ticularly in densely urbanized areas where optimal well placement is crucial for minimizing energy loss and maximizing recovery efficiency. credit authorship contribution statement elena petrova: writing – review & editing, writing – original draft, visualization, validation, project administration, methodology, inves- tigation, formal analysis, conceptualization. mauro cacace: writing – review & editing, software, methodology, investigation, conceptualiza- tion. stefan kranz: supervision, methodology, investigation, funding acquisition, formal analysis, conceptualization. arno zang: writing – review & editing, supervision, formal analysis, conceptualization. guido blöcher: writing – review & editing, visualization, supervision, software, project administration, methodology, investigation, funding acquisition, data curation, conceptualization. declaration of competing interest the authors declare that they have no known competing finan- cial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. acknowledgements this work was funded the german federal ministry of education and research (bmbf) project ‘‘speichercity’’ grant number 03g0911. we thank two anonymous reviewers for helpful comments which sub- stantially improved the manuscript quality. data availability data will be made available on request. references [1] m. bloemendal, m. jaxa-rozen, t. olsthoorn, methods for planning of ates systems, appl. energy 216 (december 2017) (2018) 534–557, http://dx.doi.org/ 10.1016/j.apenergy.2018.02.068. [2] p. fleuchaus, b. godschalk, i. stober, p. blum, worldwide application of aquifer thermal energy storage – a review, renew. sustain. energy rev. 94 (2018) 861–876, http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2018.06.057. [3] k.s. lee, a review on concepts, applications, and models of aquifer thermal energy storage systems, energies 3 (6) (2010) 1320–1334, http://dx.doi.org/10. 3390/en3061320. [4] p. bayer, g. attard, p. blum, k. menberg, the geothermal potential of cities, renew. sustain. energy rev. 106 (2019) 17–30, http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.rser. 2019.02.019. [5] s. beernink, m. bloemendal, r. kleinlugtenbelt, n. hartog, maximizing the use of aquifer thermal energy storage systems in urban areas: effects on individual system primary energy use and overall ghg emissions, appl. e