Chaudhry2024,2024,Improving the potential of fifth-generation district heating and cooling networks through robust design and operational optimization under future energy market and demand uncertainties,yes,yes,PCE / RSM,high,28,GP / kriging,21,pdf,indices). importantly the ml-model will also replace the hdss in the formulated optimization problem. 3.4.1. polynomial chaos expansion for global sensitivity analysis analyzing a complex engineering system involves understanding the functional mapping relationship between the various,,unassigned,9,Active learning,8,,,,unassigned,9,P2 acceleration,8,,,Problem UQ,high,8,_other,0,pdf,"ear programming is used to determine the optimal net- work design and operational parameters. subsequently, a probability distribution is computed for performance indices such as the levelized cost across diﬀerent uncertainties. however, their approach does not diﬀerentiate which individual input exerts a greater impact on the per- formance index; instead, it provides a probability density function (pdf) considering the combined eﬀect of all uncertain inputs. another limita- tion of the method is the utilization of linear models, which may not ac- energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 4 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. curately capture the nonlinear behavior arising from thermo-hydraulic changes inherent in 5gdhc networks, as discussed in [5]. abokersh etal.[ 34]oﬀers a comprehensive global sensitivity anal- ysis (gsa) utilizing sobol’s indices for a solar-assisted district heating (dh) network. furthermore, they devised an artiﬁcial neural network (ann)-based meta-model to depict the entire system, along with the variance-based model. however, their approach overlooks the consider- ation of bi-directional ﬂows, which have a signiﬁcant impact, especially in the case of 5gdhc networks operational design and optimization. this work addresses current research gaps by applying machine learn- ing to computationally expensive high-ﬁdelity models of network sim- ulations for heat and hydraulics. a preliminary sensitivity analysis is conducted to identify design variables and objectives, facilitating feasi- ble and computationally eﬃcient solutions for multi-objective problems, as demonstrated in [3,38,39]. thus, this study not only addresses gaps in previous approaches but also provides a systematic method to identify potential 5gdhc cases. the generic thermodynamic framework from the authors’ previous work [37]i s extended to align with a nonlinear model-based robust design approach to enhance the potential applicabil- ity of business cases by improving the components of ﬁnancial metrics. the novel contributions of this paper are summarized as: • generation of uncertain energy market and demand scenarios through probabilistic descriptions • framework to assess thermodynamic and economic performance under uncertainty • identiﬁcation of promising business cases through risk analysis of ﬁnancial metrics for business case • machine learning based on kpi responses obtained from high- ﬁdelity models •s o l v i n g multi-objective robust design optimization (mo-rdo) problem to ﬁnd pareto-optimum solutions for network designs • evaluating the risk on ﬁnancial metrics with the solved mo-rdo 1.4. paper structure the rest of the article is organized as follows: in section2, we ﬁrst explain the general scope of the work and the importance of robust de- sign analysis and optimization under the inﬂuence of uncertain markets, which hinder the transition towards 5gdhc networks. in section 3, a general thermodynamic framework for evaluating potential 5gdhc network business cases, following their preliminary assessment using gis-based methods. the section also explains how network kpis are de- ﬁned to compute ﬁnancial metrics in comparison to present-day design technologies. additionally, it addresses uncertainties from energy mar- kets and their impact on ﬁnancial metrics, along with methods for risk evaluation. furthermore, we develop machine learning algorithms to create a surrogate model that can initially identify the inﬂuential de- sign variables and objectives prior to optimization. this model is then utilized in the robust optimization problem to ﬁnd optimal trade-oﬀs between environmental and economic objectives. in section 4, ﬁrst, a small virtual network is considered to present year-based kpis correla- tion over ﬁnancial metrics, followed by a small business case of 5gdhc is analyzed using the proposed methodology. in section 5, the simula- tion results are displayed, indicating which input uncertainties should be considered from a network operational and design perspective, and the optimal trade-oﬀs determined. we discuss the outcomes and their applicability to provide insights for investors. section6presents the con- clusions of this study by highlighting the achievements yielded by the proposed methodology. 2. problem statement the transition to 5gdhc networks faces limitations due to uncertain- ties in present-day energy markets, constraining investors from making decisions even when a particular site appears attractive. these uncer- tainties encompass factors such as equipment costs, energy prices, and co2 taxes. moreover, in many 5gdhc networks, energy is exchanged among network participants, amplifying the impact of uncertainties in participant demands and further restricting the transition. impor- tantly, a preliminary analysis using gis-based approaches, as discussed in [27,40–42], is insuﬃcient to address the uncertainty eﬀects related to unpredictable energy market behaviorand ﬂuctuating energy demands. in this work, a systematic methodology is developed, beginning with an analysis of potential locations using simple linear and randomly gen- erated scenarios to assess the techno-economic performance over the project’s life, focusing on ﬁnancial metrics to assess the proﬁtability and return on investment. locations that demonstrate stable kpis are then utilized for robust design optimization, aiming to identify environ- mentally or economically eﬃcient solutions. these optimized solutions are intended to improve overall ﬁnancial metrics while accounting for uncertainties that may hinder the transition process. this will assist in- vestors in making informed decisions based on ﬁnancial analysis. 3. methodology this section presents the methods and framework to evaluate and optimize the business case for 5gdhc networks in business parks in flanders, belgium. it considers uncertain energy market and demand scenarios. the framework, shown in fig.1, consists of four steps. step 1 explains the thermodynamic modelingframework section3.1, used to analyze locations identiﬁed through a gis-based approach. it also details how prosumers’ energy demands are modeledand how net- work equipment is selected. the operational costs of the equipment are also discussed. in section3.2and section3.3, the characterization of kpi metrics and uncertain input scenarios are explained, respectively. step 2 involves risk analysis discussed in section 3.3.1, assessing the project’s net present value against uncertain energy market scenar- ios. based on key performance indicator (kpi) responses, a preliminary techno-economic assessment is conducted to rate each location’s future eﬀectiveness in energy demand sharing. in step 3, the machine learning approach detailed in section 3.4 is applied to analyze over 3000 uncertain input scenarios, identifying the key factors that inﬂuence the kpis of the 5gdhc network. this sim- pliﬁes the multi-objective optimization process in step 4, discussed in section 3.5, where trade-oﬀs between environmentally and economi- cally optimal solutions are examined using a computationally eﬃcient model based on the pc-kriging approach. 3.1. 5gdhc network modeling after conducting the preliminary analysis using a gis-based ap- proach in step 1, the most promising locations are shortlisted. in a gis-based approach, various types of data are uploaded onto platforms like qgis to evaluate the potential of speciﬁc locations. this data may include historical information about how people or systems have inter- acted, current network layouts of utilities like gas and electricity, basic information about the landscape, and weather data. the shortlisted sites are then further analyzed in step 2 using the python-based heat distri- bution system simulator (hdss) for detailed design. 3.1.1. assumption and approach for 5gdhc analysis the hdss modelinglayout for representing the 5gdhc network de- scription is illustrated in step 2 on the right side of fig. 2. the hdss considers the following assumptions to perform network simulations: •t h e r m a l losses through the main network pipes are negligible due to the proximity of network temperatures to ambient levels. •t h e hydraulic calculations are performed by assuming a ﬁxed fric- tion factor for pipe hydraulic losses. energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 5 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 1. aframeworkfor5gdhcnetworksrobustdesignoptimization. fig. 2. schematiclayoutforassessingpotential5gdhclocationsusingacombinedgisandpython-basedapproach. •t h e cost of network connection and maintenance are not consid- ered. •t h e temperature of hot and cold pipes is ﬁxed for 1y of simulation. •t h e eﬃciency of natural gas (ng) boiler (𝜂boiler) is ﬁxed to 90%. the hdss exchanges information with the gis database using a csv exchange format, listed in table1. in this format, table columns 𝑥and 𝑦represent the position of a prosumer node in meters within the earth’s coordinate system; column sa represents the building surface area, and column 𝑄represent the energy demand over the year. another exchange format given in table2, provides details about the connection between table 1 data exchange format from the gis to the hdss, listing the position, surface area, consumption, and waste heat production. id name 𝑄[mwh] sa[m2] 𝑥[m] 𝑦[m] n01 dat