n table3to simulate and compute the key performance indica- tors (kpis) for both the prosumer and network operators. 3.1.2. building demands modeling in this section, the methods for estimating the energy demands of participants in the 5gdhc network across diﬀerent seasons are dis- cussed. the methodology from the authors’ previous work [43]w a s applied to estimate prosumer energy demands, accounting for seasonal variations. outdoor temperature data was sourced from the royal me- teorological institute in belgium. hachez et al. [43]u s e s the energy data related to electric and gas consumption (non-hvac) of the prosumer to train the building energy model. this dataset includes the last ﬁve years’ consumption from 2018 to 2022 for prosumer-type buildings such as indoor skiing facilities, su- permarkets, and data centres. the methodology requires building details such as type of building, annual consumption 𝑄building, buildings’ energy performance certiﬁcates (epc), surface area (sa), consumption factors for weekends and weekdays over a year and outdoor temperature proﬁle to separates hvac energy data related to outdoor weather conditions from the non-hvac-related energy data using stochastic approach as discussed in [43]. the annual consumption for a given epc level and sa can be computed using 𝑄buildings =epc ⋅sa. (1) energy proﬁles are generated at an hourly resolution for the period span- ning from yyyy:mm:dd hr:min:secto yyyy:mm:dd hr:min:sec, corresponding to the total annual consumption (𝑄buildings). the detailed temporal breakdown provides insights into the variation of energy de- mand across the entire year as discussed in [44–46]. interested readers are referred to [43]for more information on prosumer energy demand proﬁle generation. 3.1.3. sizing of network equipment and associated costs it is important to mention that in hdss, each node is referred to as a decentralized prosumer node and has been sized according to the peak loads. the decentralized prosumer node or substation consists of a bhp that provides hydraulic isolation between primary and secondary hy- draulic circuits [47]. in decentralized prosumer node operations, a bhp is utilized, connected to both the hot and cold network, as depicted in fig. 3. additionally, the centralized assets, referred to as the balanc- ing unit and network pipes in hdss, are sized based on the variance in energy demands among participating nodes as shown in fig. 3 on the right. this concept is also applied in [7], where supplementary gener- ation units are added when borehole energy is insuﬃcient to meet all demands. for the sake of simplicity, we assumed that the balancing unit is equipped with a chiller, a natural gas (ng) boiler, and a lossless thermal energy storage. in the context of managing balancing units and network operations, we assume that the assets work at low temperatures and that the maximum part of the network imbalance should be covered by seasonal storage. in this study, two kinds of seasonal storage are considered; one is called borehole thermal energy storage (btes) and the other is called aquifer (ates) [48]. the volume of the storage can be calculated as [49]: 𝑉storage = 𝑄 𝜌⋅cp ⋅δ𝑇, (2) where δ𝑇is the temperature diﬀerence of working ﬂuid in the supply network (𝑇h) and cold network (𝑇c). as observed from eq.(2), temper- atures in hot and cold pipes close to ambient will require a larger tes volume to cover the mismatch in heating and cooling demand over a season. the speciﬁc costs for two types of storage are given in [49]as: 𝐶specific,borehole =𝑐btes ⋅𝑉−0.7 storage, (3) 𝐶specific,acquifer =𝑐ates ⋅𝑉−0.1 storage. (4) the kpis related to investment cost are then calculated using capexstorageborehole =𝐶specific,borehole ⋅𝑉storage, (5) capexstorageacquifer =𝐶specific,acquifer ⋅𝑉storage. (6) the cost of network pipe [eur∕m] varies with diameters 𝐷𝑖and trench cost as discussed in [3]. for the network connections, the considered cost includes the investment cost for the pipe, which also encompasses the excavation or trench cost. this corresponds to the expenses associ- ated with digging and preparing a trench for various purposes, such as laying pipes, cables, or other utilities and can be calculated as: capexpipe,𝐼=2.16 ⋅𝐷𝑖+trenchprice+68 . (7) those investment costs are summed on every pipe and constitute the investment cost of pipes at the network level as capexc, and the asso- ciated cost of the pumping power to maintain energy ﬂows through the pipes is summed as opexc, as shown in fig.3. it is important to note that, for comparing savings or revenue with the network design scenario, current technologies like ng boil- energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 7 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 3. cost addition scheme for 5gdhc network node scenario compared to standalone design technologies: on the left, a triangle representing standalone tech- nologies; on the right, cost evolution from individual assets to total 5gdhc network costs. ers, gshps, and ashps are considered, as discussed in [32]. the sizes and associated investment costs of the standalone design technologies, depicted on the left side of fig.3, for meeting heating and cooling de- mands using ashps, gshps, and boilers, can be calculated as: capextec =𝑐t ⋅max( ̇𝑄)+ ℎtec tec∈[bhp,ashp,gshp,ng], (8) where ℎtec =[25051, 9817, 25051, 0]is the vector of unitless regression factors for estimating capacity factors of considered technologies. in the case of a gshp, a small-scale borehole comprised of a horizon- tal tube-based ground heat exchanger to satisfy its heating and cooling demands over the seasons is also considered ([50,51]). in [51], a method is constructed to estimate the ground heat exchanger sizing and ther- mal properties. the desired lengths of the ground-based heat exchangers concerning the heating and cooling demands of the nodes are given as discussed in [51] 𝐿h = ̇𝑄h,max(𝐾p +𝐾s𝐹h) 𝑇oout −𝑇c ⋅scoph −1 scoph , (9) 𝐿c = ̇𝑄c,max(𝐾p +𝐾