section 3.1.3, based on the energy demands proﬁle of the pro- sumer, hdss selects the size of the equipment and calculates the invest- ment costs of centralized and decentralized equipment (capexc and capexd) using eqs.(5)to (8). operational cost and associated co2 tax of assets are calculated in section3.1.4using eqs.(22)and (24). it can be seen from fig.3that all the network upfront investment costs and op- erational expenditures, including co2 tax, are summed using eqs. (32) and (33), respectively. capexo =capexc +capexd, (32) opexo =opexc +opexd. (33) this leads to annualized overall cost (ac) as ac=capex o +opexo, (34) similarly, the emissions associated with the centralised and decen- tralised equipment and co2 tax can be calculated as te =emc +emd, (35) where hdss calculates centralized emissions emc and decentralized emissions emd using eqs.(23)and (29). as discussed above, some of the kpis indicating the economic per- formance of the 5gdhc networks are related to the kpis of present-day design technologies such as boilers, air source heat pumps (ashp), and energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 9 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 4. characterizationofkpi-metricstoevaluatepotentialbusinesscase. ground source heat pumps (gshp). this study compares the 5gdhc net- work scenario against ashp + boiler technology scenario to compare ﬁnancial performance indicators. when comparing boilers and chillers with the 5gdhc network, we assume that building substations work independently and aren’t connected to the network. the assumption holds for other design technology scenarios as well. the main diﬀer- ence would be that hdss will select a relevant set of equations for a particular technology scenario described in sections3.1.3and 3.1.4to compute capexr and opexr. the overall annualized cost with refer- ence technology is then calculated as acr =capexr +opexr, (36) and ter can be calculated by summing all emissions associated with reference design technologies (∑ emr). whereas hdss calculate emr using eqs.(23)and (29), respectively. the net operational cost savings (𝜒) calculated in eq. (37), derived from network operations, is compared with the operational expenditures (opexr) eq.(28)associated with a boiler’s reference design for heating and considering ashp operational cost for cooling using eq.(22). 𝜒=opexr −opexo, (37) cr= ac acr , (38) spbp= capexo −capexr 𝜒 . (39) when ∑ (opexo) < opexr, it indicates positive net savings (𝜒> 0e u r ∕ y). both capexo and 𝜒directly inﬂuence the component of ﬁnancial metrics, such as net present value (npv), as discussed in [47, 18]. the cost ratio (cr) is represented by the ratio of acto acr. a cr ≤100 %is considered a favourable indicator for a 5gdhc network. in addition to cr, another economic-kpi savings-to-capex ( 𝜒 capexo ) ratio is considered. a higher savings-to-capex ratio enhances ﬁnancial kpis and improves the project’s feasibility over its lifetime. the simple payback period (spbp), as given in eq. (39), depends on the ratio between the capex and 𝜒. since spbp and 𝜒are inversely related, a higher 𝜒leads to a shorter spbp for a given capex. 3.2.2. financial kpis reﬂecting on the primary objective of the proposed framework, it aims to optimize ﬁnancial kpis for environmentally or economically eﬃ- cient solutions. key indicators like net present value (npv), internal rate of return (irr), and discounted payback period (dpbp) help investors assess a site’s feasibility by comparing initial investment with cash ﬂows and operational cost savings. npv serves as a ﬁnancial kpi for locations, allowing the estimation of costs and beneﬁts in cash-equivalent terms. interesting business cases require npv higher values to enhance project desirability. the npv for the 5gdhc case is calculated as ([56,57]): npv= 𝑁∑ 𝑛=0 cf𝑛 (1+ 𝑟)𝑛, (40) where 𝑟represents the discounted rate on investments, cf𝑛represents net cash ﬂows over 𝑁years, cf0 =c a p e xo and cf1,⋯ ,𝑛correspond to 𝜒1, ⋯ , 𝜒𝑛against certain increase in market inﬂation ([47]). in this study, market inﬂation is considered for the base case scenario reported in powerpulse, see for example table6. the table6indicates that elec- tricity and gas prices vary ≈1 %per year (as inﬂation) over the project life of 40y. internal rate of return (irr [%]) measures the investment proﬁtabil- ity and higher irr implies more desirable investments ([56]). irr is the value of discount rate which gives npv=0 as : 𝑁∑ 𝑛=0 cf𝑛 (1+irr )𝑛=0. (41) dpbp measures the discounted payback period in years for diﬀerent locations. in simple terms, it indicates the period at which npv>0, as: dpbp=min{ 𝑁∣ 𝑁∑ 𝑛=0 cf𝑛 (1+ 𝑟nom)𝑛≥0}, (42) dpbp exhibits greater robustness compared to a simple payback period, as it includes a discount rate on cash ﬂows. calise etal.[ 24]highlighted the long dpbp for 5gdhc networks. therefore, in this study, a dpbp shorter than the equipment replacement time, such as under 20y, is considered a more suitable indicator. to evaluate all the aforementioned ﬁnancial metric components, the operation and maintenance (o&m) cost is treated as a ﬁxed replacement cost (𝑐rep) on the initial investment ([3]). some corporation tax (𝑐tax) has also been included in the net cash ﬂows. the 𝑐rep will apply after some years of the project; saini etal.[ 32]considered 15y. however, in this study, we apply this cost after half of the project’s lifespan. moreover, 𝑐tax is applied from the beginning of the project and will continue until the end of the project’s life. the parameters used to calculate lifecycle cost economics are listed in table7. 3.3. characterizing uncertainties and scenarios for risk analysis the uncertainties in energy prices, co2 tax, equipment costs, and energy demands of the buildings are three major types of uncertain- energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 10 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. table 6 electricity and gas price variations for three diﬀerent categories, opted from powerp