ly eﬃcient model can be employed by considering the design variables and objectives that are aﬀected by input uncertainties. 3.4.2. pce-kriging approach the machine-learning block is further improved to employ a pc- kriging-based modelingapproach on the modelingof inﬂuencing inputs and for selected kpis as shown in fig.1. the kriging model consists of two parts such as trend and stochastic distributions [59]. the trend part reproduces the system’s overall response, while the stochastic gaussian part accounts for the residual error between the trend part and the orig- inal function. thus, to develop an accurate meta-model of the system using the kriging method both trend part and stochastic components need to be tuned. in pce-kriging approach, the trend part is established by using pce orthonormal as discussed in schöbi etal.[ 61]  pce−kriging=𝛽𝑇𝑓(𝜉)+ 𝜎2𝑍(𝜉,𝜔), (52) where 𝛽𝑇𝑓(𝜉) = ∑𝑃 𝑖=1𝛽𝑖𝑧𝑖(𝜉)is the trending function, 𝛽=(𝛽1, 𝛽2, ⋯ , 𝛽𝑝) represents the coeﬃcient vector. the trend function (𝑓(𝜉)) can be a con- stant, linear and quadratic ([59]). however, in pce-kriging, the trend function is built on pce orthonormal basis vector eq.(49). 𝑍(𝜉, 𝜔)rep- resents a stationary gaussian process with zero mean and unit variance and 𝜎2 is the variance of gaussian process [59]. 3.4.3. assessing model convergence to evaluate the eﬀectiveness of the developed model a metric such as adjusted r-squared [34]is considered as 𝑅2 =1− ∑𝑛 𝑖=1(𝑦model,i −𝑦data,i)2 ∑𝑛 𝑖=1(𝑦model,i −̄𝑦data,i)2. (53) 3.5. multi-objective robust design optimization of 5gdhc networks the proposed framework performs ml-based robust optimization for 5gdhc networks. pce-kriging developed in section3.4.2is utilized as a ml model for the optimization. the overall objective of the formulated optimization problem is to optimize the components of ﬁnancial metrics such as capexo, 𝜒and te. heat demands, energy prices, installation costs, and co2 tax act as input uncertainties (𝜔𝑖), as shown in fig.7. a systematic approach involves seeking a robust solution that per- forms well under the inﬂuence of uncertainties. let 𝜔1, 𝜔2, ⋯ , 𝜔m be the probability distributions of the present uncertainties in the network. the robust nonlinear optimization problem is then can be deﬁned as fig. 7.overview of pc-kriging-based 5gdhc network optimization framework. min ∀̇𝑄demand ̄𝐽(𝜔𝑖)= ∑ (𝛼⋅capexo +(1−𝛼) ⋅te), (54a) s.t. 𝐷∈[𝐷min ,𝐷max]t, (54b)  ∈[δ𝑝min , δ𝑝max]t, (54c) γ∈[𝑇c,min ,𝑇c,max]t. (54d) the optimization routine selects the decision variable setting values by satisfying operational constraints as: eqs.(54b)and (54d)as: 80 ≤𝐷≤250mm, 11 ≤𝑇h ≤35 ◦c, 1 ≤𝑇c ≤10 ◦c, 50 ≤≤ 1000pa∕m. (55) in eq.(54a), the initial constraint on 𝐷is set to choose pipe diameters that are readily available in the dh market. the constraints on 𝑇h and 𝑇c delineate the limits for supply and return temperatures, aimed at op- erating the network in proximity to ambient and ground temperatures. the constraint on δ𝑝ensures adequate mass ﬂows and helps minimize peak power requirements for the pumps. 4. case studies diﬀerent locations from the flanders region can be selected to ap- ply a proposed framework for robust design analysis and optimization. but before introducing the particular case study, a small scale virtual network is considered to demonstrate the yearly-based kpis and their impact on ﬁnancial metrics. 4.1. small virtual network in fig.8is shown that consists of tes, virtual building and a variable node. the variable node is iterated with diﬀerent types of buildings that have cooling and heating demands. for the virtual building node, an oﬃce with heating only and an oﬃce with both heating and cooling (prosumer type) are considered; see, for example, table 10. the time series of energy demands for each node is generated using [43]. the yearly-based kpis mentioned in table5are evaluated using fig.5con- sidering the project life of 40 yand discount rate (𝑟 =4 %). energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 13 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 8. small virtual network with virtual oﬃce building node and a variable node of diﬀerent type of prosumers. table 10 list of virtual scenarios with a small network of virtual building node and a variable node. scenario1 scenario2 variablenode buildingnode variablenode buildingnode datacenter datacenter coolingoﬃce coolingoﬃce wastewater wastewater shop shop coﬀeefactory coﬀeefactory icecreamfactory icecreamfactory pastafactory pastafactory bnice bnice colac oﬃce colac coolingoﬃce in fig. 9, the correlation between the techno-economic and envi- ronmental kpis listed in table5is shown for two distinct scenarios, as outlined in table 10. all kpis are normalized to show the percentage improvements on a % scale. it is evident from fig.9that approximately 20% increase in both synergy and reverse ﬂow duration (rv) leads to enhanced economic kpis when considering a cooling-type building node iterated with a variable node. the spider web signiﬁcantly expands in the case of a coﬀee factory paired with a cooling oﬃce, resulting in im- provements across all kpis. on the ﬁnancial assessment scale, npv is evaluated for both sce- narios as shown in fig. 10a. it can be seen from fig. 10a that npv increases with a cooling oﬃce that acts as a prosumer in the network due to approximately 20% improvements in yearly-based kpis depicted in fig.9. moreover, the saving-to-capex ratio also increases by employ- ing a prosumer-cooling oﬃce in the network compared to the simple oﬃce building, as shown in fig.10b. hence, all yearly-based kpis inﬂu- ence the npv calculated over a project’s life. 4.2. telenet 5gdhc network for this study, a small location of telenet from the flander region is considered shown in fig.11. this location consists of ﬁve buildings or nodes (table11), four of which are consumer types and one is a producer type, along with a centralized btes. the time-series of energy demands for each node is generated using [43], and the uncertain demand variation is injected using eq.(1)with a speciﬁc surface area of the building sa and uncertain thermodynamic properties epc = 0.1 to 0.4m w h ∕ m2y. importantly producer node data center is assumed to have ﬁxed energy supply over the season, thus no variations on it are considered, the demands of each node are listed in table12. each node is connected via pipe section with a speciﬁc diameter (𝐷). as discussed in section3.3, hdss reads the layout plan of each node and section is exchanged with commercial tool zulu thermowhich can also provide an initial guess of pipe sizes as listed in table 9 against spe- ciﬁc energy ﬂows within the network. however, it does not estimate sizes under the inﬂuence of uncertain energy demands. thus, based on available size ranges mentioned in [5], the uniform and bounded distri- bution of uncertainty in pipe sizes are considered which may result in uncertainties in associated pipe costs (eq.(7)). as discussed in [8,26], the network’s supply and return tempera- tures, as well as the heat pump’s supply curves (𝑆h, 𝑆c) to provide heating at desired temperature levels to prosumer premises, may vary due to diﬀerences in complementary load proﬁles. these variations are inﬂuenced by uncertainties in the overall energy demands, as outlined in table 12. therefore, uncertainty in the operational network tem- peratures based on the 5gdhc network studies [4,8,9]n e a r ambient temperatures is considered and listed in table9. details of each node and diameter are listed in tables9and 12. the energy exchange of nodes with btes over the season causes ﬂow reversal in the network and classiﬁes this conﬁguration as a prosumer network [8]. 5. results and discussions in this study, the results are divided into two main parts sections5.1 and 5.2, followed by a discussion section5.3. in section5.1, a detailed analysis of kpi responses generated under multiple uncertain scenarios using a uniform distribution to develop the ml model is performed. in section5.2, the developed ml model is used to identify inﬂuential un- certain design variables and objectives using the pce approach. the pce is further improved by the pce-kriging modeling approach to enhance the performance of the ml model by reducing the root mean squared er- ror (rmse) between the computational model hdss and pck. the ml model is then employed in mo-rdo section 5.2.3 to ﬁnd the optimal trade-oﬀs between environmentally optimal and economically optimal solutions to support the decision-making process for investors under the inﬂuence of uncertainties. the summary of the framework steps and as- sociated outcomes are listed in table14and the parameters related to energy markets are listed in tables7and 8. in the last section of the discussion section5.3, ﬁrst, we explained the signiﬁcance of sobol’s at the network level, followed by comparing the mo-rdo solutions with non-optimal solutions, and their implications on ﬁnancial metrics are analyzed. 5.1. risk analysis via kpi responses the results of risk analysis of ﬁnancial assessment components such as npv, irr and dpbp for dc-telenet against random variations are shown in fig.12. these components are calculated against kpi-response of capexo and 𝜒using eqs. (40)t o (42). whereas each of the re- sponse of kpi is calculated against uncertain random distribution of inputs within their uniform bounds as listed in tables 8, 9 and 12. it can be seen from fig.12that when the discount rate is varied between r=0 to 10%, the npv remains greater than zero, with an irr of 11% and a dpbp of 11y, even when all inputs are varied randomly. to iden- tify the risk factors in the analysis, quantiles of npv-pdf eq. (43)a n d cdf eq.(44)in the intervals of 10%, 50% and 90% are estimated, re- spectively. from fig. 13, it can be seen that the pdf varies between the 10% and 50% quantiles, indicating a high variance of npv against uncertain inputs. this shows that the variance of input uncertainties is propagated to the output npv. additionally, while the pdf in fig.13provides in- sights into npv outcome trends, it does not pinpoint the speciﬁc input variables inﬂuencing the npv. therefore, sobol’s analysis has been per- formed using pce to identify the main uncertain inputs. the summary of the risk analysis before optimization is listed in table15. energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 14 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 9. kpis comparison between two types of buildings with a variable source node. (for interpretation of the colors in the ﬁgure(s), the reader is referred to the web version of this article.) fig. 10. npvandsavings-to-capexareshownagainstvirtualnetworkscenarioslistedintable 10. table 11 list of location from flanders region of belgium for the robust design analysis. location mainproducer networklength[ m] no.ofbuildings 𝑄h [gwh] 𝑄c [gwh] synergy[%] telenet datacenter 145 5 0.6 0.6 70 table 12 node types and energy demand variations of nodes present in dc-telenet. node buildings sa[ m2]q [ mwh∕y]p r e s e n t t y p e n01 datacenter1 237 608 sc n02 oﬃce2 596 60to 238 sh n03 oﬃce6 5021 502to 2008 sh n04 oﬃce8 375 37to 150 sh n05 oﬃce10 743 74to 297 sh 5.2. machine learning results 5.2.1. sobol’s indices for dc-telenet the proposed methodology relies signiﬁcantly on employing pce to calculate sobol’s indices (𝑖). as previously stated, 𝑖plays a pivotal role in simplifying the complex multi-objective robust design opti- mization (mo-rdo) by identifying appropriate decision variables, as illustrated in fig.1. the uncertain inputs considered of energy markets are based on table 8, energy demand variations table 12 and opera- table 13 uncertainties in the network operational and design parameters. description symbol initial ﬁnal unit source 𝐷0 20 250 mm 𝐷1 60 250 mm pipediameter 𝐷2 40 20 mm markettrend,[ 5] 𝐷3 20 250 mm 𝐷4 20 250 mm 𝐷5 40 250 mm hottemperature 𝑇ℎ 11 35 ◦c [62,9] coldtemperature 𝑇𝑐 11 0 ◦c [62,9] slopehotline 𝑆h 0.8 2.0 ◦c∕◦c assumed slope