tional- related inputs such as unit prices (𝑐gas, 𝑐el), emission factor (𝜖el), and co2 tax, are notable. regarding 𝜒, 𝑐gas has a higher impact than 𝑐el even though the decentralized prosumer nodes are hydraulically cou- pled with the heat pumps. this is because 𝜒is computed against boiler design technology as given in eq. (37). thus, variations in 𝑐gas signif- icantly inﬂuence 𝜒, and a low 𝑐gas can even undermine the potential business case. moreover, due to the large surface area of oﬃce 6 as in- dicated in table12the uncertain demand variations of oﬃce 6 which is signiﬁcantly aﬀect all kpis. the impact of uncertain input parameters related to energy mar- ket scenarios and building energy demands cannot be controlled, as discussed in [3]. for instance, energy demand ﬂuctuations are inﬂu- enced by evolving building characteristics. as a result, uncertain energy demands can lead to diverse network conﬁgurations and sizes [15]. therefore, in this study, the adjacent diameter of the section pipe (𝐷2) is obtained to make the network robust against uncertain demand varia- tions of oﬃce 6. in table16, a summary of shortlisted uncertain inputs that interact with the kpis is listed. as discussed above, not all uncer- tain inputs can be optimized. therefore, the inputs that have 𝑖>0and can also act as design variables for the optimization problem eq.(54)to improve the kpis are identiﬁed in table16. this will lead to reducing the design variables for the case of telenet from 24 to only 3 (𝑇h,𝑇c and 𝐷2). energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 16 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. table 16 key input parameters with high sobol indices and their importance in multi- objective robust design optimization (mo-rdo). no. uncertaininput 𝑖>0forkpi mo-rdo remarks 1 𝑇h and 𝑇c capexo yes controlvariable 1o ﬃ c e 6 𝜒,capexo no frommarket 2 𝑐el 𝜒,te no frommarket 3 𝑐gas 𝜒,te no frommarket 4 𝜖gas 𝜒,te no frommarket 5 𝑐co2 𝜒,te no frommarket 6 𝐷2 capexo,𝜒andte yes oﬃce6demandvariation fig. 12.npv versus real discount rate (𝑟), npvs for every location is calculated with uniform random distribution of energy prices. fig. 13. npvversuspdfandcdf. 5.2.2. pck model performance as discussed earlier, the hdss will be replaced by an ml-based model in the optimization step to improve the computational eﬃciency. we considered the polynomial choas-kriging (pck) approach as dis- cussed in section 3.4.2. the model’s performance is assessed using eq. (53). analysis depicted in fig.15reveals that most errors between the model output (𝑦model) and the actual data (𝑦data) lie along the linear line, indicating a signiﬁcant level of accuracy of the ml-based model as discussed in [34]. 5.2.3. mo-rdo after the shortlisting of location and ﬁnding the decision variables and objectives via sobol’s the last step is to optimize the kpis against uncertain inputs. the optimization problem formulated in eq. (54)i s solved over the entire design space as depicted in table 9, along with the inﬂuence of all uncertain inputs together. fig. 14. sobol’ indices (𝑖) for three kpis are shown, 𝑖≥0 means the factor is inﬂuencing the output, 𝑖≈0 means the factor has no inﬂuence. energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 17 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 15. adjustedr-squaredplotshowingtheperformanceofhdss( 𝑦model)andml-model( 𝑦data)for capexo andte. fig. 16. diﬀerentdesignchoicesfor capexo andte. the optimized solution in the formulated multi-objective problem provides a set of non-dominated designs (also called pareto frontier points) of the 5gdhc network. these designs represent optimal trade- oﬀ solutions between the environmentally eﬃcient and economically eﬃcient objectives that satisfy the technical constraints eq. (54). it is important to mention that both qualitative and quantitative criteria are being used to evaluate design and objectives. first, the qualitative analysis is presented here in this section, and quantitative analysis is performed in section5with more insights and summarized in table18. qualitative analysis of mo-rdo: in this work, we have used matlab- fmincon nonlinear solver as it can be integrated with both equation-based model and data-driven models. the optimization eq.(54)yields a pareto front between environmentally optimal and eco- nomically optimal solutions as shown in fig. 16. the environmentally optimal solution corresponds to the design that has more network up- front cost but lower co2 emissions, whereas the economically optimal solution corresponds to lower network upfront investment cost but may result in higher co2 emissions. the optimal trade-oﬀ point is to guide investors in balancing between a more expensive but lower-emission solution and a cheaper option that may result in higher co2 emissions. the evolution of the design variables is shown in fig. 17 that lead to achieving objectives at distinct optimal points shown in fig.16. 𝐷2 and δ𝑇are independent variables, as the network kpis evolution dif- fers with their variance as shown in fig.17a and 17b. in fig.17a, the evolution of 𝐷2 is shown against two set objectives of capexo and te. it can be seen from fig.17a that the optimization routine ﬁnds the high value of 𝐷2 to get the environmentally optimal solution that results in a high value of capexo and results in reduced emissions of 35tonco2∕y. the other solution of economically eﬃcient design can be obtained by selecting 𝐷2 =120 mm. the exact trade-oﬀ can be seen where the evo- lution of two objectives intersects around 𝐷2 = 137 mm. in fig. 17b, the evolution of the objective is shown against the diﬀerence of supply and return temperatures (δ𝑇). the increase in δ𝑇will reduce the main cost component of capexo, such as storage cost, see for example in eq. (2). however, increasing δ𝑇will reduce the cop of decentralized heat pumps installed at prosumer premises, leading to higher electricity consumption and ultimately an increase in co2 emissions. in table17, a summary of the diﬀerent mo-rdo designs is compared to the average mean of non-optimized solution. 5.3. discussion according to sobol’s indices, under uncertain energy markets, net- work temperatures are more important to consider when transitioning to 5gdhc networks. as such, a linear sensitivity analysis is performed using 𝑇c =0, 5, 10, … , 35 ◦c and 𝑇h =5 to35 ◦c for network tempera- tures to observe eﬀects on kpis, as shown in fig.18. the ac of the form of eq.(34)and its decomposition are illustrated in fig.18a and fig.18b. results show a nonlinear trend when 𝑇h and 𝑇c are varied. as shown in fig.18b the main component of capexo, such as capex storage, starts to decrease as δ𝑇increases between 𝑇h and 𝑇c. variations in 𝑇h and 𝑇c have no signiﬁcant impact on capex decentralized and capex pipes. a signiﬁcant change occurs in opexd and co2 cost decentralized as δ𝑇 increases. the capex pipe, however, accounts for approximately 10% of overall annualized expenses. although we have considered 𝐷2 due to high sobol’s of oﬃce 6 demand variations, the main eﬀect of diﬀer- ent network solutions in terms of capexo and te is determined by the best selection of network temperatures as indicated in fig.17b. 𝜒and te show opposite trends against 𝑇hand 𝑇cvariations. the fig.18d illus- trates that increasing δ𝑇will increase emissions, causing a rise in co2 emission costs and a reduction in 𝜒as shown in fig. 18c. thus, a low combination of the network is favorable with centralized tes and to re- cover waste heat from the data center even though the initial upfront cost is high. quantitative analysis of mo-rdo:both capexo and 𝜒exert con- siderable inﬂuence on the npv eq.(40). in table18, the main kpis that contribute to the ﬁnancial metrics are compared between mo-rdo solu- tions and non-optimized solutions. it can be seen from table18that the economically optimal solution of mo-rdo is close to the non-optimized solution obtained from hdss. as discussed in section 3.3 hdss sizes the network assets based on peak demands and does not account for uncertainties inﬂuence in the asset size optimization. by selecting opti- mization weight 𝛼>0.5in eq.(54), the mo-rdo prioritizes the network investment like the hdss. however, choosing 𝛼<0.5 will lead to the environmental solution, and will increase network investment costs by 10%, while reducing co2 emissions by 13%, ultimately causing 𝜒to increase. in terms of ﬁnancial metrics to evaluate the project feasibil- ity over its life, the npv of environmentally optimal solutions increases energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 18 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 17. optimalnetworkparameterstoyieldoptimalenvironmentalandeconomicalperformances. table 17 summary of robust optimal designs for dc-telenet. description symbol non-optimized mo-rdo mo-rdo mo-rdo unit meanvalues environmentallyoptimal optimal-trade-oﬀ economicallyoptimal supplytemperature 𝑇h 20 15 20 22 ◦c returntemperature 𝑇c 65 6 5 ◦c selectedsection 𝐷2 102 150 135 125 mm table 18 summary of kpi comparison for dc-telenet. description symbol non-optimized mo-rdo mo-rdo unit meanvalues environmentallyoptimal economicallyoptimal overallcapex capexo 815 887 812 keur netsavings 𝜒 80 83 80 keur/y totalemissions te 21 18 20 tonco 2/y netpresentvalue npv 4.0 3.8 4.0 keur/m discountedpaybackperiod dpbp 16 17 16 y by only 3% compared to economically eﬃcient solutions that are not optimized. in addition, the dpbp increases only by 1y. 6. conclusion in this study, a robust design and optimization framework was pre- sented to optimize the design parameters of a 5gdhc network under the inﬂuence of uncertain energy markets and the energy supplies and demands of the participating prosumers. 2000 uncertain scenarios are generated for a potential 5gdhc business case, and an ml is employed on a uniformly distributed input and output data set. the performance of the ml model is analyzed in comparison with the data set, which has been generated from a python-based high-ﬁdelity model. in general, al- most 90% linear agreement between the high-ﬁdelity model and ml model holds for capexo and te. as a result, the ml model replaces the computationally ineﬃcient model of the 5gdhc network with a simple surrogate. before solving the complex formulated multi-objective robust design optimization problem (mo-rdo), the proposed ml model identiﬁes critical design variables in the presence of numerous uncertain inputs from energy markets and the uncertain thermal properties of par- ticipants. this has been accomplished by performing global sensitivity analysis, yielding sobol’s indices to quantify the impact of each input on output. for the considered case study, high sobol’s indices for gas prices in- dicate a greater impact than electricity prices, suggesting that lower gas prices could undermine the 5gdhc business case. additionally, sobol’s analysis identiﬁes which buildings most signiﬁcantly aﬀect the network kpis due to variations in demand and thermal characteristics. accord- ing to sobol’s analysis, only 6 from the considered 24 inputs have a high impact. at the same time, only 3 of the 6 high sobol’s inputs are considered for the mo-rdo, being the supply (𝑇h) and return (𝑇c) tem- perature of the network and the diameter of the network pipes to the building with high sobol’s. consequently, the proposed methodology reduced the computational complexity of the optimization problem by systematically reducing the number of design variables for large net- works. the overall objective of the proposed methodology is to maximize the ﬁnancial metrics such as npv. the components of npv, as given in eq.(40), are optimized along with the objective to reduce the te in the presence of uncertainties. this transforms the network optimization problem into mo-rdo of the form eq.(54). the optimization framework selects the best combination of decision variables that can optimize the objectives as given in eq. (54). each combination represents a variety of optimal designs (environmentally optimal or economically optimal) and trade-oﬀs that form a pareto frontier. in contrast to the economically optimal design, the environmental design had a 10% higher investment cost but reduced te by 13% and improved net savings. only a 3% in- crease in npv is estimated as a risk for the environmentally optimal solution on a ﬁnancial scale. thus, following the eu energy policy for co2 reduction, the environmental design anticipates only a 3% reduc- tion in npv over the project life, despite higher investment costs and uncertain energy markets. the methodology enhances ﬂexibility in identifying key uncertain input parameters and evaluating the robustness of critical performance indicators, addressing the limitations of current gis-based approaches. additionally, applying mo-rdo to the uncertain dataset improves energy & buildings 325 (2024) 114998 19 a.m. chaudhry, g. ghorbaniasl, j. hachez et al. fig. 18. dc-telenet ac and its decomposition is shown against unique cold-side network temperature 𝑇c =0, 5, 10, … , 35 ◦c, and varying hot-side temperature 𝑇h =5 to35 ◦c. decision-making support and serves as a step towards uncertainty-based robust optimization for 5gdhc networks. however, the approach falls short in online optimization, as it lacks the ability to generate random scenarios and optimize simultaneously. future work will incorporate the robust design optimization methodology from the python frame- work rheia [63], which employs the non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm (nsga-ii) combined with polynomial chaos expansion (pce) to explore trade-oﬀs between economic and environmental objectives under uncertainty. credit authorship contribution statement afraz mehmood chaudhry:writing – original draft, visualization, validation, software, project administration, methodology, investiga- tion, formal analysis, conceptualization. ghader ghorbaniasl: writ- ing – review & editing, validation, supervision, investigation, formal analysis, data curation. jonathan hachez:visualization, software, re- sources, formal analysis, data curation, conceptualization. stanislav chicherin: writing – review & editing, visualization, validation, re- sources, formal analysis, data curation. svend bram:writing – review & editing, resources, project administration, funding acquisition, for- mal",no
