Kim2021,2021,Probabilistic modeling of wind energy potential for power grid expansion planning,yes,yes,GP / kriging,high,38,PCE / RSM,0,abstract/keywords,"ough weibull distribution, monte carlo simulation (mcs), and the enhanced spatial modeling based on universal kriging. it can be used to establish the expansion plan for transmission and distribution facilities to resolve the variability and uncertainty issues of win",Historical operational data,high,10,Latin hypercube sampling,0,pdf,"ng. we analyzed the probabilistic model based on a weibull distribution and monte carlo simulations using the measured data of existing wind farms and the estimated potential capacity of wind power resources on jeju island, korea, through the enhanced spatial modeling with",,unassigned,0,P2 acceleration,0,,,Problem UQ,high,8,_other,0,pdf,"eling based on monte carlo simulation before the monte carlo simulation, the r program was used to select the probability distribution model suitable for the capacity factor data of the wind farm. using a histogram of the monte carlo simulation results, the optimal probability model with the lowest bayesian information criterion value was selected, and the appli- cability of the probability model was veri ﬁed. monte carlo simu- lation is a method of random sampling based on parameters extracted by probability ﬁtting of measured data. it is used to model the uncertainty of system elements and to evaluate the probabi- listic steady-state stability of the power system [ 33]. we used a weibull distribution function with two parameters, namely shape and scale, for wind power estimation [ 29]. in this section, the capacity factor was applied to the 2-parameter weibull distribution function. the weibull distribution function is expressed as f ðvþ¼ /c18 k c /c19 /c16 v c /c17 k/c0 1 exp /c18 /c0 /c16 v c /c17 k/c19 (1) where v is the wind power capacity factor, k is the shape parameter, and c is the scale parameter. according to refs. [ 34,35], dimensionless factor shape parame- ter(k) determines the shape of the curve. and scale parameter having same unit as wind power capacity factor. according to ref. [36], for higher value of scale parameter the distribution will be spread over a wider range and having more probability of higher average wind power capacity factor. on the other hand, shape parameter shows the more probability of higher wind power ca- pacity factor. parameter calculation of the weibull distribution was applied using the epm [ 29e31]. eq. (2) is used to calculate the energy tendency factor, while eqs. (3) and (4) represent the shape parameter k and scale parameter c of the weibull distribution, fig. 1. renewable energy share of global electricity production [ 1]. fig. 2. south korea's renewable energy target plan. g. kim and j. hur energy 230 (2021) 120831 2 respectively, where v is the mean, and the de ﬁnition of the gamma function used in scale parameter c is given in eq. (5). epf ¼ v3 /c0 v /c1 3 (2) k ¼ 1 þ 3:69 /c16 epf /c17 2 (3) c ¼ v y /c18 1 þ 1 k /c19 (4) yðxþ¼ ð∞ 0 tx/c0 1e/c0 t dt (5) after calculating the weibull distribution parameters, the sam- ple data extraction and monte carlo simulations were performed 10,000 times to consider the correlation of the capacity factor be- tween wind farms using universal kriging. correlation between the wind farms should be considered because sampling without considering it can distort the probability characteristics of the total energy generation. eq. (6) is a function for calculating the two-parameter weibull distribution mean, m; a and b are the shape and scale parameters of the sample data [ 37]. for the reliability function used in this study [38,39], the 95% con ﬁdence interval for the monte carlo simulation is calculated using eq. (7). m ¼ ay /c18 1 þ 1 b /c19 (6) rðvþ¼ e /c0 /c16 v a /c17 b (7) 2.2. estimation of capacity factors of wind farms based on spatial modeling a variogram is characterized and shaped using the sill, nugget, and range parameters. they are applied to identify the spatial distribution characteristics of the data and are necessary to perform the kriging. a variogram can be de ﬁned as 2yðdþ¼ e /c12/c12/c12 ðzðx þ dþ/c0 zðxþþ2 /c12/c12/c12 (8) where x is the target point and d is the separation distance. theoretical variograms can have a variety of models, such as linear, spherical, exponential, and gaussian. in this section, we used spherical models that are commonly used in spatial modeling [ 40]. the spherical model function can be written as sph ¼ð s /c0 nþ "" 1:5 /c18 d r /c19 /c0 0:5 /c18 d r /c19 3# (9) where s is the sill, n is the nugget, and r is the range. kriging is a representative spatial modeling technique used in geostatistics. it uses weighted linear combinations of surrounding data that are already known to predict the characteristic data of the target point. in this paper, the capacity factor of the new wind farm was estimated using universal kriging. the weight was adjusted to fig. 3. geographic locations of wind farms in jeju island. g. kim and j. hur energy 230 (2021) 120831 3 minimize the error between the predicted and observed data. the universal kriging function is de ﬁned as [ 41]. zðsþ¼ xm i¼0 bixiðsþþ εðsþ (10) where zðsþ is the capacity factor of the target point, and s ¼ð s1s2þ0 is a two-dimensional spatial coordinate. in this paper, cross-validation of the wind power capacity factor at jeju island was performed. the root-mean-squared error (rmse) was calculated as the veri ﬁcation index as follows [ 42]: rmse ¼ ﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃﬃ ﬃ 1 n xn i¼1 /c0 byi /c0 yi /c1 2 vuut (11) where byi is the predicted data and yi is the measured data of the target point. 3. estimation of wind energy potential for power grid expansion planning the capacity factors of eight wind farms on jeju island were calculated using 2017's 1-h unit wind power data clustered over the four seasons to analyze the probability models. wind farm location data such as latitude and longitude were used to perform spatial modeling based on the probability distribution calculated via the weibull distribution and monte carlo simulations. fig. 3 shows the location of each wind farm on jeju island. in this ﬁgure, capital letters in white are existing points of wind farms and lower-case in grey are target points. before estimating the potential of wind energy through proba- bilistic modeling, the wind outputs data were preprocessed as ca- pacity factors, and the capacity factor patterns of each wind power plant in jeju were analyzed. south korea is located in a temperateeclimate zone in the mid elatitudes, with four distinct seasons (spring, summer, fall, and winter), and jeju island is a volcanic island that has stronger wind than inland south korea. wind speed has a high correlation with wind power output, and if the geographical characteristics of jeju island are considered, the wind power output is expected to be highest in winter. as a result of analysis through case study, the wind power capacity factor was highest in winter and lowest in summer. fig. 4 shows the seasonal wind power capacity factor pattern. fig. 5 shows the proposed probabilistic model to estimate wind energy potential for power grid expansion planning. as shown in fig. 5 , the potential capacity factors for new wind farm locations can be determined by the proposed probabilistic model. the algo- rithm consists of four steps, and the contents of each step are as follows. step 1 is the process of calculating the wind power capacity factor based on wind power outputs and installed capacity, and analyzing the characteristics of wind power data. in this paper, data for each wind farm were analyzed by epm and two-parameter weibull distribution. step 2 is the probabilistic modeling process. monte carlo simulations were performed based on the weibull distribution parameters calculated for each wind farm. the maximum and minimum values of the 95% con ﬁdence interval of the extracted sampling data are used as input data to perform the universal kriging of step 3. in step 3, universal kriging was per- formed based on the data of eight existing wind farms, and the proposed model was veri ﬁed through cross-validation. in step 4, the potential capacity factor is estimated using the location (lati- tude and longitude) data of jeju new wind farms for universal kriging. the estimated data is used as a renewable energy genera- tion input scenario of the practical power system, and power grid expansion planning can be established through the analysis of power system elements such as branch ﬂow and bus voltage. fig. 4. seasonal wind power capacity factor pattern for wind farm a. fig. 5. proposed probabilistic model for estimating wind energy potential. g. kim and j. hur energy 230 (2021) 120831 4 3.1. capacity factors of the wind farms based on probabilistic modeling table 1 reports the seasonal weibull distribution parameters ( k, c) at eight points calculated using the epm. monte carlo simulations were performed based on the two-parameter weibull distribution. the probability densities from the monte carlo simulation results for wind farm a based on epm are shown in fig. 6. the 95% con ﬁdence interval of each data item was calculated to compare the theoretical weibull distribution data with the data table 1 weibull distribution parameters. season parameter farm a farm b farm c farm d farm e farm f farm g farm h spring 1.1444 1.1028 1.1238 1.0785 1.1015 1.1391 1.1603 c [mw] 0.2323 0.3028 0.2612 0.3065 0.2114 0.1811 0.4672 0.3708 summer k 1.0683 1.0826 1.0346 1.0468 1.0653 1.0815 1.0552 1.0926 c [mw] 0.1441 0.1646 0.1222 0.1636 0.1385 0.1025 0.2096 0.2214 fall k 1.1803 1.1463 1.0970 1.1787 1.1685 1.2279 1.1997 1.1811 c [mw] 0.2861 0.2744 0.2546 0.3452 0.2414 0.2293 0.4442 0.3509 winter k 1.3824 1.4844 1.3409 1.5656 1.4991 1.5566 1.5971 1.6952 c [mw] 0.3910 0.4631 0.4540 0.5061 0.3961 0.3148 0.8169 0.6330 fig. 6. results based on monte carlo simulation for 