n assumes that one of the existing eight wind farms does not know wind farm capacity factor, and performs universal kriging based on the seven wind farms capacity factors. cross-validation is performed on eight wind farms. to analyze the cross-validation result, the 95% conﬁdence interval of the measured and predicted data through universal kriging were compared. the prediction error of the measured and predicted data for each wind farm was calculated through rmse. as a result of the prediction, it was con ﬁrmed that the wind farm capacity factor prediction was performed well through universal kriging. the reliability of the model estimation results was veriﬁed using cross-validation. the capacity factor of the new wind farm was estimated using the probabilistic model. the location of the new wind farm was set as the point where the wind resources were expected to be high [ 43]. table 4 reports the potential capacity factor of the area calculated through universal kriging. a table of values means the estimated capacity factor of the new wind farm at the 95% con ﬁdence interval. fig. 8 shows estimates of the capacity factor of the new wind farm c for different seasons. in this ﬁgure, red lines are the means and blue lines are 95% con ﬁdence intervals. 3.3. application of the proposed methodology the proposed probabilistic modeling algorithm for estimating wind energy potential can be applied to all spatiotemporal scales. table 5 shows the pseudocode of the proposed algorithm. the measured wind power outputs and installed capacity of wind farms are used to estimate the capacity factor of the new wind farm. the algorithm consists of two steps, the ﬁrst step is probabilistic modeling, and the second step is power system security analysis. in this paper, the weibull distribution, which is the most suit- able probability model for wind power output characteristics, was selected as the ﬁtting model. the proposed algorithm can be applied to other cases with different characteristics if the data is mounted on an appropriate probability model according to the characteristics of the data to be used by the user as well as the wind power data. table 6 line overloading rate based on power grid scenarios. scenario line overload 80% /c20/c2 < 100% line overload 100% /c20/c2 < 150% line overload x /c21 150% base case line c: 94% line d: 86% line h: 87% line k: 91% line g: 111% line i: 132% line j: 148% line a, b: 150% line e, f: 186% line l: 202% scenario 1 line a, b: 85% line d, k: 83% line e, f: 82% line g, l: 84% line i: 100% line a, b: scenario 2 line i: 87% line j: 88% line l: 86% ee fig. 9. power grid expansion planning simulation for base case scenario. g. kim and j. hur energy 230 (2021) 120831 8 3.4. case studies: power grid expansion planning in jeju island's systems the 2030 jeju power grid was modeled using the capacity factor and 95% conﬁdence intervals of the target points extracted through probabilistic modeling. the predicted data of peak power demand and power generation, excluding wind power, were the inputs, and the base case and two scenarios were set for the analysis of line overload and power grid expansion planning. scenario 1 expanded the line capacity by the overload rate for lines with a load overload rate of 100% or higher than the base case, and inputs were the 95% conﬁdence intervals of nine new wind farms estimated through probabilistic modeling. in scenario 2, the capacity of the line with an overload rate of 80% or more of the base case was expanded by the overload rate, and the power generation of the new wind farms were the same as in scenario 1. table 6 reports the line overload for each scenario. fig. 9 is the base case for jeju's power grid. there is a total of eight lines where overload occurs; ﬁve have a line overload rate of 150%, and three have a line overload rate within the 100% e150% interval. in addition, there are four potential overloads (over 80% of the line overload rate). fig. 10 shows the power simulation results of scenarios 1 and 2 with 95% con ﬁdence intervals of the nine wind farm target points estimated in this paper. for scenario 1, there were no lines with an overload rate exceeding 100%, nine lines with an overload rate of within the 80%e100% were analyzed. in the case of scenario 2, there were no lines with an overload rate exceeding 100%, three lines with an overload rate of within the 80% e100% were analyzed. as a result, scenario 2 was more relieved of line overload on a base case than scenario 1. to analysis power system security, pre- dict the power generation of new wind farms and put them into the fig. 10. power grid expansion planning based on two scenarios. g. kim and j. hur energy 230 (2021) 120831 9 system through the probabilistic modeling of wind energy poten- tial proposed in this paper. in future, that probabilistic modeling will be used in future system expansion plans. 4. conclusions the increasing integration of wind energy generation into en- ergy systems has led to difﬁculties in power ﬂow calculations due to uncertainty and variability, which signi ﬁcantly affect the stability and reliability of power grids. in this paper, we propose the prob- abilistic modeling of wind energy potential for power grid expan- sion planning. the probabilistic models that considered the correlations of wind farms were implemented using wind power output data from jeju island, korea, and the capacity factors of wind farms were estimated. to estimate the capacity factor of the new wind farm through universal kriging, power output data from jeju island's wind farms were calculated as capacity factors, and 95% conﬁdence intervals and means were sampled by seasonal weilbull distribution and monte carlo simulations. to perform monte carlo simulations, the optimal probability model with the lowest bic was selected, and each parameter ( k, c) of the weibull distribution was calculated through the epm. sampling data from probability models were used for spatial modeling, and universal kriging was performed by applying a variogram based on the spherical model and speciﬁc parameters considering the existing wind farms. cross- validation was performed to estimate the capacity factors of the new wind farm loctions and to verify the reliability of the model's estimated results. cross-va",no
