Dong2023__2,2023,Robust optimal scheduling for integrated energy systems based on multi-objective confidence gap decision theory,yes,yes,,unassigned,0,GP / kriging,0,,,,unassigned,0,Quasi-Monte Carlo / sparse-grid collocation,0,,,P5 uncertainty,high,36,P4 decomposition,12,abstract/keywords,"multi-objective bi-level confidence gap decision theory robust model (mocgdt). first, a neural network-based surrogate model is used to obtain the inverse cumulative distribution function of a gaussian mixture model for uncertain parameters, constructing a confidence interval uncertainty set. then, the upper-level optimization objective is to maximize the fluctuations of wind, solar, and load, while the lower-level objective is to minimize the total operating cost, resulting in an ies mocgdt robust model. finally, an effective bi-level solution algorithm is developed for the model's characteristics. the case study results demonstrate that the proposed method improves economic costs by 20.69% and 3.87% compared to robust optimization and stochastic optimization, respectively, and reduces computation time by approximately 601 s compared to stochastic optimization. this suggests that the proposed mocgdt model not only reduces the conservatism of conventional robust decision-making but also overcomes the coarseness of uncertain sets in the information gap decision theory model, achieving more reasonable robust scheduling with uncertainty. the effectiveness and superiority of the proposed method are thus verified. © 2023 elsevier ltd computation theory; decision making; distribution functions; electric loads; gaussian distribution; inverse problems; multiobjective optimization; renewable energy resources; uncertainty analysis; confidence gap decision theory; gaussian mixture model; integrated energy systems; multi objective; multi-objective state transition algorithm; robust modeling; robust optimization; robust scheduling; state transitions; uncertainty; decision theory",Problem UQ,high,8,_other,0,pdf,"n how uncertainty factors are modeled ( rahmani & amjady, 2019 ). in so, the uncertainty is described using a probability distribution function (pdf), and typical scenarios are generated through sampling. the optimized solution is then the average optimal solution across all scenarios. in ( vahid-pakdel et al., 2017 ), under the uncertainty of wind power output and price, an energy hub operation strategy considering demand response and the thermal energy market was proposed based on a so method. in ( mansouri et al., 2020 ), the monte carlo method is adopted to generate different scenarios, and then the k-means method is used to reduce the scenarios and propose a strategy for optimal planning. in ( zheng et al., 2021 ), the uncertainty of load and photovoltaic (pv) production in grid-connected residential pv- battery systems is addressed through a proposed two-stage so method. in ( wang et al., 2022 ), a multi-stage so model for an integrated * corresponding author. e-mail address: zhl@xju.edu.cn (h. zhang). contents lists available at sciencedirect expert systems with applications journal homepag e: www.else vier.com/loc ate/eswa https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2023.120304 received 25 february 2023; received in revised form 1 april 2023; accepted 27 april 2023 expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 2 distribution system of resilient electricity and natural gas is proposed. ro involves defining a set of possible values for uncertain parameters and finding a solution that satisfies all constraints for every value in the uncertainty set. the goal of ro is to optimize the objective function such that it performs as well as possible for the worst-case scenario within the uncertainty set ( peng et al., 2015; yang & su, 2021 ). in ( ebrahimi & amjady, 2019 ), an adaptive robust optimization framework for day- ahead microgrid scheduling is proposed, and its effectiveness is veri - fied by test cases. in ( zhong et al., 2021 ), a three-level ro model for ies is developed to address multiple uncertainties, including the output of nomenclature abbreviations ies integrated energy system chp combined heat and power so stochastic optimization ro robust optimization igdt information gap decision theory mocgdt multi-objective confidence gap decision theory cdf cumulative distribution function gmm gaussian mixture model rmse root mean square error mae absolute average error wt wind turbine pv photovoltaic eb electrical boilers ec electrical chiller ac absorption chiller es electricity storage hs heat storage sta state transition algorithm pmosta population-based multi-objective sta nsga-ii non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm mopso multi-objective particle swarm optimization cny chinese yuan index t index of times i index of gaussian components parameters λ objective deviation factor c e 1 , t electricity purchase price in period t c e 2 , t electricity sale price in period t c g , t natural gas price in period t c om n equipment n maintenance cost c waste , t penalty cost coefficient per unit of wind power, pv and cutting load in period t p min chp lower limits of power generation of chp p max chp upper limits of power generation of chp h min chp lower limits of the output heat of chp h max chp upper limits of the output heat of chp γ chp thermoelectric ratio of chp η chp power generation efficiency of chp unit p min eb lower limits of power consumed of eb p max eb upper limits of power consumed of eb γ eb conversion efficiency of the eb h min ac lower limits of heat consumed by ac h max ac upper limits of heat consumed by ac γ ac conversion efficiency of ac p min ec lower limits of power consumed by ec p max ec upper limits of power consumed by ec γ ec conversion efficiency of ec η ch es the charging efficiencies of es η dis es the discharging efficiencies of es η ch hs the charging efficiencies of hs η dis hs discharging efficiencies of hs e es ␀ min lower limits of es capacity e es ␀ max upper limits of e