-stage ro dispatch model is developed for integrated power and heat network. in (wu et al., 2022), to address heterogeneous un - certainties in the ies planning of the park, a two-stage ro model is proposed to obtain the equipment configuration scheme of each plan - ning stage. in (chen et al., 2022), an adjustable two-stage robust energy management optimization framework is established considering the reg output and load uncertainties. it should be noted that both so and ro have shortcomings: too many scene trees in so will lead to a sudden increase in the amount of calculation, and scene reduction not only loses the ergodicity of the uncertain interval but also cannot guarantee robustness. if the clustering algorithm is not accurate enough, the typical scene after the scene reduction is not representative enough. ro is generally based on the preset robustness coefficient for robustness analysis, and its robustness setting is often too subjective or conservative. information gap decision theory (igdt) is an efficient model for managing uncertain risks that involves analyzing the potential impact of uncertain parameters while still meeting predetermined objectives (izadi et al., 2023). in (mehdizadeh et al., 2018), risk-based energy management of renewable energy-based microgrids in the presence of peak load management is performed using information gap decision theory. in (vahid-ghavidel et al., 2019), an igdt-based robust decision model for short-term self-scheduling of demand response (dr) aggre - gators is proposed, considering the impact of uncertainty in market electricity prices and consumer participation on the scheduling strategy. in (liu et al., 2022), a multi-energy retailer scheduling strategy with dr based on igdt is proposed. in (najafi et al., 2022), a novel optimization framework is proposed for optimal self-scheduling of an energy hub, which utilizes a combination of igdt and ro. however, the igdt method is inadequate in dealing with uncertain variables as it does not consider their distribution probability characteristics. moreover, most igdt models only consider the impact of a single uncertain variable and fail to fully analyze the relationship between multiple uncertain vari - ables. this study aims to optimize the dispatch of ies under multiple uncertainties, including wind, pv, and load variations, with the objec - tive of minimizing economic costs. given the limitations of existing optimization models such as ro, so, and the basic igdt model for handling uncertain optimization problems, a data-driven multi-objec - tive confidence gap decision theory (mocgdt) model is proposed to solve the ies dispatch problem. table 1 intuitively summarizes the comprehensive comparison between this work and existing research work in solving energy management with uncertainty. the main con - tributions of this study can be summarized as follows: (1) to overcome the limitations of the basic igdt model, which uses a symmetric box set to describe uncertain parameters, a gaussian mixture model (gmm) is used to fit the probability distribution of the prediction error of the uncertain parameters. an surrogate -based model is then used to solve the inverse cumulative dis - tribution function, which provides confidence intervals for the uncertain parameters. the use of confidence intervals instead of box sets to describe the range of uncertain parameters improves the objectivity and accuracy of the model. (2) to address the issue of igdt’s inability to handle multiple un - certain parameters simultaneously, the multi-objective optimi - zation theory is incorporated into the igdt model, along with the confidence interval modeling of uncertain parameters, resulting in the mocgdt robust model for solving multiple uncertain optimization problems. (3) to validate the effectiveness of the proposed mocgdt robust model, it is applied to ies scheduling problems with wind, pv, and load uncertainties. since the mocgdt is a bi-level model, an efficient bi-level solution algorithm is designed based on the characteristics of the model. a multi-objective intelligent opti - mization algorithm is used to solve the upper-level multi-objec - tive problem, and the cplex solver is used to solve the lower- level mixed-integer programming problem. the rest of this article will be presented as follows. in section 2, the proposed mocgdt is described in detail. in section 3, the mocgdt- based robust model of ies is proposed and the solving algorithm for the model is designed. in section 4, the effectiveness of the proposed method is verified on a specific ies case. finally, the conclusion is given in section 5. 2. proposed method in this section, we introduce the construction process of the mocgdt in detail. firstly, the confidence interval of uncertain variables is constructed based on gmm in a data-driven way. then the confidence interval is used to replace the box uncertainty set in igdt. finally, combined with multi-objective optimization technology, the mocgdt model is proposed. 2.1. uncertainty variable description based on data-driven 2.1.1. fitting probability distribution by gmm in real-world scenarios where data is collected from multiple sources with varying characteristics, the presence of complex and unknown noise is inevitable. in such cases, it is prudent to model the probability distribution of the real noise, especially when its true distribution re - mains elusive. this approach can help improve the accuracy and reli - ability of the data analysis and enable more robust predictions and table 1 comparing this work with recent research for uncertainty optimization. ref multi-energy uncertainty uncertain modeling methods solving algorithm power heat cooling wind pv load (vahid-pakdel et al., 2017) ✓ ✓ ⨯ ✓ ⨯ ⨯ so cplex (mansouri et al., 2020) ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ⨯ ✓ so benders decomposition (zheng et al., 2021) ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ⨯ ✓ ✓ so cplex (wang et al., 2022) ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ⨯ ⨯ ✓ so gurobi (peng et al., 2015) ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ✓ ✓ ✓ ro differential evolution (ebrahimi & amjady, 2019) ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ✓ ✓ ⨯ ro benders decomposition (zhong et al., 2021) ✓ ✓ ⨯ ✓ ✓ ⨯ ro c&cg, admm (tan et al., 2022) ✓ ✓ ⨯ ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ro gurobi (wu et al., 2022) ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ro c&cg (chen et al., 2022) ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ro c&cg (mehdizadeh et al., 2018) ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ✓ ✓ ✓ igdt gams (vahid-ghavidel et al., 2019) ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ⨯ ⨯ ✓ igdt gams (liu et al., 2022) ✓ ✓ ✓ ⨯ ⨯ ⨯ igdt – (najafi et al., 2022) ✓ ✓ ✓ igdt-ro cplex this work ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ mocgdt pmosta, cplex y. dong et al. expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 4 insights ( wang, wang, hu, 2017 ). gmm has been proven to have better approximation capability for the continuous distribution of complex noise ( dong et al., 2021; zhao et al., 1995 ). therefore, gmm is employed to fit the wind-pv-load forecasting error distribution in a data-driven way. the fluctuation intervals of the wind-pv-load uncertain variables are constructed at a given confidence level, thus overcoming the shortcomings of the boxed uncertainty set. assuming that the unknown errors are random variables, the mathematical formula of the gmm can be observed in eq. (1) . ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ f gmm = ∑ k i = 1 ρ i n i ␀ u ⃒ ⃒ μ i , σ 2 i ) s . t . ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ 0⩽ ρ i ⩽1 ∑ k i = 1 ρ i = 1 (1) where k is the number of gaussian components; n i ␀ u ⃒ ⃒ μ i , σ 2 i ) represents the ith gaussian distribution; ρ i , μ i , σ 2 i are the weight coefficient, expectation, and variance of the ith gaussian distribution, respectively. this paper utilizes the historical wind power and pv data of a particular location from january 2021 to october 2021 as a case study. the wind-pv forecasting error ’ s probability distribution for this period is fitted using gmm with 2 – 6 gaussian components and is compared with the normal distribution. note that most of the current studies use the normal distribution to deal with the load uncertainty due to the relatively weak load fluctuations. therefore, for simplicity, this paper uses a normal distribution to describe the load forecasting error. fig. 1 shows the pdf of wind power and pv forecasting error. to quantify the fitting effect of each model, tables 2 and 3 show the fitting metrics of gmm and normal distribution including root mean square error (rmse) and mean absolute error (mae). eqs. (2) and (3) provide the formulas (a) pdf curve of wind power forecasting error (b) pdf fitting curve of pv forecasting error fig. 1. pdf curves of wind power and pv forecasting error with various gmm. table 2 accuracy comparison of fitting error distribution of wind power forecasting with gmm. index normal distribution gmm- 2 gmm- 3 gmm- 4 gmm- 5 gmm- 6 mae 0.1520 0.0453 0.0152 0.0092 0.0064 0.0063 rmse 0.3103 0.0949 0.0292 0.0189 0.0139 0.0141 table 3 accuracy comparison of fitting error distribution of pv forecasting with gmm. index normal distribution gmm- 2 gmm- 3 gmm- 4 gmm- 5 gmm- 6 mae 0.1139 0.0447 0.0427 0.0348 0.0304 0.0250 rmse 0.1667 0.0628 0.0613 0.0539 0.0424 0.0456 (a) probability confidence intervals of wind power forecasting error (b) probability confidence intervals of pv forecasting error fig. 2. probability confidence intervals of wind power and pv forecasting error. y. dong et al. expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 5 for mae and rmse, respectively. mae = 1 n s ∑ n s t = 1 | y t ␀ ̂y t | (2) rmse = ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅̅̅̅̅̅̅ ̅ 1 n s ∑ n s t = 1 ( y t ␀ ̂y t ) 2 √ √ √ √ (3) where y t is the actual value; and ̂y t is the value fitted to the probability distribution model; n s is the number of samples. from the fitting results in tables 2 and 3 , the fitting results of gmm are better than that of normal distribution, and with the increase of the number of gaussian components of gmm, the mae and rmse obtained by gmm are smaller, indicating that the fitting effect is getting better and better. however, there is little difference in fitting accuracy between gmm-4, gmm-5, and gmm-6. therefore, gmm-4 is selected in this paper to fit the probability distribution of forecasting error by making a trade-off between computational efficiency and fitting accuracy. 2.1.2. inverse cdf from section 2.1.1 , it is easy to understand that after obtaining the pdf of wind-pv-load forecasting error, the upper and lower limits of the forecasting error can be calculated by setting the value of the confidence level 1 ␀ α t of the probability distribution at different moments and thus constructing the fluctuation interval of the uncertain variables. as shown in fig. 2 , when 1 ␀ α t = 0 . 9 is set, the forecasting errors of wind power and pv can be obtained by fitting them with gmm-4. in fig. 2 , δ p l w , t and δ p u w , t are the maximum values of the negative and positive deviations of the wind power forecasting error; δ p l s , t and δ p u s , t are the maximum values of the negative and positive deviations of the pv forecasting error, respectively. obviously, to construct the fluctuation intervals for wind power and pv at a given confidence level 1 ␀ α t , it is necessary to solve for the specific values of δ p l w , t , δ p u w , t , δ p l s , t and δ p u s , t through the inverse cumulative distribution function (cdf). take wind power as an example, as shown in eq. (4) . δ p l w , t = f ␀ 1 w , t ( α t 2 ) , δ p u w , t = f ␀ 1 w , t ( 1 ␀ α t 2 ) (4) where f ␀ 1 w , t ( ⋅ ) is the inverse cdf of wind power forecasting error. due to the complexity of the mathematical expressions of the gmm, it is difficult to solve its inverse cdf directly. some scholars fit the cdf of gmm by high-order polynomials and then obtain the solution of its inverse function by the polynomial root method ( wang, shen et al., 2017 ). however, when the order of the polynomial is greater than 4, it is difficult to solve the analytic roots of the polynomial ( klein, 2003; serre, 1979 ). given this defect, back propagation neural network (bpnn) ( wang et al., 2011 ) with good fitting characteristics is used as the sur - rogate model to directly fit the inverse cdf, and a method to solve the inverse cdf surrogate-based model is proposed to avoid the complex process of indirectly fitting the cdf and then solving the root of the inverse cdf. the reason why the surrogate model can accurately describe the inverse cdf of gmm is that the following three conditions are met simultaneously: (1) the cdf is monotonic, and its inverse cdf is also monotonic, so there is a one-to-one mapping relationship between the input and output of the constructed proxy model. (2) the selected sample points are evenly distributed in the whole variable space and the sample size is large enough. (3) the surrogate model has a strong fitting ability. since condition (1) is an inherent property of the cdf, the above three conditions can be satisfied only by selecting appropriate samples and the surrogate model, and the surrogate model can well fit the characteristics of the inverse cdf. specifically, a large number of sample points ( x , y ) are obtained by integrating the pdf. note that x represents the probability value and y represents the random variable. then; x is taken as the input and y as the output, bpnn is used to fit the sample, to obtain the inverse cdf. fig. 3 shows the inverse cdf obtained by the bpnn surrogate model when the number of gaussian components k is 4. 2.1.3. confidence interval modeling of wind-pv-load uncertainty after obtaining the inverse cdf, confidence intervals for the wind- pv-load uncertainty variables in the ies can be constructed for each period based on the given confidence level, as described below. ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ p w , t ∈ u i ␀ δ p w , t , α w , t ) = { p w , t ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ̃ p w , t + δ p l w , t ⩽ p w , t ⩽ ̃ p w , t + δ p u w , t } δ p w , t ∈ { δ p w , t | δ p l w , t ⩽δ p w , t ⩽δ p u w , t } δ p l w , t = f ␀ 1 w , t ( α w , t 2 ) , δ p u w , t = f ␀ 1 w , t ( 1 ␀ α w , t 2 ) (5) (a) inverse cdf obtained by bpnn for wind power forecasting error (b) inverse cdf obtained by bpnn forecasting error fig. 3. the inverse cdf is obtained by the surrogate-based model. y. dong et al. expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 6 ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ p s , t ∈ u i ␀ δ p s , t , α s , t ) = { p s , t ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ̃ p s , t + δ p l s , t ⩽ p s , t ⩽ ̃ p s , t + δ p u s , t } δ p s , t ∈ { δ p s , t | δ p l s , t ⩽δ p s , t ⩽δ p u s , t } δ p l s , t = f ␀ 1 s , t ( α s , t 2 ) , δ p u s , t = f ␀ 1 s , t ( 1 ␀ α s , t 2 ) (6) ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ p l , t ∈ u i ␀ δ p l , t , α l , t ) = { p l , t ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ̃ p l , t + δ p l l , t ⩽ p l , t ⩽ ̃ p l , t + δ p u l , t } δ p l , t ∈ { δ p l , t | δ p l l , t ⩽δ p l , t ⩽δ p u l , t } δ p l l , t = f ␀ 1 l , t ( α l , t 2 ) , δ p u l , t = f ␀ 1 l , t ( 1 ␀ α l , t 2 ) (7) where p w , t , p s , t , and p l , t are the actual observation values of wind power, pv, and load in period t , respectively; ̃ p w , t , ̃ p s , t , and ̃ p l , t are respectively the predicted values in period t ; α w , t , α s , t , and α l , t are their significance levels; δ p l w , t , δ p l s , t , and δ p l l , t are respectively the lower limit of the confidence interval of wind power, pv, and load forecasting error in period t ; δ p u w , t , δ p u s , t , and δ p u l , t are the upper limits of the confidence interval in period t , respectively; 2.2. proposed multi-objective confidence gap decision theory the igdt robust model ( ben-haim, 2006 ) is designed to ensure that the impact of uncertainty on optimization results is maximized, while still guaranteeing that the optimization result will not be worse than a predetermined result ( tostado-v ´eliz et al., 2023 ). the mathematical model is as follows. ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ max x ε s . t . ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ f ( x , u ) ⩽ f c , f c = ( 1 + λ ) f 0 h ( x , u ) = 0 g ( x , u ) ⩽0 u ∈ u ( ̃u , ε ) = { u | ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ u ␀ ̃u ̃u ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⃒ ⩽ ε } (8) where x is the variable to be optimized; u is the uncertain parameter of the optimization model; u ( ̃u , ε ) denotes the fluctuation interval of the uncertain parameter, here, ̃u and ε are predicted values and fluctuation bias factors of uncertain parameters, respectively; f 0 is the optimal objective function value obtained when u is equal to ̃u under the deterministic optimization model; λ is the objective deviation factor, the larger its value is set, the more tolerant the model is to objective vari - ation and the more robust it is, and vice versa. f c is the worst objective value acceptable to the decision-maker after accounting for uncertainty. it is not difficult to understand the robustness of the aforementioned igdt model when dealing with uncertain optimization problems. however, it should be emphasized that the basic igdt model can only handle a single uncertain optimization problem. in addition, the box uniform symmetry fluctuation interval u ( ̃u , ε ) is too rough to describe the uncertainty, does not fully consider the distribution probability characteristics of uncertain parameters, and cannot accurately reflect the fluctuation interval of uncertain variables in practice. given the above shortcomings, in this paper, the confidence interval of the prob - ability distribution is employed instead of the boxed uncertainty set in the igdt model, and the deviation coefficient of uncertainty quantity in the igdt is eliminated. the proposed mocgdt model combines multi- objective optimization with igdt to handle multiple uncertainties. through the mocgdt model, decision-makers can analyze and select the most optimal solution from a set of options, considering multiple objectives and uncertainties. in essence, the mocgdt model provides a robust and comprehensive framework for decision-making that can effectively manage multiple uncertainties. ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ max x { 1 ␀ α 1 , 1 ␀ α 2 , 1 ␀ α 3 , ⋯ , 1 ␀ α m } s . t . ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ 1 ␀ α i = pr i { δ u l i ⩽δ u i ⩽δ u u i } 0⩽ α i ⩽1 , i = 1 , 2 , 3 , ⋯ , m f ( x , u 1 , u 2 ⋯ , u n ) ⩽ f c , f c = ( 1 + λ ) f 0 h ( x , u 1 , u 2 ⋯ , u n ) = 0 g ( x , u 1 , u 2 ⋯ , u n ) ⩽0 u i ∈ u i ( δ u i , α i ) = { u i ⃒ ⃒ ̃u i + δ u l i ⩽ u i ⩽ ̃u i + δ u u i } δ u i ∈ { δ u i | δ u l i ⩽δ u i ⩽δ u u i } δ u l i = f ␀ 1 i ( α i 2 ) , δ u u i = f ␀ 1 i ( 1 ␀ α i 2 ) (9) fig. 4. structure diagram of ies. y. dong et al. expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 7 where m is the number of uncertain parameters and u i is the ith uncer - tainty; pr i ( ⋅ ) , 1 ␀ α i and f ␀ 1 i ( ⋅ ) denotes the probability distribution, confidence level, and inverse cdf of the deviation of the ith uncertain parameter, respectively; ̃u i , δ u i , δ u l i and δ u u i is the predicted value, de - viation quantity, negative deviation and positive deviation, respectively. generally, δ u l i < 0, δ u u i > 0. obviously, the proposed mocgdt model can handle multiple un - certainty optimization problems simultaneously compared to the basic igdt model. in addition, the uncertainty confidence intervals based on probability distributions can more accurately reflect the fluctuation in - tervals of uncertain variables in practice. the optimization process is achieved by driving the confidence level of each uncertain variable to be maximized, thus maximizing the ability to avoid the influence of un - certain variables on the optimization results. 3. mocgdt-based robust scheduling for ies 3.1. mathematical formulation of ies the ies studied in this paper mainly includes electrical chiller (ec), absorption chiller (ac), wind turbine (wt), pv, combined heat and power (chp), electrical boilers (eb), electricity storage (es), heat stor - age (hs), power load, heating load, and cooling load. its structure is shown in fig. 4 . this paper 