on objective eq. (10) , and constraint eqs. (11)- (30). ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ max x { γ a , γ b , γ c } s . t . ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ γ a = ∑ t t = 1 ␀ 1 ␀ α w , t ) , γ b = ∑ t t = 1 ␀ 1 ␀ α s , t ) , γ c = ∑ t t = 1 ␀ 1 ␀ α l , t ) 1 ␀ α w , t = pr w { δ p l w , t ⩽δ p w , t ⩽δ p u w , t } 1 ␀ α s , t = pr s { δ p l s , t ⩽δ p s , t ⩽δ p u s , t } 1 ␀ α l , t = pr l { δ p l l , t ⩽δ p l , t ⩽δ p u l , t } 0⩽ α w , t ⩽1 , 0⩽ α s , t ⩽1 , 0⩽ α l , t ⩽1 f ␀ x , p w , t , p s , t , p l , t ) ⩽ f c , f c = ( 1 + λ ) f 0 eqs . ( 5 ) ␀ ( 7 ) , ( 11 ) ␀ ( 30 ) (31) where, f 0 is the expected objective value obtained under the determin - istic ies scheduling model. γ a , γ b and γ c represent the robustness targets of wind power, pv and load, respectively. x represents the scheduling scheme. the mocgdt model cannot be directly solved and is dependent on the uncertainty variable fluctuations. this is essentially a bi-level opti - mization model with a multi-objective upper model to maximize the fluctuation range of wind-pv-load uncertainties while considering a predetermined operating cost, and a mixed-integer lower model to solve for the operating cost given a range of fluctuations and confirm it is less than the predetermined cost. as per the eq. (10) , the operating cost of ies increases when the wind-pv output is low, and the load demand is high. therefore, the lower optimization model checks whether the operating cost meets the predetermined cost when taking the lower limit of wind-pv output and the upper limit of load, given a known confidence level. fig. 10. pareto front of robust optimal dispatching of ies based on mocgdt when λ = 0 . 1. table 6 pareto solutions for the four typical scenarios obtained by the mocgdt when λ = 0 . 1. scenario robustness objective costs/cny γ a γ b γ c optimal compromise 14.52 16.75 14.02 10,207.58 optimal γ a 18.12 13.44 12.45 10,223.36 optimal γ b 12.76 18.10 12.78 10,100.89 optimal γ c 11.54 12.47 16.93 10,205.94 fig. 11. optimal dispatching for power supply. y. dong et al. expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 12 3.3. solving algorithm of mocgdt robust model according to the characteristics of the mocgdt model, an efficient bi-level solution algorithm is designed. swarm-based intelligent algo - rithms have demonstrated natural advantages in solving such problems. the state transition algorithm (sta) ( zhou et al., 2012 ) is a popular and efficient optimization algorithm known for its fast convergence, robustness, and strong search capabilities. in recent years, it has gained widespread recognition and has been successfully implemented in various domains. therefore, a population-based multi-objective sta (pmosta) ( dong et al., 2021 ) is used to solve the upper-level model. pmosta employs state transformation operators (stos) to generate candidate populations, and then the next generation population is selected by combining the concepts of pareto domination and crowding ranking in nsga-ii ( deb et al., 2002 ). the four stos used in pmosta are as follows: x k + 1 = x k + ε α 1 n ‖ x k ‖ 2 r r x k (32) x k + 1 = x k + ε β r t x k ␀ x k ␀ 1 ‖ x k ␀ x k ␀ 1 ‖ 2 (33) x k + 1 = x k + ε γ r e x k (34) x k + 1 = x k + ε δ r a x k (35) where ε α , ε β , ε γ and ε δ are positive constants, called state transformation factors. r r ∈ r n × n is a matrix of randomly generated variables uniformly distributed in the interval [-1,1]. r t ∈ r is a uniformly distributed random variable in the interval [0,1]. r e ∈ r n × n is a diagonal matrix with randomly generated elements that follow a standard normal dis - tribution. r a ∈ r n × n is a diagonal matrix with randomly distributed el - ements following a standard normal distribution, except for a single position where a non-zero value is randomly assigned. the stos in sta have different functions that satisfy the functional needs of global search, local search, and heuristic search, which enable sta to proba - bilistically find the global optimal pareto front. for example, the candidate solutions generated by the rotation operator are all within a hypersphere of radius ε α , and the operator has global search capability when taking a large value; conversely, it has local exploitation capa - bility. in addition, this paper proposes a non-linear control parameter strategy, presented in eq. (36) , to balance the global and local search ability of the rotation operator. ε α = c 1 ␀ ( c 1 ␀ c 2 ) × sin ( π 2 ⋅ ( g g max ) 2 ) (36) where g and g max denote the current iteration number and the maximum iteration number, respectively. c 1 and c 2 denote the upper limit and lower limit of the rotation factor, respectively; for the solution of the lower-level model, when the confidence level of the upper-level is given, the range of uncertainties can be obtained, thus obtaining the extreme scenarios of wind-pv-load, as shown in eq. (37) . fig. 12. optimal dispatching for heat supply. fig. 13. optimal dispatching for cool supply. y. dong et al. expert systems with applications 228 (2023) 120304 13 ⎧ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ p w , t = ̃ p w , t + f ␀ 1 w , t ( α w , t 2 ) p s , t = ̃ p s , t + f ␀ 1 s , t ( α s , t 2 ) p l , t = ̃ p l , t + f ␀ 1 l , t ( 1 ␀ α l , t 2 ) (37) it is not difficult to understand that the lower-level model is a mixed integer programming problem. in this paper, the yalmip toolbox in the matlab platform is used for simulation, and cplex solver is called to solve it. fig. 5 depicts the framework diagram of the proposed method for solving ies robust optimal scheduling. 4. case study 4.1. simulation setup to validate the proposed mocgdt model, this paper employs the ies illustrated in fig. 4 for simulation. the parameters and data used in ies scheduling case are all from reference ( peng et al., 2021; shi et al., 2019 ; l. yang et al., 2018 ). the predicted values of wind power, pv, and loads are shown in fig. 6 ; the natural gas price is 2.5 cny/m 3 ; the parameters of ies and the electricity prices are shown in table 4 and table 5 , respectively; the parameters of pmosta algorithm are set as follows: the population size is 40, the maximum number of iterations is 40, ε β = ε γ = ε δ = 1, c 1 = 1 and c 2 = 1 e ␀ 2. 4.2. simulation analysis 4.2.1. robust scheduling with single uncertainty in this subsection, only the effect of a single uncertain variable of wind-pv-load on the robust scheduling results is considered, and the upper model is a single-objective problem, which can be solved by using the basic sta algorithm. firstly, we get the optimal operating cost 9301.56 cny by solving the deterministic model, and then according to different expected operating costs of decision-makers, curves of eco - nomic cost and robustness varying with the objective deviation factor can be obtained, as depicted in fig. 7 and fig. 8 . fig. 7 and fig. 8 reveal a positive correlation between the objective deviation factor and both operating cost and robustness. in the robust - ness model, the maximum fluctuation confidence level of wind-pv-load, both increase with the increase of the objective deviation factor, that is, the larger the acceptable maximum fluctuation range of wind-pv-load when the expected operation cost is high, the better the robustness of the obtained dispatching strategy is and the more it can resist large uncertain fluctuations. it should be emphasized that when the objective deviation factor reaches a certain threshold, the maximum confidence level of wind-pv-load for each dispatching period will reach the fig. 14. fluctuation interval of actual wind power, photovoltaic and load forecasts, respectively. table 7 the optimal compromise solutions of ies robust scheduling under differen λ . λ preset worst cost robustness objective costs/cny γ a γ b γ c 0.05 9,766.64 11.48 13.81