bendriss_novel_2025,2025,A novel improved Frilled Lizard algorithm for solving the optimal planning problem of renewable energy sources within distribution grids under uncertainties,yes,yes,Neural network,high,8,PCE / RSM,0,pdf,"grat- ing renewable energy sources into the ieee 33-bus distribution grid is proposed, using a bi-directional long short-term memory network to manage uncertainties from pv and wind generation. the model, optimized with binary particle swarm optimization (bpso), aimed to minimize e",Historical operational data,high,9,Latin hypercube sampling,0,pdf,"pdfs) is operated to model the stochastic behavior of pv and wt power generation, respectively, based on real historical data collected over a decade from the study area. 3. the optimal locations and number of wts and pv strings, as well as the pf of wts, are determined simu",,unassigned,0,P2 acceleration,0,,,Problem UQ,high,8,_other,0,pdf,"gorithm considered uncertainties related to pv and wind output, electricity prices, and load variations using probability distribution functions. in [26], a hybrid model for integrat- ing renewable energy sources into the ieee 33-bus distribution grid is proposed, using a bi-directional long short-term memory network to manage uncertainties from pv and wind generation. the model, optimized with binary particle swarm optimization (bpso), aimed to minimize energy losses and voltage deviations while considering long- term and seasonal impacts. most of these studies have addressed the opp of ress while considering load, solar irradiation, and wind speed uncertainties. however, they have largely overlooked temperature- related uncertainties, which limits their scope. additional limitations in the existing literature include the assumption of fixed locations or sizes for ress, as well as the neglect of certain grid constraints. moreover, the majority of these approaches assumed that wts operate at unity power factors (pfs). consequently, these studies focus exclusively on the location and size of the wts, neglecting the optimization of their pf, which is crucial for enhancing system performance and overall grid efficiency. table 1 summarizes the current work and other relevant research on the opp of ress within rdg. in this paper, we present a novel optimization approach, termed the improved frilled lizard (iflo) algorithm, designed to simultaneously determine the optimal positions and number of wts and pv strings, as well as the optimal pf of wts, within the 85-bus radial distribution grid. our primary objective includes minimizing average annual power losses and voltage deviation (vd) while maximizing the yearly aver- age voltage stability index (vsi) within operational constraints. the study considers uncertainties in res, including fluctuations in wind speed, availability of solar irradiance, temperature variations, and time- varying load demand, based on real historical collected data from the study area over a seasonal planning horizon. the proposed approach is evaluated against several established optimization techniques, includ- ing the well-known grey wolf optimizer (gwo) [27], harris hawks optimization (hho) [28], chernobyl disaster optimizer (cdo) [29], dingo optimization algorithm (doa) [30], whale optimization algo- rithm (woa) [31], black-winged kite algorithm (bka) [32] and the original flo [33]. however, the novelty and main achievements of this research are summarized below: 1. a novel improved frilled lizard (iflo) algorithm is developed and proposed to solve the optimal planning problem (opp) of ress within the radial distribution grids (rdgs). 2. a more realistic approach based on beta and weibull probability density functions (pdfs) is operated to model the stochastic behavior of pv and wt power generation, respectively, based on real historical data collected over a decade from the study area. 3. the optimal locations and number of wts and pv strings, as well as the pf of wts, are determined simultaneously under sea- sonal uncertainties. this aims to reduce average annual power losses and voltage deviation (vd) while enhancing the yearly average voltage stability index (vsi), through a multi-objective optimization problem formulation. energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 3 b. bendriss et al. table 1 comparison analysis between the current study and other relevant works for the opp of ress within rdg. ref. year solving method improved version technical objectives installation uncertainty power voltage voltage wt pv loading wind solar temperature losses deviation stability speed irradiance [13] 2023 apso ✓ ✓ × × ✓ × × × × × [18] 2015 wapso × ✓ × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × [19] 2021 mrfo × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × ✓ × × × [20] 2021 modified hgso ✓ ✓ × × × ✓ ✓ × ✓ × [21] 2022 ga × ✓ × × × ✓ ✓ × ✓ × [23] 2021 roa × ✓ × × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × [24] 2023 aha × × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × [22] 2023 aeo-obl ✓ ✓ × × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × [17] 2023 gwo × ✓ ✓ × ✓ × × × × × [25] 2024 pi-gsa ✓ ✓ ✓ × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × [26] 2024 bpso ✓ ✓ ✓ × ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ × this study iflo ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ notation: each column corresponds to a specific feature. the symbol ‘‘✓’’ indicates that the paper includes the feature, while the ‘‘×’’ signifies its absence. 4. the effectiveness and reliability of the iflo algorithm are thor- oughly assessed via extensive statistical comparison with other efficient optimization techniques using standard benchmark func- tions. 5. the proposed iflo outperforms other developed optimization techniques, namely gwo, bka, and the original flo, in solv- ing the optimal planning problem of ress in the ieee 85-bus rdg, demonstrating its effectiveness in dealing with uncertain- ties within a complex optimization framework. 6. to the best of the authors’ knowledge, no one has considered the uncertainties in the ambient temperature which has a major influence on pv systems. the subsequent parts of the paper are organized as follows: sec- tion 2 introduces the probabilistic system parameters model, which includes both load and ress. section 3 provides a detailed mathemat- ical formulation of the objective functions and constraints. the design of the flo and the proposed iflo algorithms is described in sections 4 and 5, respectively. the analysis of load profiles and weather data is discussed in section 6. section 7 presents the simulation results and discussion. finally, section 8 concludes the paper and outlines future research directions. 2. system modeling this section details the modeling approaches for each uncertain pa- rameter. the study considers time-varying constant load demand. since power generated by wts and solar pv systems depends on meteorologi- cal factors like wind speed, solar irradiance, and ambient temperature, the stochastic behavior of these factors must be thoroughly analyzed during the initial phase to ensure efficient integration of wt and pv systems. 2.1. load modeling the system being studied is presumed to adhere to the time-varying load curve of the ieee-rts [34,35]. to analyze seasonal variations in demand, the yearly load is segmented into four seasons: summer, autumn, winter, and spring. each load season is represented by the hourly load of each day (24 h). thus, with four season days, this results in 96 h (24 × 4), representing the entire year load (8760 h). 2.2. renewable energy resources (ress) modeling 2.2.1. wind speed modeling the stochastic behavior of wind speed over a specified time period is described using the weibull pdf [36,37]. the following equation expresses the weibull pdf of wind speed 𝑤𝑡 in (m∕s) at 𝑡th hour. 𝑓 𝑡 𝑤(𝑤) = ( 𝑘𝑡 𝑐𝑡 ) ( 𝑤𝑡 𝑐𝑡 )(𝑘𝑡−1) ×exp [ − ( 𝑤𝑡 𝑐𝑡 )𝑘𝑡 ] for 𝑤𝑡 > 0, 𝑐𝑡 > 0 and𝑘𝑡 > 0 (1) the shape factor (𝑘𝑡) and scale factor (𝑐𝑡) at 𝑡th hour are computed as below. 𝑘𝑡 = ( 𝜎𝑡 𝑤 𝜇𝑡 𝑤 )−1.086 (2) 𝑐𝑡 = 𝜇𝑡 𝑤 𝛤 (1 + 1∕𝑘𝑡) (3) here, 𝜇𝑡 𝑣 and 𝜎𝑡 𝑣 symbolize the mean and standard deviation of wind speed at the 𝑡th hour. 2.2.2. solar irradiance modeling the random nature of solar irradiance is represented using the beta pdf [36,38]