e determined based on the cut-in wind speed 𝑤𝑐 𝑖𝑛 and the nominal wind speed 𝑤𝑛 as follows, 𝛼 = 𝑃𝑟𝑎𝑡𝑒𝑑(𝑤3 𝑛 − 𝑤3 𝑐 𝑖𝑛 ) (10) 𝛽 = 𝑤3 𝑐 𝑖𝑛(𝑤3 𝑐 𝑖𝑛 − 𝑤3 𝑛 ) (11) the non-linear performance characteristics curve of the wt is dis- played in fig. 1. 2.2.3.2. power generation by solar pv. the average power output of solar pv that corresponds to a partic- ular timeframe ‘𝑡’, (𝑃 𝑡 𝑃 𝑉 ) can be computed using, 𝑃 𝑡 𝑃 𝑉 = 𝑁𝑖∑ 𝑘=1 𝑃 𝐺𝑃 𝑉 𝑘 × 𝑃𝑖(𝑖𝑡 𝑘) (12) where 𝑁𝑖 denotes the number of discrete solar irradiance phases and 𝑖𝑡 𝑘 is the 𝑘th phase of solar irradiance at 𝑡th hour. the probability of solar irradiance for each phase ‘k’ during any particular hour is estimated using, 𝑃𝑖(𝑖𝑡 𝑘) = ∫ 𝑖𝑡 𝑘,𝑚𝑎𝑥 𝑖𝑡 𝑘,𝑚𝑖𝑛 𝑓 𝑡 𝑖 (𝑖)𝑑 𝑖 (13) the power output of solar pv arrays is influenced by the level of solar irradiance and the ambient temperature at the site where the modules are installed. the generated power by solar pv panels at average solar irradiance (𝑖𝑎𝑘) for the 𝑘th phase can be evaluated as [34] in eq. (14). 𝑃 𝐺𝑃 𝑉 𝑘(𝑖𝑎𝑘) = 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 𝑚𝑜𝑑 × 𝐹 𝐹 × 𝑉𝑘 × 𝐼𝑘 (14) where 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 𝑚𝑜𝑑 is the total pv modules number; 𝐹 𝐹 is the fill factor; 𝑉𝑘, 𝐼𝑘 are the cell voltage and the cell current at the 𝑘th phase, respectively. fig. 2. equivalent two-bus model of the grid. these parameters can be calculated using the following formulas: 𝐹 𝐹 = 𝑉𝑀 𝑀 𝑃 × 𝐼𝑀 𝑀 𝑃 𝑉𝑜𝑐 × 𝐼𝑠𝑐 (15) 𝑉𝑘 = 𝑉𝑜𝑐 − 𝐾𝑣 × 𝑇𝑐 𝑘 (16) 𝐼𝑘 = 𝑖𝑎𝑘[𝐼𝑠𝑐 + 𝐾𝑖(𝑇𝑐 𝑘 − 25)] (17) 𝑇𝑐 𝑘 = 𝑇 𝑡 𝑎 + 𝑖𝑎𝑘 (𝑁𝑂 𝑇 − 20 0.8 ) (18) here, 𝑉𝑀 𝑀 𝑃 and 𝐼𝑀 𝑀 𝑃 are respectively the voltage (𝑉 ) and current (𝐴) at maximum power point; 𝑉𝑜𝑐 and 𝐼𝑠𝑐 are respectively the open circuit voltage (𝑉 ) and the short circuit current (𝐴); 𝑇𝑐 𝑘(◦c) is cell temperature at the 𝑘th phase; 𝐾𝑣 and 𝐾𝑖 are the voltage and current temperature coefficients (𝑉 ∕◦c) and (𝐴∕◦c); 𝑁𝑂 𝑇 is the nominal operat- ing temperature of cell (◦c) and 𝑇 𝑡 𝑎 denotes the hourly average ambient temperature (◦c). 3. problem articulation the problem presented in this paper is to address the challenge of optimizing the locations and sizes of ress (wts and solar pv), alongside the pf of wt, within the rdg. the goal is to achieve optimal grid operation through both separate and simultaneous optimizations. the detailed mathematical formulations of the three objective functions and constraints are presented in the next subsections. 3.1. objective functions the main goal of this optimization problem is to reduce power losses and the voltage deviation, while improve the voltage stability index while adhering to various equality and inequality constraints. 3.2. power loss reduction radial distribution grids suffer from elevated real power losses due to their high line resistance relative to reactance ratios. evaluating and reducing these losses is crucial to enhance the operational efficiency of the grid. fig. 2 represents an equivalent two-bus model of the grid. after power flow calculation, the annual average power loss can be expressed as follows, 𝑃 𝑙 𝑜𝑠𝑠 = ∑𝑁𝑡 𝑡=1 ∑𝑁𝑏 𝑖=1 𝑅𝑏𝑖 ( 𝑉 𝑡 𝑖 −𝑉 𝑡 𝑖+1 𝑅𝑏𝑖+𝑗 𝑋𝑏𝑖 )2 𝑁𝑡 (19) where, 𝑉𝑖 and 𝑉𝑖+1 represent the voltage magnitude at the sending bus 𝑖 and reciving bus 𝑖 + 1 for the 𝑡th hour, respectively. 𝑅𝑏𝑖 and 𝑋𝑏𝑖 are respectively the resistance and reactance of the 𝑖th branch; 𝑁𝑏 denotes the total number of branches and 𝑁𝑡 denotes the total number of time periods (hours). energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 5 b. bendriss et al. fig. 3. simulation flowchart for the proposed method. fig. 4. simulation flow process for addressing the opp of ress into the distribution grid. 3.2.1. voltage deviation minimization voltage deviation (vd) refers to the difference in voltage magnitude at various bus points from a specified reference value (𝑉𝑟𝑒𝑓 ) equal to 1 p.u. minimizing vd is crucial for meeting operational requirements and maintaining high service quality standards. the annual average total vd can be formulated as follows, 𝑉 𝐷 = ∑𝑁𝑡 𝑡=1 ∑𝑁 𝑖=1|𝑉𝑟𝑒𝑓 −|𝑉 𝑡 𝑖 ||2 𝑁𝑡 (20) where, 𝑁 denotes the total number of buses, and 𝑉 𝑡 𝑖 refers to the voltage magnitude at the bus 𝑖 for the 𝑡th hour. 3.2.2. voltage stability index maximization the voltage stability index (vsi) reflects the power system’s capa- bility to maintain steady voltage levels. for stable grid operation, the vsi should be greater than zero across all the buses of a distribution grid. buses with lower vsi values are more susceptible to voltage instability. therefore vsi must be maximized to insure system stability. the mathematical expression of the vsi at the (𝑖 + 1)th bus for a radial distribution grid at the 𝑡th hour is given as follows [39,40]: 𝑉 𝑆 𝐼 𝑡 𝑖+1 = |𝑉 𝑡 𝑖 |4 − 4(𝑃 𝑡 𝑑 𝑖+1𝑅𝑏𝑖 + 𝑄𝑡 𝑑 𝑖+1𝑋𝑏𝑖)|𝑉 𝑡 𝑖 |2 − 4(𝑃 𝑡 𝑑 𝑖+1𝑋𝑏𝑖 − 𝑄𝑡 𝑑 𝑖+1𝑅𝑏𝑖)2 (21) where, 𝑃 𝑡 𝑑 𝑖+1 and 𝑄𝑡 𝑑 𝑖+1 are respectively the demand for active and reactive powers at the (𝑖 + 1)th bus for the 𝑡th hour. the annual average of voltage stability for the entire distribution grid can be expressed as, 𝑉 𝑆 𝐼 = ∑𝑁𝑡 𝑡=1 ∑𝑁−1 𝑖=1 𝑉 𝑆 𝐼 𝑡 𝑖+1 𝑁𝑡 (22) energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 6 b. bendriss et al. fig. 5. seasonal hourly load profile. fig. 6. the studied area location on google maps. fig. 7. probability distribution of wind speed for 22th hour of summer (k=3.4217, c= 9.8850). energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 7 b. bendriss et al. fig. 8. beta distribution of solar irradiance for 14th hour of spring (𝜇=0.4042, 𝜎= 0.1012). fig. 9. seasonal hourly average temperature. 3.2.3. the multi objective function the optimization problem considers the aforementioned objective functions simultaneously (power loss, voltage deviation and voltage stability index). the weighted aggregation approach is used to formu- late the multi-objective function. therefore, the fitness function can be described in the following way, 𝐹 𝑖𝑡 = 𝛿1 × 𝐹1 + 𝛿2 × 𝐹2 + 𝛿3 × 𝐹3 (23) with, 3∑ 𝑖=1 𝛿𝑖 = 1, 𝛿𝑖 ∈ [0, 1] (24) the following formulas are for the normalization of the objective functions 𝐹1 = 𝑃 𝑙 𝑜𝑠𝑠 𝑃 𝑙 𝑜𝑠𝑠𝑏𝑎𝑠𝑒 (25) 𝐹2 = 𝑉 𝐷 𝑉 𝐷𝑏𝑎𝑠𝑒 (26) 𝐹3 = 𝑉 𝑆 𝐼 𝑉 𝑆 𝐼𝑏𝑎𝑠𝑒 (27) where, 𝐹1, 𝐹2, and 𝐹3 denote the normalized objective functions, with 𝛿1, 𝛿2, and 𝛿3 being the weighting factors are selected as 0.5, 0.25, and 0.25, respectively [41]. 3.3. system limitations in addressing the opp of ress, the proposed planning technique completely meets all system limitations, including the equality and inequality constraints, as highlighted below. 3.3.1. equality constraints maintaining a balance between power generation and the total power demand plus losses is essential to prevent reverse power flow, which could damage the system. these balance constraints are ex- pressed as follows: 𝑃 𝑡 sub + 𝑁𝑓∑ 𝑖=1 𝑃 𝑡 wt × 𝑛wt,𝑖 + 𝑁𝑓∑ 𝑖=1 𝑃 𝑡 pvs × 𝑛pvs,𝑖 = 𝑁∑ 𝑗=2 𝑃 𝑡 𝑑 ,𝑗 + 𝑁 𝑏∑ 𝑗=1 𝑃 𝑡 𝑙 𝑜𝑠𝑠,𝑗 (28) 𝑄𝑡 sub + 𝑁𝑓∑ 𝑖=1 𝑄𝑡 wt × 𝑛wt,𝑖 = 𝑁∑ 𝑗=2 𝑄𝑡 𝑑 ,𝑗 + 𝑁 𝑏∑ 𝑗=1 𝑄𝑡 𝑙 𝑜𝑠𝑠,𝑗 (29) energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 8 b. bendriss et al. table 2 the statistical analysis of diverse optimization algorithms on the standard functions. function measure gwo [27] hho [28] cdo [29] doa [30] woa [31] bka [32] flo [33] iflo f1 best average worst sd 1.287e−16 3.442e−15 1.858e−14 3.834e−15 3.766e−75 1.767e−60 5.218e−59 9.522e−60 1.892e−82 8.327e−79 8.524e−78 1.912e−78 4.120e−247 2.167e−28 6.500e−27 1.187e−27 2.197e−54 3.221e−42 4.059e−41 9.726e−42 6.818e−63 7.522e−44 2.256e−42 4.120e−43 5.179e−245 6.963e−227 2.022e−225 0 0 0 0 0 f2 best average worst sd 5.297e−10 1.316e−09 3.509e−09 7.725e−10 2.489e−38 7.004e−33 7.354e−32 1.650e−32 1.189e−43 6.960e−41 4.876e−40 1.070e−40 1.376e−124 3.737e−23 1.111e−21 2.028e−22 3.446e−35 9.276e−30 1.942e−28 3.561e−29 3.106e−31 1.741e−28 1.143e−27 3.153e−28 1.549e−122 7.132e−114 2.118e−112 3.865e−113 0 0 0 0 f3 best average worst sd 5.384e−04 8.805e−02 4.287e−01 1.086e−01 1.232e−63 1.089e−45 2.028e−44 4.235e−45 6.503e−71 3.142e−54 9.421e−53 1.720e−53 2.714e−289 8.763e−36 2.629e−34 4.800e−35 2.866e+04 6.282e+04 1.089e+05 1.963e+04 2.358e−62 3.884e−49 7.588e−48 1.549e−48 3.595e−212 1.374e−162 4.121e−161 7.372e−162 0 0 0 0 f4 best average wors