f of each wind farm is treated as an adjustable parameter within the optimization process, as it allows for reactive power support when required. it is constrained within the following range: 𝑃 𝐹𝑚𝑖𝑛 ≤ 𝑃 𝐹𝑖 ≤ 𝑃 𝐹𝑚𝑎𝑥 𝑖 = 1, 2, … , 𝑁𝑓 (33) thus, the corresponding reactive power supplied by each wind farm at each hour ‘𝑡’ is expressed as a function of active power and pf as follows: 𝑄𝑡 wt × 𝑛wt,𝑖 = 𝑃 𝑡 wt × 𝑛wt,𝑖 × t an (𝑐 𝑜𝑠−1(𝑃 𝐹𝑖)) 𝑖 = 1, 2, … , 𝑁𝑓 (34) in contrast, pv systems are assumed to operate at a unity power factor (pf = 1), as they are typically configured to generate only active power without injecting or absorbing reactive power. • thermal limit : to maintain line security, the current flowing through branch 𝑖 at the 𝑡th hour, symbolized as 𝐼 𝑡 𝑏𝑖, must remain below its specified maximum limit, as follows: 𝐼 𝑡 𝑏𝑖 ≤ 𝐼 𝑚𝑎𝑥 𝑏𝑖 𝑖 = 1, 2, … , 𝑁𝑏 (35) where 𝐼 𝑚𝑎𝑥 𝑏𝑖 signifies the maximum current carrying capacity of the 𝑖th branch. 4. frilled lizard optimization algorithm the flo is a bio-inspired algorithm that simulates the frilled lizard’s hunting strategy. this lizard waits patiently for prey, then swiftly attacks and feeds on it before retreating up a tree. flo mimics this behavior by exploring the solution space, rapidly refining candidate solutions, and strategically converging to optimal results [33]. 4.1. initialization flo begins by creating a set of random solutions within the search space, where each frilled lizard represents a potential solution and is formulated as a vector, initialized using eq. (35). collectively, the flo population can be expressed in the form of a matrix x, as shown in eq. ( 36). during each iteration of the optimization process, the algorithm evaluates the fitness of each solution and identifies the best candidate among them. 𝑥𝑖,𝑘 = 𝐿𝑏 + 𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑 × (𝑈 𝐵 − 𝐿𝐵) 𝑘 = 1, 2, … , 𝑑 𝑖𝑚. (36) 𝑋 = ⎡ ⎢ ⎢ ⎢ ⎢ ⎢⎣ 𝑋1 ⋮ 𝑋𝑖 ⋮ 𝑋𝑛 ⎤ ⎥ ⎥ ⎥ ⎥ ⎥⎦ = ⎡ ⎢ ⎢ ⎢ ⎢ ⎢⎣ 𝑥1,1 ⋯ 𝑥1,𝑘 ⋯ 𝑥1,𝑑 𝑖𝑚 ⋮ ⋱ ⋮ ⋱ ⋮ 𝑥𝑖,1 ⋯ 𝑥𝑖,𝑘 ⋯ 𝑥𝑖,𝑑 𝑖𝑚 ⋮ ⋱ ⋮ ⋱ ⋮ 𝑥𝑛,1 ⋯ 𝑥𝑛,𝑘 ⋯ 𝑥𝑛,𝑑 𝑖𝑚 ⎤ ⎥ ⎥ ⎥ ⎥ ⎥⎦ (37) energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 10 b. bendriss et al. fig. 10. convergence curves comparison. here, 𝑋 represents the flo population matrix, while 𝑋𝑖 denotes the 𝑖th candidate solution. 𝑥𝑖,𝑘 represents the 𝑘th decision variable at the 𝑖th candidate solution, 𝑑 𝑖𝑚 refers the problem dimension, and 𝑛 denotes the total number of frilled lizards. 4.2. hunting strategy: exploration phase one distinctive natural behavior of the frilled lizard is its hunting strategy as a sit-and-wait predator, attacking prey upon sight. simu- lating this movement leads to significant shifts in population positions within the problem-solving space, enhancing the algorithm’s global search exploration. in the initial phase of flo, the population positions are updated based on this strategy. each frilled lizard considers the positions of other members with better fitness values as prey positions, determined by eq. (37) [33]. 𝑃𝑖 = {𝑋𝑗 ∶ 𝐹𝑗 < 𝐹𝑖 and 𝑗 ≠ 𝑖}, 𝑖 = 1, 2, … , 𝑛 and 𝑗 ∈ {1, 2, … , 𝑛} (38) here, p denotes the group of potential prey for the 𝑖th frilled lizard, where 𝑋𝑗 represents a member of the population with a better fit- ness value compared to the 𝑖th frilled lizard, and 𝐹𝑗 represents the corresponding objective function value. the flo design assumes that the frilled lizard randomly selects one of the potential prey and then attacks it. using the modeled movement of the lizard toward the chosen prey, a new position for each member of the population is determined based on eq. (38). if this updated position results in a better fitness value, it replaces the previous position of the corresponding individual as per eq. (39) [33]: 𝑥𝑃 1 𝑖,𝑘 = 𝑥𝑖,𝑘 + 𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑 × (𝑆 𝑃𝑖,𝑘 − 𝐼 ⋅ 𝑥𝑖,𝑘), 𝑖 = 1, 2, … , 𝑛, and 𝑘 = 1, 2, … , 𝑑 𝑖𝑚 (39) 𝑋𝑖 = { 𝑋𝑃 1 𝑖 , 𝐹 𝑃 1 𝑖 < 𝐹𝑖 𝑋𝑖, else (40) energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 11 b. bendriss et al. fig. 11. boxplots comparison. where, 𝑋𝑃 1 𝑖 symbolizes the newly proposed position of the 𝑖th frilled lizard based on the initial phase of flo. the term 𝑥𝑃 1 𝑖,𝑘 represents its 𝑘th dimension, while 𝐹 𝑃 1 𝑖 indicates its objective function value. 𝑆 𝑃𝑖,𝑘 is the 𝑘th dimension of the chosen prey for the 𝑖th frilled lizard, and 𝐼 is a random number from the set {1, 2}. 4.3. moving up the tree: exploitation phase once it finishes feeding, the frilled lizard ascends to the top of a nearby tree. this behavior, when simulated, leads to slight adjustments in the individuals’ positions within the search area, thereby boosting the local search capabilities of the algorithm. during the second phase of the flo algorithm, the positions of individuals are updated according to the frilled lizard’s retreat strategy. by modeling the frilled lizard’s movement, a new position for each individual is computed according to eq. (40). if this updated position enhances the objective function, it replaces the old position, as described in eq. (41) [33]. 𝑥𝑃 2 𝑖,𝑘 = 𝑥𝑖,𝑘 + (1 − 2𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑) ⋅ (𝑈 𝐵𝑘 − 𝐿𝐵𝑘) 𝑡 , 𝑖 = 1, 2, … , 𝑛, 𝑘 = 1, 2, … , 𝑑 𝑖𝑚, and 𝑡 = 1, 2, … , 𝑇 (41) 𝑋𝑖 = { 𝑋𝑃 2 𝑖 , if 𝐹 𝑃 2 𝑖 < 𝐹𝑖 𝑋𝑖, else (42) energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 12 b. bendriss et al. fig. 12. schematic diagram of the 85-bus rdg. fig. 13. the seasonal hourly power produced by wind farms. here, 𝑋𝑃 2 𝑖 refers to the newly proposed position of the 𝑖th frilled lizard according to the second stage of flo, 𝑥𝑃 2 𝑖,𝑘 indicates its 𝑑th dimension, 𝐹 𝑃 2 𝑖 provides its objective function value, 𝑡 denotes the current iteration number, and 𝑇 specifies the maximum number of iterations. 5. improved frilled lizard optimization algorithm the suggested improved frilled lizard optimization (iflo) algo- rithm incorporates three distinct strategies: fitness distance balance (fdb), quasi-opposite-based learning (qobl), and cauchy mutation (cm), each contributing uniquely to improving the algorithm’s perfor- mance. 5.1. fitness distance balance (fdb) the fdb method is an efficient selection strategy in optimization algorithms that improves the exploration phase by evaluating candi- date solutions according to their fitness and proximity to the optimal solution [42–44]. fdb prioritizes solutions with superior fitness values energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 13 b. bendriss et al. fig. 14. the seasonal hourly power produced by solar pv farms. fig. 15. seasonal voltage profiles for the 85-bus distribution grid without ress integration. and minimal distances to the optimal solution, ensuring the algorithm effectively explores new areas while refining promising regions. the distance of the solution candidates (𝐷 𝑃𝑖) from the best solution (𝑃𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡) is computed as follows: 𝐷 𝑃𝑖 = √√√√ 𝑑 𝑖𝑚∑ 𝑘=1 (𝑥𝑖,𝑘 − 𝑃𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡,𝑘)2, ∀𝑖 ∈ {1, 2, … , 𝑁}, 𝑃𝑖 ≠ 𝑃best (43) then, create the vectors for both fitness and distance values in the following way: 𝐷 𝑃 = [𝐷 𝑃1, 𝐷 𝑃2, … 𝐷 𝑃𝑁 ] (44) 𝐹 = [𝐹1, 𝐹2, … 𝐹𝑁 ] (45) in the last stage of the fdb method, the score is figured using the following formula: 𝑆𝐹 𝐷 𝐵 𝑖 = 𝜔 × (1 − 𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚𝐹𝑖) + (1 − 𝜔) × 𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚𝐷 𝑃𝑖 (46) where, 𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚𝐹𝑖 and 𝑛𝑜𝑟𝑚𝐷 𝑃𝑖 are respectively the normalized fitness and distance values. 𝜔 is a weight coefficient that also defines the influence of fitness and distance values and can be described as: 𝜔 = 0.5 × ( 1 + 𝑡 𝑇 ) (47) 5.2. quasi-opposite-based learning (qobl) qobl is an advanced strategy derived from oppositional based learning, aiming to improve the exploration and exploitation capabil- ities of optimization algorithms [44,45]. by evaluating both current solutions and their quasi-opposite counterparts, it explores the search process more effectively, leading to better convergence to the global op- timum. in qobl, the opposite of a candidate solution 𝑋(𝑥1, 𝑥2, … 𝑥𝑑 𝑖𝑚), where 𝑥𝑘 ∈ [𝐿𝐵𝑘, 𝑈 𝐵𝑘] ∀𝑘 ∈ {1, 2, … , 𝑑 𝑖𝑚} is defined as 𝑌 (𝑦1, 𝑦2, … 𝑦𝑑 𝑖𝑚). each opposite point 𝑦𝑘 is calculated as 𝑦𝑘 = 𝐿𝐵𝑘 + 𝑈 𝐵𝑘 − 𝑥𝑘 and energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 14 b. bendriss et al. fig. 16. seasonal voltage profiles for the 85-bus distribution grid in the presence of ress. the midpoint is obtained as : 𝑀𝑘 = 𝐿𝐵𝑘 + 𝑈 𝐵𝑘 2 (48) to generate a quasi-opposite point 𝑦𝑝 𝑘, which is more likely to be nearer to the optimal solution than the opposite point 𝑦𝑘, we use the following formula: 𝑦𝑞 𝑘 = { 𝑀𝑘 + (𝑦𝑘 − 𝑀𝑘) × 𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑 if 𝑦𝑘 < 𝑀𝑘 𝑦𝑘 + (𝑀𝑘 − 𝑦𝑘) × 𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑 otherwise (49) where 𝑟𝑎𝑛𝑑 is the uniformly distributed random number in the range of [0, 1]. 5.3. cauchy mutation (cm) cm is incorporated to enhance flo’s ability to explore the solution space more thoroughly. this strategy involves injecting significant variability into the population using a cm operator, enabling the algo- rithm to jump out of the local optima and thereby preventing it from falling into the local optima trap [46–48]. the cauchy distribution is a continuous probability distribution defined by two parameters (𝜎 , 𝜇). the formula for one-dimensional cauchy probability density function is presented in eq. (49): 𝑓 (𝑧; 𝜎 , 𝜇) = 1 𝜋 𝜎 𝜎2 + (𝑧 − 𝜇)2 , 𝑧 ∈ (−∞, ∞), (50) when 𝜎 = 1 and 𝜇 = 0, the cauchy density function simplifies to its standard form, defined by eq. (50): 𝑓 (𝑥; 1, 0) = 1 𝜋(𝑧2 + 1) , 𝑧 ∈ (−∞, ∞), (51) the formula for the standard cauchy distribution is represented by eq. (51): cauchy(0, 1) = t an [𝜋 (𝜉 − 0.5)] , 𝜉 ∈ [0, 1]. (52) eq. (52) is utilized for cm processing on randomly generated new promising positions. 𝑋𝑡+1 𝑖 = 𝑋𝑡 𝑖 + 𝛼 ⊗ cauchy(0, 1) ⊗ (𝑋𝑡 𝑖 − 𝑋𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡) (53) where 𝛼 denotes to the step size while ⊗ represent entrywise multipli- cation. fig. 3 depicts a flowchart of the proposed iflo algorithm to the opp solution, while the methodology of the suggested framework is explained in fig. 4. 6. data analysis analyzing weather data at the specified location, along with load patterns, is crucial as an initial step for solving the opp of ress into the distribution grid. the system being studied adheres to the time-varying load curve of the ieee-rts system [34,35]. accordingly, the seasonal hourly load profile is presented in fig. 5 as a percentage of the annual peak load. historical data on wind speed, solar irradiance, and ambient tem- perature was collected from in salah region, a province located in central algeria, in the sahara, over a decade (from 01 january 2012 to 31 december 2021). the data are available on the nasa website [49]. fig. 6 shows the precise location of in salah province. each year within this study period is divided into four seasons: summer (may to july), autumn (august to october), winter (november to january), and spring (february to april). each season is represented by a single day of 24 h, representing the hourly random behavior of ress during that season. consequently, four seasonal days represent one year, totaling 96 h (24 h × 4 seasons). weibull and beta pdfs are then generated for each hour to accurately fit the hourly seasonal wind speed and solar irradiance, respectively. the adjustment steps are set at 1 m/s for wind speed and 0.05 kw/m2 for solar irradiance. for clarification, the probability distribution of wind speed for the 22th hour in summer and solar irradiance for the 14th in spring are represented in figs. 7 and 8, respectively. the ambient temperature is represented by averaging the hourly temperature values for each season. thus, the seasonal hourly average temperatures are depicted in fig. 9. 7. simulation results and discussions the suggested iflo algorithm is designed to solve the opp of ress in the 85-bus rdg [50]. initially, the performance of the proposed algorithm was evaluated through a comparison with several established optimization methods, including the well known gwo [27], hho [28], cdo [29], doa [30], woa [31], bka [32], and the original flo [33]. the simulation was conducted using matlab r2021a on a computer equipped with an intel(r) core(tm) i5-7200u cpu @ 2.50 ghz and energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 15 b. bendriss et al. fig. 17. seasonal power losses for the 85-bus rdg without and with the integration of ress. fig. 18. minimum vsi recorded at prior and after the integration of ress for each season and hour. 2.71 ghz, 8.00 gb of ram, and a 64-bit operating system. the cases covered by the study are detailed as follows: 7.1. performance assessment of the iflo algorithm on standard benchmark functions the proposed iflo technique was applied to 23 standard bench- mark functions. these functions encompass unimodal, multimodal, and fixed-dimension multimodal benchmarks, as detailed in appendix [51– 53]. the parameter settings for the different algorithms are provided in table 3, and the results were recorded over 30 trial runs. 7.1.1. analyzing the statistical results here, the performance of the suggested iflo algorithm was com- pared with other optimization methods, including the gwo [27], hho [28], cdo [29], doa [30], woa [31], bka [32], and the original flo [33]. table 2 presents the statistical results, encompassing the best, average, worst, and standard deviation (sd) of all methods. from the table, it can be observed that the proposed iflo outperforms other methods in terms of best, average, worst, and sd for the overwhelming majority of the test functions. this confirms that the proposed iflo table 3 setting parameters of each algorithm. algorithm parameters for standard benchmark functions gwo [27] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝑎=[2,0] hho [28] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝐸𝑜=[−1,1] cdo [29] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝑟=rand[0,1] doa [30] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝑃 = 0.5, 𝑄=0.7, 𝛽1=[−2,2], 𝛽2= [−1,1] woa [31] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝑏=1, 𝑙=[−1,1], 𝑎1 = 𝑎2=[2,0], bka [32] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝑝 = 0.9 flo [33] population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝐼= rand[1,2] iflo population size=30, 𝑇𝑚𝑎𝑥=30, 𝐼= rand[1,2] exhibits a higher degree of superiority in finding the best solutions, as compared to the other optimizers. 7.1.2. convergence curves analysis the convergence graphs of the proposed iflo, along with those of other reported techniques, including gwo [27], hho [28], cdo [29], doa [30], woa [31], bka [32], and the original flo [33], are energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 16 b. bendriss et al. fig. 19. convergence curves of different methods addressing the opp of ress in the 85-bus rdg. shown in fig. 10. according to the convergence curves, the proposed iflo demonstrates faster convergence for unimodal, multimodal, and fixed-dimension multimodal functions. furthermore, the proposed iflo outperforms the conventional flo, converging to the optimal solu- tion more rapidly. this enhanced performance is due to the proposed modifications, which improve both the exploration and exploitation phases of the optimizer, enabling it to reach the optimal solution more efficiently. 7.1.3. boxplot analysis boxplots are a powerful tool for visualizing data distributions by quartiles, optimally highlighting the key characteristics of the data. in the context of our study, fig. 11 displays boxplots comparing the perfor- mance of the proposed iflo algorithm against other reported optimiz- ers, namely gwo [27], hho [28], cdo [29], doa [30], woa [31], bka [32], and the original flo [33]. these boxplots summarize the distribution of performance across multiple simulation runs using 23 benchmark functions. the narrower boxplot represents the reduced variance in the performance of the iflo algorithm, meaning it is less prone to outliers and produces results that are more consistent in terms of solution quality and convergence. on the other hand, the wider box- plots for the other algorithms in specific benchmark functions indicate greater variability in performance, suggesting that these algorithms are less consistent in achieving the optimization objectives. the presence of outliers in the compared algorithms boxplots further emphasizes that these methods may occasionally perform poorly in certain scenarios. hence, the iflo algorithm outperforms the other methods in terms of consistency, demonstrating its robustness and reliability across multiple simulation scenarios. 7.2. application of the iflo for solving the opp of ress into the 85-bus distribution grid in this portion, the suggested iflo technique is implemented on the 85-bus rdg, employing the developed model serving as input data to address the opp of ress. fig. 12 illustrates the single-line diagram of the ieee 85-bus rdg of 11 kv. the system comprises 85 buses and 84 branches, having a combined peak load of 2570.28 kw and 2622.08 kvar. the complete technical descriptions are sourced from [50]. the load flow solution is conducted using the backward/forward sweep method [54]. this research considers the integration of two wind farms and two solar farms into the rdg. the wts used in this study have a peak capacity of 250 kw. additionally, each pv string is configured table 4 parameter values specification of pv and wt unit. parameters value pv unit current at maximum power point, 𝐼mpp 7.76 a voltage at maximum power point, 𝑉mpp 28.36 v short circuit current, 𝐼sc 8.38 a open circuit voltage, 𝑉oc 36.96 v nominal cell operating temperature, 𝑁ot 49 ◦c current temperature co-efficient, 𝐾𝑖 0.00545 a/◦c voltage temperature co-efficient, 𝐾𝑣 0.1278 v/◦c wt unit rated output power, 𝑃rated 250 kw cut-in speed, 𝑤in 3 m/s rated speed, 𝑤n 12 m/s cut-out speed, 𝑤out 25 m/s table 5 parameters values limitations. parameters value number of population 30 maximum iterations 50 voltage boundaries [24] 0.9 𝑝.𝑢 ≤ 𝑉 𝑡 𝑖 ≤ 1.05 𝑝.𝑢 size of pv strings 0 ≤ 𝑃 𝑉 ≤ 584 strings size of wt units 0 ≤ 𝑊 𝑇 ≤ 10 turbines pv system power factor 1 wt power factor 0.7 ≤ 𝑃 𝐹 ≤ 1 with 20 pv panels, producing a total rated power of 4.4 kw per string. table 4 offers the characteristics of the pv module and wt [18]. to ensure a fair comparison, the population sizes and the maximum number of iterations were fixed to 30 and 50, respectively, as outlined in table 5, which also lists the operational system constraints. the effectiveness of the proposed iflo algorithm is proved through a comparative study against other recent algorithms, namely gwo, bka, and the original flo. these algorithms were also developed using the same dataset, ensuring a fair and consistent comparison in performance across the different methods. the uncertainties related to wts and solar pvs, along with load fluctuations, are addressed in section 2 within the context of the developed model through a seasonal planning horizon. table 6 presents the energy management results for the 85-bus system obtained using different algorithms before and after the instal- lation of res units. it provides insights into how wts and pv strings can enhance system performance. the annual average power losses, voltage deviation, and voltage stability index in the base case without any ress integrated into the rdg were 118.2953 kw, 0.30662 p.u., and 0.7355 p.u., respectively. whereas, after the installation of res units, the proposed iflo algorithm successfully selects appropriate control parameters for wts and pv strings. this reduces the annual average power losses from 118.2953 kw to 35.1939 kw (reduction of 70.25%) and the voltage deviation from 0.30662 p.u. to 0.0659 p.u. (improvement of 78.51%), while also enhancing the annual average voltage stability index, rising from 0.7355 p.u. to 0.8884 p.u. (a gain of 20.79%). the optimal locations for wt farms are identified as bus 35 and bus 68, with 5 and 3 installed wt units, respectively. likewise, the optimal locations for pv farms are identified as bus 52 and bus 70, with 74 and 55 pv strings, respectively. the corresponding optimal wt ratings are 1250 kw and 750 kw, while the optimal pv ratings are 326 kw and 242 kw, respectively. according to the obtained results in table 6, it is clear that the proposed iflo algorithm outperforms other methods, including gwo, bka, and the original flo, in achieving optimal results. figs. 13 and 14 display the predicted seasonal power generation from each wt and solar pv farm. these figures demonstrate that the power output from wts and solar pvs varies throughout the year due to energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 17 b. bendriss et al. table 6 energy management results for opp of ress into 85-bus distribution grid. factor without ress with the installation of ress gwo bka flo iflo res parameters optimal location of wts – 63 34 34 68 35 64 35 68 optimal number of installed wt units – 1 7 5 3 4 3 5 3 optimal rated power of wts (kw) – 250 1750 1250 750 1000 750 1250 750 optimal pf of wts (kw) – 1 0.7000 0.7068 0.7005 0.7264 0.7066 0.7000 0.7000 optimal location of pvs – 46 29 42 51 45 48 52 70 optimal number of pv strings – 25 84 114 12 83 103 74 55 rated power of pvs (kw) – 110 370 502 53 365 453 326 242 system performance metrics annual average power loss (kw) 118.2953 46.0170 35.6760 40.0428 35.1939 annual average vd (p.u) 0.30662 0.0790 0.0688 0.0753 0.0659 annual average vsi (p.u) 1.3744 1.1757 1.1359 1.1515 1.1315 annual average min vsi (p.u) 0.7355 0.8566 0.8856 0.8737 0.8884 annual average min voltage (p.u) 0.9252 0.9614 0.9696 0.9663 0.9704 annual average max voltage (p.u) 1 1.0037 1.0016 1.0007 1.0022 notation: bold values indicate the best obtained solutions. table 7 performance comparison of algorithms. algorithm best average worst sd gwo 0.47277 0.48293 0.50171 0.008884 bka 0.41347 0.425872 0.44654 0.012735 flo 0.44013 0.45965 0.47262 0.010950 iflo 0.40834 0.40960 0.41207 0.001232 notation: bold values indicate the best obtained solutions. changes in uncertain factors such as wind speed, solar irradiance, and temperature. figs. 15 and 16 display the system voltage profiles of both with and without integration of res. it is obvious from these figures that the voltage profile significantly improved with the incorporation of wt units and solar pv strings. furthermore, as depicted in fig. 17, the integration of res has notably reduced power losses. this reduction is evident across seasons of the year (96-hour period), especially during peak hours, highlighting the positive impact of res on improving system efficiency and minimizing energy losses. additionally, fig. 18 illustrates the effect of res integration on system voltage stability. the figure clearly shows a marked enhancement in voltage stability throughout seasons of the year, further underscoring the benefits of res in maintaining stable and reliable voltage levels. table 7 presents a performance comparison between the proposed iflo algorithm and other optimization techniques, including gwo, bka, and the original flo. the results clearly show that the proposed approach surpasses other methods in terms of mean, best, and worst values, emphasizing its robustness and superior performance in solving the opp for ress. additionally, fig. 19 illustrates the convergence curves for the opp of ress into the rdg, where iflo demonstrates a faster convergence rate and achieves a lower fitness value compared to the other techniques. this superior performance is due to the improved strategies incorporated into the proposed algorithm. 8. conclusion this research addressed the complex challenge of the optimal plan- ning problem of ress, specifically wt and pv farms within the dis- tribution grids. our primary goal was to enhance system performance by minimizing power losses and voltage deviations while improving the voltage stability index, across the entire system. we proposed a novel improved flo algorithm (iflo) specifically designed to solve the presented problem. the iflo incorporates three distinct strategies, including fdb, qobl and cm to enhance its searching capabilities and avoid it getting trapped in local optima. we tested the iflo on 23 different functions and compared it to other effective optimization algorithms, such as gwo, hho, cdo, doa, woa, bka, and the original flo. the results show that iflo achieves superior performance compared to conventional algorithms. the optimal locations and rating capacities of pv strings and wts, along with the optimal pf of wts, were determined simultaneously. the planning process accounted for seasonal uncertainties in wind speed, solar irradiance, and temperature throughout the year, alongside the seasonal fluctuation in load demand, all within a time-dependent generation-load model. the proposed ap- proach effectively solved the opp of ress into the 85-bus distribution grid, reducing power losses, elevating the voltage profile, and improv- ing grid voltage stability. specifically, iflo reduces annual average power losses by 70.25%, decreases voltage deviation by 78.51%, and enhances the voltage stability index by 20.79% compared to the base case, outperforming other recent optimization techniques such as gwo, bka and the original flo. these results highlight the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed method in addressing the complex challenges of res planning within the distribution grid, making it a valuable and efficient tool for future applications in modern grid planning. future research could explore more complex integrations, such as electric vehicle (ev) charging stations alongside ress in larger distribution grids. we aim to expand the current framework by incorpo- rating additional emerging factors such as cost-effectiveness, resilience under extreme scenarios, and grid capacity expansion. besides, we plan energy conversion and management 326 (2025) 119465 18 b. bendriss et al. to explore and investigate incorporating advanced probabilistic models, such as fuzzy cloud theory and interval analysis, to handle uncertainty effectively. credit authorship contribution statement badreddine bendriss: writing – original draft, software, data cu- ration. samir sayah: writing – original draft, validation, supervision, resources, methodology. abdellatif hamouda: writing – review & editing, validation, supervision, methodology. declaration of competing interest the authors declare that they have no known competing finan- cial interests or personal",no
