Zhang20203731,2020,Two-Stage Optimal Operation Strategy of Isolated Microgrid with TSK Fuzzy Identification of Supply Security,yes,yes,RBF / kernel,high,47,PCE / RSM,0,abstract/keywords,"on isolated power system, it creates a supply-security identification model with the tsk fuzzy approach under radial basis function (rbf) neural network, and deduces optimal weight values with a recursive least square method. for properly avoiding potential risks, security index i",Transfer learning,high,8,Latin hypercube sampling,0,pdf,"dentiﬁcation of dynamic systems. a knowledge-leverage-based fuzzy system is discussed from the perspective of transfer learning in [21], which not only make full use of data from the current scene, but also effectively leverage the existing knowledge from reference scenes. in",P1 replacement,high,manual,,0,PDF,p. 11: The TSK fuzzy approach identifies the relationship between uncertainty parameters and supply security before the second-stage economic dispatch optimization.,Feasibility,high,8,_other,0,pdf,"2: initialize population set q and archive set b 3: g = 0, b = ∅, size = 0 4: while g<g max do 5: check total constraint violation 6: if violation > ϵ then 7: embedded constraint handling strategy 8: end if 9: gd based mutation operator 10: crossover operator 11: selection operat",yes
Pourmohammadi2023,2023,Robust metamodel-based simulation-optimization approaches for designing hybrid renewable energy systems,yes,yes,PCE / RSM,high,41,GP / kriging,11,abstract/keywords,"the stochastic system do not have closed-form expressions, they can only be estimated via simulation. in the response surface methodology, the simulation input/output data is used first to locally fit first-degree surrogate functions for each supply scenario to perform a stochastic grad",Factorial / response-surface design,high,46,Historical operational data,10,abstract/keywords,"the stochastic system do not have closed-form expressions, they can only be estimated via simulation. in the response surface methodology, the simulation input/output data is used first to locally fit first-degree surrogate functions for each supply scenario to perform a stochastic grad",P5 uncertainty,high,36,P1 replacement,0,abstract/keywords,"the response surface methodology, the simulation input/output data is used first to locally fit first-degree surrogate functions for each supply scenario to perform a stochastic gradient descent, before a second-degree function is fitted and optimized when the gradient becomes small. in contrast, the global response surface technique globally fits a convex quadratic surrogate function on the simulation input/output data, then uses its minimizer to iteratively refine the search space. to account for estimation errors, a distributional ambiguity set based on a linear ϕ-divergence function is constructed around the nominal probability distribution of supply scenarios, and the expected reliability-adjusted cost of the system is computed using the worst-case distribution in this set. the validity of the proposed approaches and the value of considering distributional ambiguity are demonstrated through a realistic case study of a remote community in northern ontario. © 2023 elsevier ltd ontario [canada]; digital storage; gradient methods; optimization; probability distributions; renewable energy resources; surface properties; distributionally robust optimization; energy systems; hybrid renewable energies; hybrid renewable energy system; input/output datum; meta model; response-surface methodology; robust optimization; simulation optimization; surrogate function; alternative energy; hybrid; optimization; probability; response surface methodology; simulation; stocha",Problem UQ,high,8,_other,0,pdf,"ors, a distributional ambiguity set based on a linear 𝜙-divergence function is constructed around the nominal probability distribution of supply scenarios, and the expected reliability-adjusted cost of the system is computed using the worst-case distribution in this set. the validity of the proposed approaches and the value of considering distributional ambiguity are demonstrated through a realistic case study of a remote community in northern ontario. 1. introduction with the steady growth in global energy demand and the rising concerns over environmental change, pollution, and depletion of fossil energy resources, renewable energy sources (res) like solar and wind are posed to play a key role in our transition to a sustainable econ- omy [1,2]. stand-alone hybrid renewable energy systems (hres) that combine locally available res with conventional energy generation and/or energy storage technologies constitute a viable alternative for serving the energy requirements of remote and isolated communities when their connection to centralized power grids is not technically and/or economically feasible [ 3]. however, given the intermittent nature of res and the high cost of large-scale energy storage, it is crucial that the design of hres is optimized so they can deliver energy economically and reliably. therefore, the problem of optimal design of hres has received a considerable attention from the scientific community in the last two decades [ 4–8]. simulation–optimization (so) is a widely-used method for hres planning [9]. so is particularly suitable when the problem’s objective and/or constraint functions are difficult to express explicitly (analyti- cally), but can be evaluated in a black-box model through simulation for a particular input [ 10]. generally speaking, so approaches can be categorized into model-based and metamodel-based methods [ 11]. ∗ corresponding author. e-mail addresses: pardis.pourmohammadi@dal.ca (p. pourmohammadi), ahmed.saif@dal.ca (a. saif). model-based so encompasses ranking and selection, gradient search methods ( i.e., stochastic optimization), direct (derivative-free) search methods and random/heuristic search methods like genetic algorithms (ga), simulated annealing (sa) and tabu search (ts) [ 10]. on the other hand, metamodel-based or surrogate models approximate the unknown input/output relationship of the system using an easy-to- optimize function [12]. response surface, kriging and neural network methods are among the most widely-used metamodels [ 13]. given the stochastic, nonlinear and dynamic nature of hres op- erations, metamodel-based so represents a promising framework for planning them [ 14]. however, the literature on hres design using metamodels in general, and response surface methods (rsm) in par- ticular, is still sparse. ekren and ekren [ 15] used rsm to optimally size an autonomous solar/wind hres with battery storage by min- imizing the system cost and loss of load probability (llp). subse- quently, ekren et al. [ 16] conducted a break-even analysis and com- pared a grid-connected system to a stand-alone system in terms of their net present values using a similar rsm. ren et al. [ 17] presented a simulation-based strategy for optimal design of a renewable cooling and heating system, and devised a solution approach that integrates an integer-based ga with rsm. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apenergy.2023.121132 received 17 january 2023; received in revised form 16 march 2023; accepted 8 april 2023 applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 2 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif a key limitation of so methods in the context of stochastic systems is that they require the probability distribution (or the underlying data- generating process) of the simulation inputs to be known with certainty, otherwise the solutions obtained might be sub-optimal [18]. in this sense, they are quite similar to sample average approximation (saa) methods that are known to have poor out-of-sample performance, especially when based on small data samples [19], which is often the case in hres planning. to reduce the sensitivity of so models to the simulation parameters and to hedge against uncertainty about them, a robust approach was investigated by dellino et al. [18,20]. their proposed approach adopts taguchi’s view of the uncertain world, but replaces its statistical techniques to tackle robustness using metamodels by kriging and rsm. a less-conservative approach for dealing with parameter uncertainty that has gained significant interest recently is distributionally-robust optimization (dro) [21]. in dro, the expected value of a probabilistic cost function is optimized, where the expectation is taken with respect to the worst-case probability distribution from a distributional ambigu- ity set (das) that is constructed based on historical data or parametric information about the ‘‘true’’ distribution. although dro has found application in some energy optimization studies [22–24], dro models used so far are based on explicit formulations that require the objective and constraint functions to be fully known, which is not the case in hres design problems. although so is the most commonly-applied approach to design hres, the vast majority of so models use metaheuristics that often fall into local optima and require excessive numbers of simulation evalua- tions [25,26]. metamodel-based approaches are shown to outperform metaheuristics, especially for complex problems [27]. however, among the handful of studies that apply metamodel-based so in the context of hres design, e.g., [15,17], the issue of distributional ambiguity, arising when few supply and demand scenarios extracted from limited data are used in the simulation, is largely ignored, leading to hres designs that perform sub-optimally in reality. on the other hand, classical dro models that can alleviate this issue are based on canonical-form optimization ( i.e., explicit objective function and constraints) and are not suited for so methods. the current paper fills this research gap by showing how metamodel-based so methods can be embedded in a dro framework to provide robust hres designs that significantly outper- form designs based on classical methods that directly use the empirical probability distribution of scenarios. in particular, the contribution of this paper is twofold. first, it provides novel and generic design approaches that combine rsm and dro to overcome the limitations of existing methods. second, it demonstrates the superiority of the robust designs obtained using the proposed approaches through extensive numerical analysis. we propose two novel approaches for robustly optimizing the design of hres under ambiguity about the probability distribution of solar and wind energy supply scenarios. since the objective function can be evaluated only through simulation, response surface metamodels are fitted on the input/output (i/o) simulation data using design of experiments (doe) techniques. the first approach uses a classical rsm that locally approximates the response function and uses gradient infor- mation and a line search to update the best design, before performing and optimizing a second-order approximation in the last iteration. the second approach, in contrast, globally approximates the response function and optimizes the convex quadratic approximation by solving a dro problem to refine the search space in each iteration until the algorithm converges to an optimal design. to model uncertainty in both cases, first, a manageable number of solar and wind supply scenarios are generated from raw data using 𝑘-means clustering, and their probabilities are estimated and used to generate an empirical distribution. then, a 𝜙-divergence ambiguity set is constructed around the empirical distribution of scenarios to account for estimation errors. the two proposed approaches are applied to a hypothetical (yet re- alistic) case study that entails designing a solar–wind–battery–diesel hres for an isolated community in canada. comparisons between the two approaches are conducted for both the nominal and the robust problems. the remainder of this paper is organized as follows. the next section describes the hres to be optimized and presents the mathematical formulation of its optimal design problem. section 3 presents the 𝑘- means clustering method used to extract supply scenarios and the 𝜙-divergence ambiguity set constructed based on their empirical dis- tribution. section 4 explains, in detail, the two approaches proposed to robustly designing a hres: the classical rsm and the global response surface technique (grst). the regression models used to fit the i/o data in each approach are presented in the same section, along with the way distributional ambiguity is incorporated into the design process. in section 5, the numerical results obtained from applying the proposed approaches on a realistic case study are compared and thoroughly an- alyzed. finally, conclusions and ideas for future research are provided in section 6. 2. system description and problem formulation this section provides a brief description of the system under con- sideration, including its operational rules. then, it presents the no- tations used to formulate the hres design problem, along with the mathematical formulation of the objective functions and constraints. 2.1. the hres and its operational rules the hres of interest consists of photovoltaic panels (pv), wind turbines (wt), a battery bank (bb) and a diesel generator (dg). we assume that the types and characteristics of these system components are pre-selected by the planner based on the location of the system and the available technologies, whereas their numbers/capacities are to be determined by solving an optimization problem. since the re- mote/isolated community to be served is not connected to the grid, it has to rely on available local resources to satisfy its energy demand. this is to be achieved while minimizing the annualized total cost of the system, which includes both the capacity cost of its components, amortized over their useful lives, and the expected annual cost of fuel consumed by the dg. power outputs of the pv and wt are intermit- tent and unpredictable since they depend on meteorological variables (e.g., solar irradiance, wind speed, temperature), but the planner has access to some historical output data for both. on the other hand, and without loss of generality, we assume that the total demand for energy in each period ( e.g., hour) of the year can be predicted with high accuracy. if this assumption does not hold, one can simply treat the stochastic demand in the same way pv and wt outputs are treated, as will be explained later. all generation and storage units, along with the load, are assumed to be connected to the same bus, i.e., power flow losses are ignored. this is a reasonable assumption for small-scale hres typically used in remote communities. hres are usually operated according to preset rules that specify, for example, which energy source is to be utilized when there is a mismatch between res supply and demand. the utilization priority of the system resources is illustrated in fig. 1. based on this priority, the operational rules of the system are described as follows: in any time interval, if demand exceeds the energy supplied by the renewable generators (pv & wt), the shortage is compensated by the bb within its discharge rate and energy limits. the remaining demand (if any) is met by the dg within its rated capacity, then any excess loads are curtailed. on the other hand, if the energy supplied by the renewable generators exceeds demand, the excess energy is used to charge the bb within its charge rate and energy limits, then any remaining energy is dumped. there rules ensure that the bb is never charged directly from the dg, that load curtailment happens only when all sources (wt, pv, bb & dg) are not enough to satisfy the demand, and that energy cannot be dumped if the demand is yet to be fully satisfied. applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 3 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif fig. 1. utilization priority of system resources. 2.2. mathematical formulation of the hres design problem table 1 depicts the notations used to formulate the hres design problem. note that square brackets denote index sets, e.g., [𝑇 ] ∶= {1, 2, … , 𝑇 }. we first present the deterministic model, so the subscript 𝑠 is suppressed in all parameters and variables, but will be used later in the stochastic case. the first set of constraints (1) links the design variables to the state and control variables by capping the charging and discharging powers and the energy stored in the bb based on the number of battery modules installed. it also stipulates that the power delivered by the dg cannot exceed its rated capacity. 𝐷𝑐ℎ𝑡 ≤ 𝑀𝑅 𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 ∀𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] 𝐶ℎ 𝑡 ≤ 𝑀𝑅 𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 ∀𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] 𝑆𝑜𝐶𝑡 ≤ 𝑆𝐶𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 ∀𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] 𝑃 𝐷𝐺 𝑡 ≤ 𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺 ∀𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] (1) constraint (2) enforces the energy conservation law in the bb, which states that the change in the energy quantity stored in it over a time period equals the difference between charged and discharged quantities (adjusted for charging cycle efficiency) during this period. 𝑆𝑜𝐶𝑡 − 𝑆𝑜𝐶𝑡−1 = 𝛥 ( 𝐶ℎ 𝑡 − 𝐷𝑐ℎ𝑡 𝜂𝑏𝑎𝑡 ) ∀𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] (2) the next constraint (3) links the supply and demand in every time period, ensuring that the total power extracted from all sources (pv, wt, dg and the power discharged from the bb) is greater than the power used (net demand, i.e., demand less curtailed loads, and the power used to charge the bb), where the difference (if any) represents the power dumped. 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 𝑃 𝑃 𝑉 𝑡 + 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 𝑃 𝑊 𝑇 𝑡 + 𝐷𝑐ℎ𝑡 + 𝑃 𝐷𝐺 𝑡 ≥ 𝑑𝑡 − 𝑈 𝐷𝑡 + 𝐶ℎ 𝑡 ∀𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] (3) finally, the following non-negativity and integrality constraints are added. 𝐷𝑐ℎ𝑡, 𝐶ℎ𝑡, 𝑃 𝐷𝐺 𝑡 , 𝑆𝑜𝑐𝑡, 𝑈 𝐷𝑡 ≥ 0 ∀ 𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] (4) 𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺, 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 , 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 , 𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 ≥ 0, integer (5) two objectives are sought: minimizing cost and maximizing reli- ability. in particular, the first objective function (6) encompasses the annualized costs of the system components and the fuel cost of the dg, and is referred to hereinafter as the total annual cost (tac). the first term represent the installation costs of the pv, wt and dg, annualized using the parameter 𝐴 = 𝑖𝑟 1−(1+𝑖𝑟)−𝑛 , where 𝑖𝑟 and 𝑛 are, respectively, the interest rate and the hres lifetime. the second term represents the bb cost, calculated based on the assumption that battery modules can function for a specific number of full charging cycles ( 𝐿𝑏𝑎𝑡) before replacement. specifically, the initial cost of a battery module is divided by its usable storage capacity and the lifetime number of charging cycles to compute the cost per kwh charged to the bb, which is then multiplied by the total energy charged over a year to obtain the annual cost of the bb. the last term represents the fuel cost of the dg, which is proportional to the total energy delivered by it over a year. 𝑇 𝐴𝐶 = 𝐴 (𝐶𝑃 𝑉 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 + 𝐶𝑊 𝑇 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 + 𝐶𝐷𝐺,1𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺 ) + 𝛥𝐶𝑏𝑎𝑡 𝑆𝐶𝑏𝑎𝑡𝐿𝑏𝑎𝑡 ∑ 𝑡∈[𝑇 ] 𝐶ℎ 𝑡 + 𝐶𝐷𝐺,2 ∑ 𝑡∈[𝑇 ] 𝑃 𝐷𝐺 𝑡 (6) the second objective function (7) to be minimized measures the ‘‘un- reliability’’ of the system. the llp, which is a widely-used reliability index in hress [15,28,29], is defined as the fraction of total de- mand the system cannot satisfy because of insufficient energy supply, i.e., total curtailed load divided by total demand. 𝐿𝐿𝑃 = ∑ 𝑡∈[𝑇 ] 𝑈 𝐷𝑡 ∑ 𝑡∈[𝑇 ] 𝑑𝑡 (7) applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 4 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif table 1 notations. indices/sets 𝑡 ∈ [𝑇 ] time periods 𝑠 ∈ [𝑆] scenarios 𝑟 ∈ [𝑅] design variables parameters 𝑑𝑡 power demand during time period 𝑡 (kw) 𝑃 𝑃 𝑉 𝑡,𝑠 power output from a pv panel during time period 𝑡 in scenario 𝑠 (kw) 𝑃 𝑊 𝑇 𝑡,𝑠 power output from a wind turbine during time period 𝑡 in scenario 𝑠 (kw) 𝑞𝑠 nominal probability of scenario s 𝑆𝐶𝑏𝑎𝑡 usable energy storage capacity of a battery module (kwh) 𝑀𝑅 𝑏𝑎𝑡 maximum charge/discharge rate (power) of a battery module (kw) 𝐶𝐷𝐺,1 capacity cost of a diesel generator ($/kw) 𝐶𝐷𝐺,2 operating cost for a diesel generator ($/kwh) 𝐶𝑃 𝑉 unit cost of a pv panel ($/unit) 𝐶𝑊 𝑇 unit cost of a wind turbine ($/unit) 𝐶𝑏𝑎𝑡 unit cost of a battery module ($/unit) 𝐿𝑏𝑎𝑡 lifetime number of full charging cycles of a battery module 𝜂𝑏𝑎𝑡 bb charging cycle efficiency 𝛥 duration of each time period 𝛼 penalty factor for unmet demand ($/kwh) 𝑖𝑟 interest (discount) rate 𝑛 useful life of the hres (year) design variables 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 number of pv panels 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 number of wind turbines 𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 number of battery modules 𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺 rated capacity of the diesel generator (kw) control variables 𝐷𝑐ℎ𝑡,𝑠 power discharge from the bb during time period 𝑡 under scenario 𝑠 (kw) 𝐶ℎ 𝑡,𝑠 power charge to the bb during time period 𝑡 under scenario 𝑠 (kw) 𝑃 𝐷𝐺 𝑡,𝑠 power supplied by the dg during time period 𝑡 under scenario 𝑠 (kw) state variables 𝑆𝑜𝐶 𝑡,𝑠 bb state-of-charge at the end of time period 𝑡 under scenario 𝑠 (kwh) 𝑈 𝐷𝑡,𝑠 unmet power demand during time period 𝑡 under scenario 𝑠 (kw) for the purpose of applying the proposed solution methods, the bi- objective hres design problem is transformed into a single cost- minimizing problem through multiplying llp by𝛼𝛥 ∑ 𝑡∈[𝑇 ] 𝑑𝑡 and adding it to tac. this is equivalent to penalizing unmet demand at a rate of 𝛼/kwh. it should be noted that, under very mild assumptions about the problem parameters (e.g., 𝛼 > 𝐶 𝐷𝐺,1), the mathematical formulation provided above enforces the operational rules described in the previous section. we also note that the system cost does not depend on the initial number of battery modules installed, but rather on the rate at which they are used ( i.e., charged and discharged). hence, to avoid having an unnecessarily large bb (beyond what is needed in constrain set (1)), we penalize 𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 using a small artificial cost that is added to tac when solving the problem. 3. modeling uncertainty the mathematical model presented in the previous section treats pv and wt outputs as deterministic parameters. in realty, however, these are time-series data characterized by high variability, uncertainty and auto-correlation [30]. to capture these characteristics, and given the daily cyclic nature of res supply data, one can simply consider each available supply sample ( i.e., day) as a ‘‘scenario’’ and optimize the expected objective value, where the expectation is taken with respect to the distribution of supply scenarios. however, the problem becomes computationally expensive when the number of scenarios is large, which calls for scenario reduction techniques to be applied. hence, we use 𝑘-means clustering to extract a manageable number of supply scenarios that can reasonably model parameter variability while maintaining tractability. furthermore, since the probability distribu- tion of the extracted scenarios is based a relatively small number of sample points, it can significantly deviate from the true distribution. thus, we employ a robust approach to hedge the hres design against distributional ambiguity. a brief description of the das utilized for this purpose is provided later. 3.1. 𝑘-means clustering to extract supply scenarios a 𝑘-means clustering algorithm iteratively tries to partition the dataset, which consists of 𝑁 = 365 sample points, into 𝑘 non- overlapping subgroups (in this study, 𝑆 scenarios). it is based on the minimization of an objective function defined as [31]: min ∑ 𝑠∈[𝑆] ∑ 𝑛∈[𝑁] 𝜇𝑛𝑠 ‖‖𝑥𝑛 − 𝑣𝑠‖‖2, (8) where 𝑥𝑛 is the actual power output in day 𝑛 and 𝑣𝑠 is the center of scenario 𝑠. also 𝜇𝑛𝑠 is the assignment variable which equals 1 if the daily profile 𝑛 is assigned to cluster 𝑠 and 0 otherwise. both 𝑥𝑛 and 𝑣𝑠 are vectors consisting of 24 elements (24 h per day). this optimization problem can be solved using a heuristic iterative algorithm explained as follows [31]: 1. specify the number of clusters/scenarios (𝑆) and initialize their centroids (𝑣𝑠, 𝑠 ∈ [ 𝑆]) by drawing randomly from the element- wise intervals [𝑥min 𝑛 , 𝑥max 𝑛 ]. 2. in iteration 𝑗: • calculate the distance 𝑑𝑗 𝑛𝑠 = ‖𝑥𝑛−𝑣𝑗 𝑠‖2 between each sample point (𝑥𝑛) and cluster centroid (𝑣𝑗 𝑠). • tentatively assign each sample point to the cluster with the nearest centroid to it, i.e., set 𝜇𝑗 𝑛 ̄ 𝑠 = 1 , where ̄ 𝑠= argmin𝑠∈[𝑆] 𝑑𝑗 𝑛𝑠 and all other 𝜇𝑗 𝑛𝑠, 𝑠 ∈ [𝑆]∕{ ̄ 𝑠} to 0. • update the coordinates of each cluster’s centroid to co- incide with the arithmetic mean of the sample points as- signed to it, as follows: 𝑣𝑗+1 𝑠 = ∑ 𝑛∈[𝑁] 𝜇𝑗 𝑛𝑠𝑥𝑛 ∑ 𝑛∈[𝑁] 𝜇𝑗 𝑛𝑠 ∀𝑠 ∈ [𝑆]. (9) applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 5 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif 3. keep repeating step 2 until the change in the sum of squared distances is less than a certain tolerance 𝜖: |||||| ∑ 𝑠∈[𝑆] ∑ 𝑛∈[𝑁] (𝑑𝑗+1 𝑛𝑠 𝜇𝑗+1 𝑛𝑠 )2 − ∑ 𝑠∈[𝑆] ∑ 𝑛∈[𝑁] (𝑑𝑗 𝑛𝑠𝜇𝑗 𝑛𝑠)2 |||||| ≤ 𝜖. (10) 4. calculate the probability of each scenario as: 𝑞𝑠 = ∑ 𝑛∈[𝑁] 𝜇𝑛𝑠 𝑁 ∀𝑠 ∈ [𝑆]. (11) clearly, this is a heuristic that does not guarantee global optimality for the non-convex 𝑘-means clustering problem. to reduce the like- lihood of converging to a local optimum, the algorithm is repeated several times starting from different initial cluster centroids, and the best clustering output ( i.e., one with the least within-cluster variance) is declared optimum. moreover, the clustering output improves as the number of clusters increase. thus, we begins with 𝑆 = 2 and increase the number of clusters incrementally until the marginal reduction in within-cluster variance drops below a specific threshold. 3.2. 𝜙-divergence ambiguity set to represent the ambiguity about the probability distribution of the scenarios extracted in the previous section, we construct a das based on 𝜙-divergence, similar to that introduced in [32]. a 𝜙-divergence measures the difference between a pair of non-negative vectors, 𝑝 = (𝑝1, ..., 𝑝𝑆 )⊺ and 𝑞 = ( 𝑞1, ..., 𝑞𝑆 )⊺ where 𝑝 is the unknown true prob- ability distribution and 𝑞 is the empirical estimate of 𝑝. formally, a 𝜙-divergence is defined as 𝐼𝜙(𝑝, 𝑞) = ∑ 𝑠∈[𝑆] 𝑞𝑠𝜙 (𝑝𝑠 𝑞𝑠 ) , (12) where 𝜙(𝑡) is referred to as the 𝜙-divergence function and is convex for 𝑡 ≥ 0 with 𝜙(1) = 0 . the ambiguity set for 𝑝 defines the family of distributions 𝑃𝜙 ∶= { 𝑝 ∣ 𝑝 ≥ 0, ∑ 𝑠∈[𝑆] 𝑝𝑠 = 1, 𝐼 𝜙(𝑝, 𝑞) ≤ 𝜌 } , (13) i.e., all distributions supported on the extracted scenarios and have divergence values of at most 𝜌 from the empirical distribution. most 𝜙-divergences do not satisfy the triangle inequality, and many are not symmetric in the sense that 𝐼𝜙(𝑝, 𝑞) ≠ 𝐼𝜙(𝑞, 𝑝) [33]. however, one exception is the variation distance (vd), defined as 𝐼𝜙(𝑝, 𝑞) =∑ 𝑠∈[𝑆] |𝑝𝑠 − 𝑞𝑠|, or simply the 𝑙1-norm of the difference between the two vectors. besides begin a linear function, thus preserving tractability, using this divergence metric enables ‘‘suppression’’ and ‘‘popping’’ of scenarios, i.e., allowing, respectively, 𝑝𝑠 = 0 for a scenario for which 𝑞𝑠 > 0, and 𝑝𝑠 > 0 for a scenario for which 𝑞𝑠 = 0 [33]. also, as shown in [34], using vd as the𝜙-divergence makes the problem solvable using efficient decomposition algorithms. thus, this is the 𝜙-divergence of choice herein to construct the das. the parameter 𝜌 plays a key role in determining the quality of the obtained solution. setting 𝜌 = 0 leads to a unitary das that includes only the empirical distribution 𝑞, thus reduces the problem to the saa case, which is prone to over-fitting. at the other extreme, setting 𝜌 = 2 (for a vd-based das) ensures that all the probability mass can be moved from some scenarios to others, e.g., the entire probability mass is placed on scenario 𝑠1 in 𝑝 and on scenario 𝑠2 in 𝑞. using an oversized das (by setting 𝜌 too large) admits many irrelevant distributions and leads to solutions that are overly-conservative and under-performing. the ‘‘optimal’’ value of 𝜌, which leads to the best expected performance, is usually small, and depends on the variance of the uncertain parameters, size of the data sample used to construct the empirical distribution, the number of scenarios, and the desired confidence level. this optimal value can be determined through cross- validation, by which the solutions obtained using different 𝜌 values are simulated on new ( i.e., out-of-sample) data and the 𝜌 value corre- sponding to the best-performing solution is considered optimum. the cross-validation method used to properly size the das is explained in more detail and implemented in sections 5.2 and 5.3. 4. solution methods unlike the deterministic model where explicit functions exist to evaluate tac and llp under simplifying assumptions, such closed-form expressions do not exist for the real-case ( i.e., with real res supply data). instead, these objectives have to be estimated using simulation and optimized in a so framework. in what follows, we present two metamodel-based so approaches that successively approximate the complex i/o surface of the simulation model using easy-to-optimize surrogate functions and show how to deal with distributional ambiguity in both. 4.1. classical response surface methodology rsm is a widely-used, step-wise heuristic method that estimates the best inputs combination which can minimize a given objective function [35]. in this study, a robust version of the classical rsm is proposed along with its nominal version. the rsm description provided below is based on [36]. initialization: select a starting point (hres configuration) based on intuition, prior knowledge, or the current system configuration. stage 1: for the current solution 𝑋, do: 1. estimate the combined objective value 𝑌𝑠(𝑋) = 𝑇 𝐴𝐶𝑠 + 𝛼𝛥 ∑ 𝑡∈[𝑇 ] 𝐿𝐿𝑃𝑠 for each scenario 𝑠 ∈ [𝑆] through simulation. 2. find the worst-ca