enario is calculated by dividing the cluster size by 365. moreover, assuming independence between the pv and wt scenarios, 12 combined supply scenarios are generated and their joint probabil- ities are computed as products of the marginal probabilities. next, to generate annual supply profiles (to be used in the simulation), 365 daily supply profiles (scenarios) are drawn randomly with replacement ac- cording to the underlying probability distribution, whether nominal or robust. table 3 depicts the marginal probabilities of supply scenarios. 5.2. classical rsm results when applying the classical rsm, problem ( 14) is solved using gurobi, whereas the final quadratic function is optimized via its first- order optimality conditions. the design factors and levels considered for the r-iii and ccd designs are presented in tables 4 and 5 , re- spectively. as an example for the r-iii design, if the starting point is (𝑁𝑃 𝑉 = 200, 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 = 40, 𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 = 650, 𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺 = 600), the minimum and maximum perturbed levels would be (190, 38, 520, 585) and (210, 42, 715, 630), respectively. similar calculations are made for the ccd. the results obtained from optimizing each scenario individually using the classical rsm are summarized in tables 6–8 for three penalty costs. these penalty costs were selected deliberately to demonstrate the trade-off between minimizing tac and maximizing reliability, oth- erwise higher values always lead to a 100% reliability. the optimal configuration, best tac, computational time required for every sce- nario, number of iterations in the steepest descent procedure, reliability and the step size used for every scenario in the steepest descent are reported in table 6 for a penalty cost of $40/kwh. applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 8 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif fig. 3. supply scenarios extracted from historical data (each scenario is a vector of size 24). table 3 sizes and probabilities of the extracted clusters. cluster size (pv) probability (pv) cluster size (w