 case with a $20 penalty, when 𝜌 = 0 , tac is $452,276, which is close to the nominal problem’s tac of $452,041. as 𝜌 approaches 2 ($690,834 for 𝜌 = 1.8), tac becomes close to that of the worst scenario, scenario #9 in table 7, with tac of $691,383. the small difference between these values might have various explanations. it can happen because of the random doe in every simulation iteration. also, there is a possibility that the search converges to a local optimum in some cases. if there is no ambiguity about the probability of scenarios, the nomi- nal problem solution is the optimal. however, when there is ambiguity, uncertain parameters can randomly perturb the probabilities around their nominal values. usually, a small value of 𝜌 leads to a better out-of-sample performance than the nominal solution. in this study, considering a penalty cost of $40/kwh, the best out-of-sample tac found for the robust problem is when 𝜌 = 0 .01, leading to a tac of is $470,473 with 414 pv panels, 28 wind turbines, 640 battery modules and a diesel generator with 712 kw capacity and 99% system reliability. the optimal solution achieved for 𝜌 = 0 .01 and $20/kwh penalty cost is 257 pv panels, 22 wind turbines, 450 battery modules and a diesel generator with 347 kw capacity. tac of this configuration is $451,138 with 94.66% reliability. optimal robust solutions achieved for different penalty costs are depicted in table 10 . to confirm that 𝜌 = 0 .01 is the actual optimum value of 𝜌 (i.e., it did not happen due to random sampling) and it outperforms the nominal solution ( 𝜌 = 0 ), we perform a paired 𝑡-test for 𝜌 = 0 vs. 𝜌 = 0 .01 to determine which value of 𝜌 (size of ambiguity set) gives a better expected out-of-sample performance and leads to a lower tac. therefore, the confidence interval for the difference between the two expected responses is constructed to determine whether there is significant difference between tac calculated with 𝜌 = 0 and 𝜌 = 0.01. 𝜇0,𝑗 and 𝜇0.01,𝑗 are paired together to define 𝑍𝑗 = 𝜇0,𝑗 − 𝜇0.01,𝑗 for 𝑗 = 1, 2, … , 𝑛. 𝑍𝑗 are i.i.d. variables and they are normally distributed. thus the confidence interval for 𝐸(𝑍𝑗 ) is calculated as follows: ̄𝑍(𝑛) = ∑𝑛 𝑗=1 𝑍𝑗 𝑛 (22) and ̂𝑉 𝑎𝑟[ ̄𝑍(𝑛)] = ∑𝑛 𝑗=1[𝑍𝑗 − ̄𝑍(𝑛)]2 𝑛(𝑛 − 1) (23) we then form the (approximate) 100 × (1 − 𝑎)% confidence interval as: ̄𝑍(𝑛) ± 𝑡𝑛−1,1−𝛼∕2 √ ̂𝑉 𝑎𝑟[ ̄𝑍(𝑛)] (24) to assess the out-of-sample performance, 10 new random supply power realizations are generated similar to the original supply data in section 5.1, i.e., 365 random data points are selected from both solar and wind clusters 10 times and new scenarios are used as simulation inputs to estimate the expected out-of-sample performance for the solutions obtained with different 𝜌 values. tac values are depicted in table 11 for $40/kwh penalty cost. assuming normality of the sample mean difference and 𝛼 = 0 .1, a paired t-test is carried out. the obtained ̄𝑍(10) = 1744 .6 and ̂𝑉 𝑎𝑟[ ̄𝑍(𝑛)] = 51 , 593.6, leading to the (approximate) 90% confidence interval [1328.2, 2160.9] for 𝑍. thus, with approximately 90 percent confidence, it can be concluded that 𝜇0 differs from 𝜇0.01, and it furthermore appears that 𝜇0.01 is superior, since it leads to a lower applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 11 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif table 11 classical rsm — tac for 10 independent real- izations with two 𝜌 = 0 and 𝜌 = 0 .01, and their differences. j 𝜇0,𝑗 𝜇0.01,𝑗 𝑍𝑗 1 472,922 470,102 2,820 2 472,180 470,232 1,948 3 472,831 469,927 2,904 4 471,555 470,520 1,035 5 472,374 470,396 1,978 6 471,899 471,123 776 7 472,911 471,021 1,890 8 472,893 471,262 1,631 9 471,795 470,714 1,081 10 472,207 470,824 1,383 fig. 5. classical rsm — pareto frontier for tac vs. reliability. tac (between 1328.2 and 2160.9 lower). based on the same normality assumption, similar calculations are done with new realizations for the penalty costs of $20/kwh and $10/kwh, resulting in confidence intervals for the mean differences of [964.6, 1645.5] and [1069.0, 1709.9], respectively, thus, likewise, indicating the superiority of 𝜌 = 0.01. in this study, when a high penalty cost is considered for unmet demand, the system attempts to install a large number of generation and storage units to avoid shortages, which enhances the reliability and increases the tac. on the other extreme, by having a zero penalty cost, tac can be reduced to zero by not installing any resources. however, the reliability index becomes zero, too. therefore, the purpose is to look for ‘‘non-dominated’’ (or pareto-optimal) solutions. the frontier in fig. 5 is constructed to illustrate the trade-off between tac and reliability by changing the penalty cost and observing how the cost and reliability change. optimal tac values obtained from the robust prob- lem in table 10 and their corresponding reliability values are drawn for three penalty costs in fig. 8 . based on this pareto frontier, when a high value of penalty is considered for unmet demand, the system attempts to avoid any supply deficit and installs a large number of units, which leads to high system reliability and high tac, accordingly. as the penalty cost decreases, the system allows more demand to remain unmet since supply deficiency is penalized at a lower rate. 5.3. grst results in performing the first step of the grst, convexity of the esti- mated quadratic function is assured by using cvx [ 43] version 2.2, a matlab-based modeling system for convex optimization. each scenario is simulated and optimized using different penalty costs, and their op- timal configuration, solution, computational time, number of iteration and reliability are depicted in tables 12 –14 with $40/kwh, $20/kwh and $10/kwh penalty costs, respectively. all the costs stated in the tac column are actual costs, i.e., the penalty cost is deducted from tac in the scenarios with reliability less than 100%. it can be inferred from table 12 that scenario #1, which uses cluster 1 from both the pv and wind supply power, has the lowest tac since fewer renewable resources are needed when a high amount of power is generated. on the contrary, scenario #9, which uses cluster 3 from both pv and wind as inputs, has the highest cost due to the large number of required resources. the same conclusion can be derived from tables 13 and 14. the grst terminates when the difference between the lower and upper bounds of all the variables are negligible. therefore, the number of iterations depends on the lower and upper limit of variables. to hold the same initial conditions for both methods, grst upper bound and lower bound for each scenario are chosen based on the starting point of that scenario, i.e., the starting point of each scenario in the classical rsm is at the center of the starting range in the grst. for example, if the starting point is [500, 40, 750, 750] in the classical rsm, the upper bound and lower bounds for grst are set at [+20%, +25%, +20%, +20%] = [600 , 50, 900, 900] and [−20%, −25%, −20%, −20%] = [400 , 30, 600, 600], respectively. starting with a high penalty cost ($40/kwh) for unmet demand in table 12 has resulted in 100% reliability for most of the scenarios since high cost must be paid as penalty for supply deficiency. next, smaller values ($20/kwh and $10/kwh) of the penalty cost are also tested and the results are presented in tables 13 and 14 . the same upper and lower bounds for each scenario are used for these two cases. due to the lower penalty cost, fewer number of sources are installed, and accordingly, tac has decreased and reliability has deteriorated. to solve the nominal problem, a convex quadratic function is fit on each scenario using eq. ( 15) and empirical probabilities derived from clustering are used to obtain the optimal solution and configuration as in table 15 for the three penalty costs. similar to the classical rsm, cross-validation is applied to determine the optimal 𝜌 value in the grst, and its results are demonstrated in fig. 6 for the three penalty costs. for the first penalty cost of $40/kwh, like the previous method, when 𝜌 = 0 , there is no ambiguity and the problem is similar to the nominal problem ($472,565) with tac of $472,893. whereas when 𝜌 is approaching its maximum value of 2, the tac becomes close to the worst scenario, scenario #9 ($710,267), with tac of $709,234 ( 𝜌 = 1 .8). perturbing probabilities around their nominal values with small 𝜌, the best obtained tac is with 𝜌 = 0 .01, which is $471,327 with 421 pv panels, 25 wind turbines, 662 battery modules and a diesel generator with 695 kw capacity. a similar explanation can be given regarding the solutions with $20/kwh and $10/kwh penalty costs. due to their relatively lower costs, their plots situate below the solutions of $40/kwh, and as the size of the ambiguity set increases, their tac increases too. for a $20/kwh penalty, when 𝜌 = 0, tac is $453,293 (nominal problem = $453,499) and as 𝜌 gets closer to 2, the problem acts like the worst scenario ($692,291) with a tac of $691,534 (𝜌 = 1.8). moreover, with a penalty of $10/kwh, tac is $414,823 and $645,921 with 𝜌 = 0 and 𝜌 = 1 .8, respectively. the nominal solution and the solution with 𝜌 = 0, as well as the worst scenario solution and solution with 𝜌 = 2, must coincide in theory. however, due to the random nature of grst in every iteration of monte carlo simulation and the possibility of approaching a local optimum, there is a negligible difference (less than 0.001) between their values. the optimal solutions found for both robust problems are with 𝜌 = 0 .01, which has led to the lowest tac. best solutions and configurations found for the robust problem with the three penalty costs are summarized in table 16. a similar paired t-test is conducted to make sure there is a sig- nificant difference between the results of 𝜌 = 0 vs. 𝜌 = 0 .01, and that 𝜌 = 0 .01 does give a better expected out-of-sample performance and leads to a lower tac. therefore, a confidence interval for the difference between the 