h approximately 90 percent confidence, it can be concluded that 𝜇0 differs from 𝜇0.01, and it furthermore appears that𝜇0.01 is superior, since it leads to a lower tac (between 942.4 and 1366.3 lower). based on the same normality assumption, similar calculations are done with new realizations for the penalty costs of $20/kwh and $10/kwh. the obtained confidence fig. 7. grst — qq plot for 𝑍𝑗. intervals for 𝑍 are [740.1, 1489.4] and [836.2, 1545.3] for $20/kwh and $10/kwh, respectively, indicating the superiority of 𝜌 = 0.01. next, a pareto frontier is constructed to demonstrate the trade-off between tac and reliability. looking for the pareto-optimal solutions, the optimal tac deduced from the robust problem in table 16 and their corresponding reliability values are plotted for three penalty applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 14 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif fig. 8. grst — pareto frontier for tac vs. reliability. table 18 comparison between classical rsm and grst results. problem penalty cost 𝑁𝑃 𝑉 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡 𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺 (kw) tac reliability computational time (s) nominal $40/kwh 6.9% −3.7% 3.1% 4.3% 0.0% 0.0% −48.9% $20/kwh 10.9% −9.1% 4.2% −1.4% 0.3% −0.6% −60.0% $10/kwh −3.3% −5.6% 6.7% 4.5% 0.2% 1.2% −64.9% robust $40/kwh 1.7% −10.7% 3.4% −2.4% 0.2% 0.0% 20.4% $20/kwh 26.5% −13.6% −6.7% 3.7% 0.3% −1.2% −7.6% $10/kwh −10.7% −5.9% 6.2% 15.5% 0.3% −0.2% −26.8% costs in fig. 8 . for a high penalty cost such as $40/kwh, reliability of the system is near 100% since no unmet demand is allowed due to the high penalty added to tac ($471,327). as the penalty cost is decreased, the system allows some demand to remain unmet for lower tac ($453,127 and $413,527), which also results in the lower reliability levels of 93.52% and 84.72% for $20/kwh and $10/kwh penalty costs, respectively. from the obtained solutions, the designers can choose the penalty costs they consider more appropriate to their utility. 5.4. comparison between classical rsm and grst given that two methods are proposed in this paper for designing hress, it is worth comparing them in light of the numerical results presented earlier. first, the optimal results obtained for both the nomi- nal and the robust problems with different penalty costs are directly compared, then the pros and cons of each method are highlighted. finally, system operation in several cases is simulated and analyzed. table 18 depicts the percentage differences between the optimal solutions (𝑁𝑃 𝑉 , 𝑁𝑊 𝑇 , 𝑁𝑏𝑎𝑡&𝐶𝑎𝑝 𝐷𝐺), optimal values (tac & reliability) and computational times of the two methods for the nominal and the robust problem with $40/kwh, $20/kwh and $10/kwh penalty costs. the reported percentage difference for a metric 𝜃 is computed as 𝜃classical rsm −𝜃grst 𝜃classical rsm , i.e., the difference is positive when 𝜃classical rsm > 𝜃grst, and vice versa. we first note that the two methods led to substantially different solutions in all cases, with relative differences in component num- bers/capacities ranging between 1.7% and 26.5%. however, the rel- ative differences in tac and reliability between the optimal designs obtained from the two methods are much smaller, not exceeding 0.3% and 1.2%, respectively. this observation demonstrates the ability of both methods to find high-quality, albeit different, solutions. the clas- sical rsm slightly (yet consistently) outperforms the grst in terms of tac, whereas the reliability comparison results are mixed with no clear advantage for either method. careful examination of the optimal solutions reveals the substitutability of system components with others to achieve a similar performance. for example, in the nominal problem with $40/kwh penalty cost, the grst solution used 3.7% fewer wt than the classical rsm solution, but compensated for them by installing 6.9% more pv, 3.1% more bb capacity and 4.3% more dg capacity to achieve almost the same tac and reliability. this substitutability explains why the problem might have multiple near-optimal, but very different, solutions. furthermore, it is clear that the grst enjoys much smaller computational times compared to the classical rsm when implemented to the nominal problem, with savings that range between 48.9% and 64.9%. in contrast, this computational time advantage of the grst is inconclusive for the robust problem, perhaps because it requires the min–max problem (20) to be solved in every iteration. based on the comparative analysis performed above, the main advantage of the classical rsm over the grst is its ability to find slightly better solutions in terms of tac, and sometimes also reliability. however, its performance is found to be highly dependent on the starting point and the design variables’ levels, i.e., r-iii and ccd levels and intervals. a starting point that is too far from optimal or improperly set design levels might lead the search in a ‘‘wrong’’ direction based on local information, thus ending at a local optimum. in other words, to reach a near-optimal solution using the classical rsm, prior knowledge about the system is needed. on the other hand, although the solutions obtained using the grst have marginally higher tac values compared to the classical rsm solutions, the computational times for the latter are significantly smaller, especially in the nominal case. furthermore, the number of iterations can be controlled by the planner via setting the initial search space and the stopping criterion (since the search space is reduced by a predetermined factor in each iteration). another advantage of the grst is that it uses global information from the applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 15 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif fig. 9. hres operation for one day. entire search space in each iteration, thus is less sensitive to the initial point selection than the classical rsm. however, if the response surface cannot be reasonably approximated using a convex quadratic function, the accuracy of the grst can severely suffer, thus reducing its likelihood of finding the global optimum. a possible remedy for this issue is by partitioning the search space into smaller areas and applying the grst to each one independently. moreover, increasing the number of sample points generated and simulated in each iteration can increase the approximation accuracy. finally, the optimal solutions obtained from the two methods with different penalty costs ($10/kwh, $20/kwh and $40/kwh) for both the nominal ( 𝜌 = 0 ) and the robust ( 𝜌 = 0 .01) cases are simulated using demand and res supply data of the same day (1 december 2019), and the simulation results are presented in fig. 9. the bar charts show the bb charging and discharging operations (negative and positive, respectively), whereas the dg power output is shown using the line charts. the bb and dg operation profiles for the day are quite similar across the 12 cases considered, in the sense that the bb is discharged and the dg is used during nighttime, and the bb is charged in the morning, which is expected given the pv supply profile. however, there are still some notable differences among the cases. in general, we note that the grst solutions make slightly more use of the bb and dg to satisfy demand than the classical rsm solutions, which seem to rely more on their larger wt capacity. likewise, the usage of the bb and dg in the robust solutions is slightly more than in the corresponding nominal solutions. the penalty cost seems also to play a significant role in determining how they are used. the smallest value of $10/kwh is associated with the highest usage of the dg across the board, whereas the dg is not used at all when the penalty cost is $40/kwh. this result is probably related to the high pv, wt and bb capacity installed in the latter case, reducing the need for the dg. likewise, usage of the bb seems to be inversely correlated with the penalty cost. 6. conclusions and future research stand-alone hres are generally more suitable than single-source systems to supply power in off-grid applications, especially in remote areas with difficult access. however, these systems must be designed carefully and account for the stochasticity and uncertainty in supply data to serve the demand at high reliability and affordable cost. in this study, a stand-alone hres that consists of pv panels and wind turbines, integrated with battery storage and diesel generator, is considered to implement a cost-minimization design approach. in order to capture un- certainty in supply power, a finite number of scenarios with uncertain probabilities are extracted from time-series data of both solar and wind power through 𝑘-means clustering. next, to tackle the uncertainty in scenarios’ probability, an ambiguity set based on the variation distance 𝜙-divergence is constructed around the nominal probability of scenar- ios. since tac and reliability functions cannot be evaluated explicitly, two novel robust simulation–optimization methods, namely classical rsm and grst, are proposed to estimate the objective function from a surrogate model and optimize it. the presented methods are very generic and flexible as they can be used for different applications, and not only hres design, with different objectives, operational rules, reliability metrics, scenario generation methods, ambiguity sets, etc. the numerical experiments conducted on a realistic case study have shown the applicability and effectiveness of both methods. the optimal designs obtained using the classical rsm were found to marginally outperform the grst designs in terms of tac, whereas the reliabil- ity comparison results were mixed. however, the grst is faster to applied energy 341 (2023) 121132 16 p. pourmohammadi and a. saif fig. 10. flowchart of the classical rsm. converge and less dependent on the starting point than the classical rsm. also, through cross-validation and hypothesis testing, it was shown that using the robust model with a properly-sized distributional ambiguity set leads to significantly better solutions than the nominal model. the effect of the penalty cost on tac, reliability, system design and operation was also investigated and found to be quite significant. in particular, the pareto frontiers constructed show a clear trade-off between tac and reliability. for future studies, other sources of renewable energy such as biomass, hydro, etc. can be used alongside/instead of pv/wind/battery/ diesel units in order to build a robust and reliable hres. short-term (intra-hour) fluctuations in demand and res supply can also be con- sidered in future research, e.g., by using shorter time intervals and/or other energy storage technologies. moreover, alternative metamodel- based approaches for simulation optimization such as kriging and neural network can be implemented to estimate the objective function fig. 11. flowchart of the grst. as a surrogate model. finally, other types of𝜙-divergence function such as kullback–leibler or 𝜒 2 distance, can be utilized to construct the ambiguity set. credit authorship contribution statement pardis pourmohammadi: conceptualization, methodology, soft- ware, validation, formal analysis, investigation, data curation, writing – original draft, writing – review & editing, visualization. ahmed saif: conceptualization, methodology, software, validation, formal analy- sis, investigation, resources, writing – original draft, writing – review & editing, supervision, project administration, funding acqu",yes
